RANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a), (c) & (d):       Mahatma Gandhi NREGA is a demand driven wage employment \nprogramme and funds are released to the States/UTs on the basis of agreed to Labour Budget \nand performance of the States during the financial year and there is no provision of State/UT -\nwise allocation of funds.   State/UT wise release of funds for wage, material and \nadministrative component in the last three financial years under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is  \ngiven at Annexure. \n  \n(b):       The number of the persons provided employment under Mahatma Gandhi NREGA at \nNational level and in the Ahmadnagar district of Maharashtra in last three financial years is \ngiven below:  \n  \nFinancial Year 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 \nTotal number of persons provided \nemployment [In Crore] \n7.77 7.88 11.19 \n(as per MIS) \n \nAhmadnagar Districts of Maharashtra \nFinancial Year 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 \nTotal number of persons provided \nemployment [In Lakh] \n1.13 1.08 1.24 \n(As per MIS)  \n  \n \n\nThe number of the persons provided employment under Mahatma Gandhi NREGA in last \nthree financial years in Shirdi Parliamentary constituency which includes 6 blocks of \nAhmadnagar district of Maharashtra: \nBlock Name \nFY \n2018-2019 \n(number of \npersons provided \nemployment) \nFY \n2019-2020 \n(number of \npersons \nprovided \nemployment) \nFY \n2020-2021 \n(number of \npersons provided \nemployment) \nKOPARGAON 4286 4804 4646 \nNEVASA 5866 8188 11719 \nRAHATA 4974 4554 5321 \nRAHURI 4244 3488 4357 \nSANGAMNER 9262 7538 9441 \nSHRIRAMPUR 6418 5112 5264 \n(as per MIS) \n \n \n****** \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure referred in reply to parts (a), (c) & (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.307 dated 30.11.2021. \n  \nState/UT wise release of funds for wage, material and administrative component for the last \nthree financial years under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS in FY 2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-21.  \n(Rs. in crore) \nSI. \nNo. States/UTs FY  \n2018-19 \nFY  \n2019-20 \n FY  \n2020-21 \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 7.62 5.84 4.86 \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH 6684.54 7311.48 10365.48 \n3 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 163.25 107.57 348.95 \n4 ASSAM 1050.37 1476.24 2514.55 \n5 BIHAR 2891.94 3283.88 6647.01 \n6 CHHATTISGARH 3082.94 2792.41 4144.19 \n7 GOA 0.49 2.17 0.91 \n8 GUJARAT 1060.80 775.84 1513.75 \n9 HARYANA 356.25 345.27 776.67 \n10 HIMACHAL PRADESH 778.74 615.37 963.69 \n11 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 620.36 825.62 1166.63 \n12 JHARKHAND 1538.06 1311.15 3489.84 \n13 KARNATAKA 3040.25 5546.20 5605.01 \n14 KERALA 2354.74 3541.12 4300.32 \n15 LADAKH 0.00 0.00 22.49 \n16 LAKSHADWEEP 0.16 0.24 0.00 \n17 MADHYA PRADESH 4681.41 4825.63 9225.40 \n18 MAHARASHTRA 2014.64 1723.25 1639.78 \n19 MANIPUR 269.60 610.75 737.57 \n20 MEGHALAYA 786.14 1026.64 1286.41 \n21 MIZORAM 392.37 526.74 576.81 \n22 NAGALAND 138.86 298.54 483.82 \n23 ODISHA 2218.21 2488.22 5409.50 \n24 PUDUCHERRY 14.75 17.04 27.13 \n25 PUNJAB 598.55 776.89 1287.86 \n26 RAJASTHAN 5492.31 6891.74 9129.03 \n27 SIKKIM 97.43 83.66 101.46 \n28 TAMIL NADU 4951.66 5447.80 8941.26 \n29 TELANGANA 2958.17 2246.78 4163.57 \n30 THE DADRA AND NAGAR \nHAVELI AND DAMAN AND DIU 4.84 0.00 0.00 \n31 TRIPURA 442.54 740.05 1199.93 \n32 UTTAR PRADESH 5464.65 6240.17 12257.35 \n33 UTTARAKHAND 610.05 470.61 908.89 \n34 WEST BENGAL 7358.38 8458.21 10993.56 \n  Total 62125.07 70813.12 110233.65 \n           \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |308", "t": "Legal Backing for MSP", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 308 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.12.2021 \n \nMINIMUM WAGE FOR GIG WORKERS \n \n*308. SHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA:  \n          SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)whether the Government has considered or is considering any \nproposal to introduce minimum wages for gig workers and if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(b)the relief measures put in the Government’s labour codes for gig \nworkers carrying out their duties in harsh conditions like rain, \nstorm, cold, heat, etc.;  \n(c)whether the Government is cognizant of the fact that any \nintroduction of minimum wages will hamper the economic models of \ncompanies/startups which are dependent on gig workers and if so, \nthe details of any study or consultation done by the Government in \nthis regard; and  \n(d)whether the Government is maintaining or is considering to \nmaintain any registry for gig workers and if so, the details of such a \nproposal or details of the number of registrations in such a registry? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*                                                    ***** \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 308 FOR ANSWER ON 20.12.2021 \nREGARDING MINIMUM WAGE FOR GIG WORKERS  BY SHRI SANGANNA \nAMARAPPA and SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA. \n \n(a): The Code on Wages, 2019, provides for universal minimum wage \nand floor wage across organized and unorganized sector which include \ngig workers. It, inter alia, provides for fixation of minimum rate wages on \na time work basis with any one or more wage periods, namely by the \nhour, by the day or by the month. Besides, the Code mandates the Central \nGovernment to fix floor wage applicable across the Central and the State \nsphere.  Section 9 of the Code lays down that the minimum rates of \nwages fixed by the appropriate Government under section 6 shall not be \nless than the floor wage. The said provisions of the Code on Wages, 2019, \nhave not come into force. \n \n(b): The Code on Social Security, 2020, recognises gig workers as a new \noccupational category. It defines gig worker as a person who performs \nwork or participates in work arrangement and earns from such activities, \noutside of the traditional employer -employee relationship. The Code \nenvisages various benefits to gig and platform workers through \nformulation of schemes including (i) life and disability cover; (ii) accident \ninsurance; (iii) health and maternity benefits ; (iv) old-age protection; (v) \ncrèche and other benefits as may be determined by the Central \nGovernment. The provisions of the Code have not come into force.  \n \n(c): Payment of minimum wages  is a statutory requirement under the \nMinimum Wages Act, 1948, and the Code on Wages, 2019.  \n \n(d): The Central Government, on 26.08.2021, has launched e -SHRAM \nPortal which is a National Database of the Unorganised Workers \nincluding gig workers. It has been  made available to the States/ UTs for \nregistration of unorganised workers including gig workers on e -SHRAM \nportal. All eligible registered unorganised workers including gig workers \nare entitled to get benefit of an accidental insurance cover of Rs. 2.0 \nLakh for a year free of cost through Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima \nYojana (PMSBY). One of the key objectives of the portal is to deliver the \nbenefits of Social Security Schemes of the Central Government and the \nState Governments through e -SHRAM portal to the unorganized workers \nincluding gig workers. Number of gig workers registered on e -SHRAM \nportal as on 14.12.2021 is 7,27,927. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |309", "t": "National Cyclone Risk Mitigation Projects", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 309 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 9, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nNATIONAL CYCLONE RISK MITIGATION PROJECTS \n \n309.   DR. T. SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has left out Tamil Nadu  State from National Cyclone \nRisk Mitigation Projects  (NCRMP) Phase -I & II which were implemented to  \nrespond better to cyclones and for the construction of infrastructure to address \nthe vulnerability of coastal community to cyclones \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the effective measures taken by the  Government to implement projects under \nNCRMP in Tamil Nadu; and \n \n(d) the details of the funds allocated for the projects under NCRMP? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a) to ( c): National Cyclone Risk Mitigation Project (NCRMP) is a World Bank \nassisted centrally sponsored scheme, being implemented in the 8 coastal States, \nin two phases. Two States were covered in Phase-I, namely, Andhra Pradesh and \nOdisha; and six States are covered in Phase - II, namely Goa, Gujarat, Karnataka, \nKerala, Maharashtra and West Bengal. The project has the following four \ncomponents: \ni. Component A: Early Warning Dissemination System (EWDS), the state of \nthe art multi -technology option (Alert Siren, Satellite Radio, Mass \nMessaging, etc.), to provide reliable communication and ens uring last \nmile connectivity during disaster. \n-2/... \n\n \n-2- \nLS.US.Q.NO.309 FOR 30.11.2021 \nii. Component B: Cyclone Risk Mitigation Infrastructure (CRMI), to enhance \nthe preparedness and mitigate the  vulnerability of the coastal \ncommunities through investments for construction of multi -purpose \ncyclone shelters (MPCS), roads, bridges and saline embankments. \niii. Component C: Technical Assistance for Capacity Building on multi \nhazard risk management. \niv. Component D: Project Management and Monitoring. \n \nAt the time of the i nception of NCRMP, as Tamil Nadu State was already \nimplementing a similar World Bank assisted project viz. “Coastal Disaster Risk \nReduction Project” with a total outlay  of Rs. 1481.80 crore , having similar \ncomponents, it was not made part of NCRMP. \n \n(d):Revised fund allocation under NCRMP Phase -I and NCRMP Phase -II are Rs. \n2541.60 crore and Rs. 2059.83 crore, respectively.  \n \n*** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |31", "t": "Digi Saksham Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 31 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \nDIGI SAKSHAM PROGRAMME \n \n31. SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \n      SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \n      SHRI SUBRAT PATHAK: \n      SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \n      SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n      SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n      SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \n      SHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL: \n      SHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has launched any Digi  Saksham Programme recently in \njoint venture with Microsoft India; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the key fact or salient features of the said \nprogramme; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to provide free of cost training to rural youth in \nthe field of digital skills including basic skills and advance computing through \nNational Career Service Portal and if so, the details thereof and the steps taken in \nthis regard; \n \n(d) whether the Government is also considering free o f cost training to disadvantaged \ngroup of the society who have been adversely affected by the ongoing Covid -19 \npandemic and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) the details of funds sanctioned and released under the said programme during the \ncurrent year; and \n \n(f) the number of youths, particularly from rural areas who get benefitted by the \ntraining under the said programme? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n \n \n\n \n(a) to ( d) Yes, Ministry of Labour and Employment has partnered with Microsoft India \nand launched DigiSaksham - a joint digital skills programme to enhance the \nemployability of youth by imparting digital skills that are required in an increasingly \ntechnology driven e ra. This initiative is an extension of the Government ’s ongoing \nprograms to support the youth from rural and semi -urban areas to acquire digital skills \nfor better career opportunities. The salient features of the programme are: (i) to \nsupport government’s accelerated efforts to skill youth for employment; (ii) to develop \nstandardized curriculum and content that would be able to meet the continuously \nevolving demands of the industry; and (iii) to transform ‘youth’ into a ‘workplace ready \nprofessional’ through knowledge acquisition and skill development. \n \nUnder this programme, various free of cost courses such as Excel, Azure, Security \nFundamentals and HTML are provided to the NCS jobseekers through Self -Paced \nLearning, Virtual Instructor Led Training Mode and  Instructor Led Training Mode. The \nDigiSaksham Programme gives priority to the jobseekers of semi urban areas \nbelonging to disadvantaged groups of the society, including those who have lost their \njobs due to Covid-19 pandemic. \n \n(e) The Government of India has neither sanctioned nor released any funds for this \nprogramme. However, Microsoft India has contributed funds in this programme through \nits Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) mandate to train jobseekers on digital skills. \n \n(f) The total number of youths who got benefitted by the training of the said \nprogramme are as under: \n \nJobseekers registered till date for training 13079 \nTrained through Virtual Instructor Led Training Mode 2095 \nTrained through Self-paced learning 5897 \n \n ******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |310", "t": "Strengthening the Cooperative Movement", "head": "����������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n����������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������\n��\n����������������������������\n��\n������������ ��������������������������������������������\n�� �� ��\n�������