f Chhattisgarh;  \n(b) the details of the number of the said centres which have become \npaperless and have adopted the ‘e-office’ system;  \n(c) whether the employees at the said centres have adapted \nthemselves to the ‘e -office’ system for the operation of the said \ncentres;  \n(d) whether any efforts are being made to make the remaining \ncentres paperless;  \n\n(e) if so, the details and the time by which the said centres are \nproposed to be made paperless, State/UT -wise including the \nState of Chhattisgarh; and  \n(f) the total number of Akashvani Kendras currently operational and \nalso the number of non -operational centres in the State of \nChhattisgarh? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS  \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n(a) to (f) A statement is laid on the Table of House. \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f ) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *330 FOR ANSWER ON 21.12.2021. \n(a) AIR has 500 Akashvani broadcasting centre s currently operational \nacross the country. State/UT-wise numbers are given at Annexure. \n(b) to (e) Offices of Prasar Bharati  across th e country are using \ndifferent modules of e -office for paperless operation including offices \nin All India Radio. \nEmployee centric modules such as eLeave, eTour are used by the \nemployees across the network. The e -file module, which is mainly \nused for process ing administrative and other matters in various \noffices, has been adopted by most of the offices for paperless working.     \nThe usage of e -file module by different offices in All India Radio is \nregularly being monitored.  Online training sessions are condu cted \nregularly to increase its effective usage by all the concerned staff in \nthe country including the state of Chhattisgarh.    \n(f) There are 13 AIR broadcasting centres operational in the State of \nChhattisgarh and all are using e-Office. \n**** \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE AS  REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *330 FOR ANSWER ON 21.12.2021 \nSTATE/UT-WISE NUMBERS OF AIR BROADCASTING STATIONS  \n \nS. No.    States \n \nTotal  AIR \nStations \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 14 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh 39 \n3.  Assam 23 \n4.  Bihar 16 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 13 \n6.  Goa 1 \n7.  Gujarat 17 \n8.  Haryana 5 \n9.  Himachal Pradesh 16 \n10.  Jharkhand  14 \n11.  Karnataka 20 \n12.  Kerala 12 \n13.  Madhya Pradesh     25 \n14.  Maharashtra 30 \n15.  Manipur 9 \n16.  Meghalaya  7 \n17.  Mizoram 13 \n18.  Nagaland 10 \n19.  Odisha 21 \n20.  Punjab 8 \n21.  Rajasthan  26 \n22.  Sikkim   16 \n23.  Tamilnadu 15 \n24.  Telangana 11 \n25.  Tripura 18 \n26.  Uttar Pradesh  29 \n27.  Uttarakhand 22 \n28.  West Bengal  16 \nUnion Territory  \n29.  UT (A & N Island) 1 \n30.  UT(Delhi)  1 \n31.  UT (Chandigarh) 1 \n32.  UT (Dadra, Nagar Haveli, Daman & Diu) 3 \n33.  UT (Lakshadweep and Minicoy Islands) 1 \n34.  UT(Puducherry) 2 \n35.  UT (Jammu & Kashmir) 16 \n36.  UT (Ladakh) 9 \nTotal 500 \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |331", "t": "Funds allocated for TOP Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.331 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n  \nFunds allocated for TOP Scheme \n  \n331. SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \n  \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the sports persons from North East excelling at \ninternational levels over the post few years, if so, the details thereof; \n  \n(b) whether funds allocated for the Target Olympic Podium (TOP) \nscheme is sufficient and if so, the details thereof along with the \nprogress of the scheme; \n  \n(c) if not, the manner in which the Government will generate fund for \nthe above mentioned scheme and overall sports development; \n  \n(d) whether Karate has been included in Olympics Game and if so, the \nother steps taken by the Government to develop Karate; and \n  \n(e) whether the Government would consider establishing a State-of-\nthe-art sports training facility in Barpeta Lok Sabha Constituency of \nAssam and if so, the details thereof? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n  \n(a): Yes, Sir. Sports persons from North East are excelling at \ninternational levels over the past few years. The North-Eastern States \nin India are home to some of India’s most recognised international \n\nplayers such as, Ms. M.C. Mary Kom - 2012 Olympics bronze medallist \nand a five-time world amateur-boxing champion, footballer Baichung \nBhutia, boxer L. Sarita Devi, silver medallist at the 2014 Glasgow \nCommonwealth Games, Jayanta Talukdar, Gold Medallist and Arjuna \nAward winner, weightlifter K. Sanjita Chanu, who won gold at the 2014 \nGlasgow Commonwealth Games, etc. \n  \nIn Tokyo Olympics 2020, out of 7 medal winners, 2 medal winners are \nfrom North-East States i.e., Ms. Mirabai Chanu (Weightlifting) - Silver \nMedal and Ms. Lovlina Borgohain (Boxing) - Bronze Medal. \n  \nFurther, 18 medals were won by athletes from North-Eastern States in \nAsian Games 2018 and Commonwealth Games 2018. \n  \n(b) & (c):  Target Olympic Podium Scheme (TOPS) was formulated in \nJuly 2014 under the overall ambit of National Sports Development \nFund (NSDF) with the aim to identify, groom and prepare potential \nmedal prospects for Olympic and Paralympic Games. No separate \nfunds are allocated for the Scheme. Funds are being released from \nNSDF to Sports Authority of India (SAI) for this Scheme. The details of \nblock grant released from NSDF to SAI for Target Olympic Podium \nScheme (TOPS) are given below: \nYear Amount Received (in Crore Rupees) \n2016-17 20.00 \n2017-18 10.00 \n2018-19 10.00 \n2019-20 08.50 \n2020-21 05.00 \n\n2021-22 12.00 \nTOTAL 65.50  \n \nIn addition, Rs.25.06 crore has been released by NSDF to SAI from \n2017-18 to till date for Out of Pocket Allowance (OPA) under TOPS. \n  \n(d):   Karate is not included in Paris 2024 Olympic Games. \n  \n(e):  At present, there is no such proposal. \n  \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |332", "t": "Celebration of Azadi Ka Amrit Mahotsava", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 332 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021) \n \nCELEBRATION OF AZADI KA AMRIT MAHOTSAVA \n \n332. SHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of programmes organized by the Government in pursuit of \ncelebration of Azadi Ka Amrit Mahotsava; and \n(b) the details of the programmes proposed to be organized during the cours e of \ncelebration of Azadi Ka Amrit Mahotsav in the days ahead? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND  \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n \n(a) & (b) Celebrations of Azadi Ka Amrit Mahotsav  commenced on \n12.03.2021 starting a 75 week countdown to 75 th anniversary of our country’s \nIndependence and will end post a year on 15 th August, 2023. It is an intensive, \ncountrywide campaign focusing on citizen participation as Jan Andolan with a \nwhole-of-Government approach with large number of events being organized by \nthe Central Government, State Governments, etc., in various parts of the \ncountry. The Ministry of Culture, which is the nodal ministry for celebrations of \nAzadi Ka Amrit Mahotsav, has info rmed that up to 24 th November, 2021, 6714 \nprogrammes have been organized by the various Ministries of the Government \nof India under the Mahotsav. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |333", "t": "Agricultural Systems", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 333\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30\nth\n \nNOVEMBER, 2021\n \n \nAGRICULTURAL SYSTEMS\n \n \n333. \n \nSHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n  \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the pandemic has exposed both the strengths and the vulnerabilities of \nIndia‘s agri food systems, and has also emphasized the need for agr\nicultural market \nreforms and digital solutions to connect farmers to markets, create safety nets, ensure \nreasonable working conditions, and decentralize the agri food systems to make them \nmore resilient; and\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details of the steps taken by th\ne Government in this regard, and if not, \nthe reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n       \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH\n \nTOMAR)\n \n(a) & (b):\n \nAs per Provisional Estimates of Annual National Income, released \nby \nNational Statistical Office (NSO), Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation \n(MoSPI) on 31st, May 2021, the share of Agriculture and Allied Sectors in total Gross \nValue Added (GVA) of the country has increased from 18.4% in 2019\n-\n20 to 20.2% in\n \n2020\n-\n21 at current prices.\n \n       \nThe Gross Value Added of Agriculture and Allied Sectors at current price has \nincreased from 33.94 lakh crore in 2019\n-\n20 to 36.16 lakh crore in 2020\n-\n21.\n \n \n \n \n \nIn order to facilitate the marketing of agricultural produce of \nthe farmers during \nthe COVID\n-\n19 pandemic, the Government had issued advisory to States/Union \nTerritories (UTs) to facilitate Direct Marketing, enabling direct purchase from the \nfarmers/ Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs)/ Cooperatives etc. by liberalizin\ng \nregulations under respective State Agricultural Produce Market Committee (APMC) \nActs.\n \n\n \nThe Government also issued advisories to States/UTs to ensure inter\n-\nstate and \nintra\n-\nstate movement of vehicles carrying the agriculture produce in addition to setting \nup of monitoring cell to keep watch and extend immediate help for seamless movement \nof such vehicles including coordination for issue of permits to trucks.\n \n \n \nFurther, in order to decongest wholesale markets, FPO trading module has been \nlaunched to facilita\nte FPOs to trade their agri produce from their collection centre \nwithout bringing the produce to APMC. Warehouse based trading module was also \nlaunched to enable farmers to sell their produce from WDRA registered warehouses \nnotified as deemed market\n \n \nFurth\ner, to address the transportation issues of Farmers and other stake holders \nduring Covid\n-\n19, the Government has launched the Kisan Rath Mobile App on \n17.04.2020.\n \n \nThe App facilitates the Farmers, FPOs and Traders to\n \npost their load \nrequests which are sent \nto transport aggregators and individual transporters who in turn \nrespond to the load requests.\n \n \nThe Government has taken necessary measures to ensure smooth sowing and \nharvesting operations during COVID\n-\n19 pandemic.\n \n \nAs per 3\nrd\n \nadvance estimates for \n2020\n-\n2\n1, total food grain production in the country is estimated at record 305.44 million \ntones which is higher by 7.94 million tonnes than the production of food grain during \n2019\n-\n20. Similarly, total horticulture production in 2020\n-\n21 is estimated to a record \n331.05 million tones which is an increase of about 10.6 million tone over that achieved \nin 2019\n-\n20.\n \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |334", "t": "Departmental Standing Committee at Village/Block Level", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 334 \nANSWERED ON-30.11.2021 \n  \nDEPARTMENTAL STANDING COMMITTEE AT VILLAGE/BLOCK LEVEL \n \n†334. SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n           \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the Government proposes to constitute the departmental Standing \nCommittee/Advisory Committee at the village level in all the panchayats of the country, \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to constitute the departme ntal Standing \nCommittee/Advisory Committee at block level across the country; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) whether the Government in coordination with the State Government of Haryana \nproposes to constitute the departmental Standing Committee/Advisory Committee, if \nso, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n  \n(a) & (b) No, Sir. \n \n(c) Question does not arise. \n \n(d) No, Sir. \n \n*** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |335", "t": "Utilization of Funds under MGNREGS", "head": "������������������� �\n������������������������������������ �\n����������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�\n��������������������� ��� �\n������������������ ���������� �\n�\n�������������������������������� �\n�\n���� ��� ��������������������� ���\n������������������� ���\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n���� ���� ����� ���� ����������� ��� ���������� ����� ���������� ���������\n��������������� �\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������� �\n������ ���������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������� �\n���� ���� ������� ��� ����������� ���� ���������� �������������� ���������\n��������������������������������������� �\n���� ���� �������� ���� ���� ������� ��� ������ ��� ��� ��������� ������������\n�������� ������ ���� ������� ������� ����� ��� ���� ����� ������ ������ ����\n���������������������� ��������������������������� ����������� �\n���� �������� ���� ���������� ���� ������ ������ ����� ���������� ����\n������������������������������������������������ �������������������\n����������������������������������������� �\n�\n������ �\n�\n�������������������������������������������� �\n�������������������������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n������\n\n���������� ������������ ��� ������ ��� ������ ������ ����� ��� ����\n������ �������� ��������� ���� ��� ����� ������� ��� ���������� �\n���������� ����������� ����� ���������� �������� ��� ��� �����\n�������� �������� � ���� ����� ����� ��������� ���� ����������������\n� ���������� � ��������� �\n�\n�\n������������� ��� ������� �� ����� ��� ���������� ���� ������������� ����� ���\n������� ������������ ��� ��������� ������ ����� �������������� ����\n��������� ���� ����������� ���� ����������� � � ������� ������� ����������\n����������������������� ������� ������������������������������� ����\n�������������������������� �\n�\n��� �������������������������������������������������� ���\n���� �������������� ��� ������� �������� ���������� �������\n��������������� �\n����� ������������������������������������������������������� �\n���� ����������������������� �\n�\n����� ���� ���������� ��� ���������� ���� �������� ������������ � ��\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������� ������������������\n�������������������������� ��\n�\n���������� ����� ��������� ����� ����������� ���������� ������������� ������\n�������������������� �������� �������������������������� �\n�\n���������������� ��������������������������������������������������\n����� ��� ������� ������������ ��� ��������� ������������ ��� �����������\n������������� �� ������ ���� ��������������� ���� ��������� ��� ����� �� ��\n��� ������������������������������� ���������������� �\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������� ������� ������� ������������� ������� ���� ������ ��������\n����� ������ ���� ��� ����� ��� ���� ������ ��� ������ ���� ������� �����\n��������� ��� ���� ������� ����������� �� ������������� ��� ���� � ��������� ���\n���������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������� ���������� ����\n�\n��� �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |336", "t": "Gender mainstreaming in Agriculture", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *336 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 21ST DECEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 30, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nSECURITY TO JOURNALISTS/MEDIA PERSONS \n \n*336.     KUNWAR DANISH ALI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that incidents of attack on  media persons/murder of \njournalists, etc. are on the rise in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the total number of  journalists injured/killed \nseparately, guilty persons  arrested and the action taken against them during \neach of the last five years and the current year, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has received any report/  recommendations from the \nPress Council of India on the safety of media persons/journalists; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the action taken by  the Government in this \nregard; \n \n(e) whether the Government has constituted any  committee to investigate the \nattacks on journalists and settle the case within a specified time frame and if so, \nthe details thereof; and \n \n(f) the other steps taken by the Government for the  protection of journalists, \nbloggers, scribes, reporters, Newspaper Offices and TV Stations? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.S.Q.NO.*336 FOR 21.12.2021 \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *336 \nFOR 21.12.2021 REGARDING SECURITY TO JOURNALISTS/MEDIA PERSONS \n \n‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects as per the Seventh Schedule to \nthe Constitution of India. The responsibilities of maintaining law and order, \nincluding safety and security of journalists, rests primarily with the respective \nState Gov ernments. National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) does not maintain \nspecific data on cases in respect of journalists. \n \nThe Government is committed to ensure the right to freedom of speech and \nexpression enshrined under the Article 19 of the Constitution of In dia.  Press \nCouncil of India (PCI), a statutory autonomous body, set up under the Press \nCouncil Act, 1978 considers complaints filed ‘by the Press’ concerning \ncurtailment of press freedom, physical assault/attack on journalists etc.  PCI is \nalso empowered to take suo-motu cognizance in matters on the pressing issues \nconcerning freedom of Press and safeguarding of its high standards. \n \nThe Press Council of India had constituted a Sub -Committee to examine the \nissue of Safety of Journalists. The report of Sub -Committee submitted on \n23.07.2015 carried various recommendations with regard to safety and security \nof Journalists. The existing laws are adequate for protection of citizens including \njournalists.  \n \n\n \n-3- \nL.S.S.Q.NO.*336 FOR 21.12.2021 \n \nCentral Government attaches the highest importance to the safety and \nsecurity of citizens of the country including the journalists. The Ministry of Home \nAffairs have issued advisories to States and UTs, from time to time, to maintain \nlaw and order and ensure that any person who takes law into his/her own hand is \npunished promptly as per law. An advisory specifically on safety of journalists \nwas issued to States/UTs on 20 th October, 2017 requesting them to strictly \nenforce the law to ensure safety and security of media persons. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |337", "t": "Schemes for Animal Husbandry and Dairying", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  MINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.337 \n ANSWERED ON 21.12.2021 \n   \nAllocation of Funds to NSFs \n \n*337. SHRI BHOLA SINGH: \n  SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH  AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \na) whether the Government allocates funds to the recognize d National \nSports Federation (NSFs) in the country; \n b) if so, the details of funds allocated to the recognized Sports \nFederations in the country since 2018, year and Federation –wise; \nc) the utilization of these funds by the Sports Federation s during the said \nperiod, year and Federation-wise; \nd) whether any instance s of misuse of funds by NSFs have come to \nnotice during the said period; \ne) if so, the details thereof and the remedial action taken by the \nGovernment thereon; and \nf) whether the Government has undertaken an annual audit of these \nSports Federation s and if so, whether any cases of financial \nmismanagement have been identified and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n \na) to f) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\n2 \n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFEERED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (f) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 337 FOR REPLY ON 21.12.2021 ASKED \nBY SHRI BHOLA SINGH  AND SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR  REGARDING \nALLOCATION OF FUNDs TO NSFs \n \n(a)Yes Sir. Government allocates funds to the National Sports Federations \n(NSFs) under the Scheme of Assistance to NSFs for promotion of sport for \nwhich it is recognised. \n \n( b) & (c) Funds under the Scheme of Assistance to NSFs are disbursed  by \nthe Ministry  through Sports Authority of India  (SAI), which is an \nautonomous body under the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports. SAI \nfurnishes the Utilisation Certificates for the funds transferred to the NSFs , \nand for the expenditure incurred by SAI to organise coaching and training \ncamps proposed by NSFs in SAI centres across the country . SAI also \nmeets expenditure on international travelling, boarding and lodging, entry \nfee and other incidental expenses of Indian teams/players selected for \ninternational competitions and sporting events by the NSFs.  In the case of \nfunds transferred by SAI to NSFs, 75% of the admissible amount is \ndisbursed as advance, while the remaining amount is provided on \nreimbursement basis. The details of funds allocated under th e Scheme of \nAssistance to NSFs and expenditure since 2018 are given in Annexure -I \nand Annexure-II respectively. \n \n(c) to (f) No instances of misuse of funds by N SFs have come to the notice \nof the Government during the said period. NSFs receiving funds of Rs1 \ncrore and above are subject to audit by the CAG of India. Audit of select \nNSFs is done by CAG and audit findings/o bservations are sent to the \nconcerned NSFs for necessary action  at their end. Settlement of \nobservations and audit paras are taken up by the respective NSFs with the \n\n3 \n \nOffice of CAG.  In order to ensure transparency and accountability in the \nfunctioning of NSFs, the Ministry has, since 2010, introduced the system of \nannual recognition, under which recognition is renewed every year on  \nsubmission of relevant documents, including the audited annual accounts \nof NSFs. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n4 \n \nAnnexure – I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (b) to (c) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 337 FOR REPLY ON 21.12.2021 ASKED \nBY SHRI BHOLA SINGH AND SHRI VINOD KUMAR SONKAR  REGARDING \nALLOCATION OF FUNDS \n \n \nBUDGET ALLOCATION UNDER ASSISTANCE TO NSF SCHEME \n \n(In ₹Crore) \n \nSl. \nNo. \nDiscipline 2018-\n2019 \n2019-20 up \nto Sep, \n2020 \n2020-21 2021-22 \n1 Archery Association of \nIndia \n18.53 10.19 12.15** 6.92 \n2 Athletic Federation of \nIndia \n42.95 55.00* 32.52** 12.84 \n3 Badminton Association of \nIndia \n21.00 33.27* 27.25** 13.36 \n4 Boxing Federation of \nIndia \n47.00 53.79* 36.09** 10.87 \n5 Hockey India 37.5 36.18* 30.34** 12.54 \n6 Judo Federation of India 5.76 5.62 Nil 4.08 \n7 National Rifle \nAssociation of India \n38.61 28.40* 27.15** 8.25 \n8 Indian Weightlifting \nFederation \n8.14 10.96* 10.23** 5.28 \n9 Wrestling Federation of \nIndia \n23.56 34.50* 16.91** 6.70 \n10 Gymnastics Federation of \nIndia \n3.31 2.55 Nil 2.53 \n11 The Indian Golf Union 3.93 2.70* 0.78 2.04 \n \n\n5 \n \nDiscipline 2018-\n2019 \n2019-20 up \nto Sep, \n2020 \n2020-21 2021-22 \n12 Amateur Kabaddi \nFederation of India \n3.03 1.60 0.90 1.15 \n13 Rowing Federation of \nIndia \n5.62 8.50 2.00 3.50 \n \n \n14 Squash Racket \nFederation of India \n3.52 2.93* 1.18 2.04 \n15 Swimming Federation of \nIndia \n7.89 7.05 3.29 3.32 \n16 All India Tennis \nAssociation \n3.70 2.40* 2.70 4.89 \n17 Wushu Association of \nIndia \n4.41 6.00* 2.75 4.03 \n18 Yachting Association of \nIndia \n4.78 7.58* Nil 4.28 \n19 Bridge Federation of \nIndia \n1.83 1.50 0.49 1.50 \n20 Basketball Federation of \nIndia \n5.24 4.60* 5.09 3.00 \n21 Billiards & Snooker \nFederation of India \n3.2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |338", "t": "Setting-up of Food Processing Industry Parks in Bihar", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 338   \nANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \n  \nSETTING-UP OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRY PARKS IN BIHAR  \n  \n338.  SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR: \n \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the food processing industry parks being set up in Bihar and its current \nstatus location-wise; and \n \n(b) the role being played by the Government in the development of food processing industry \nparks  being set up in Bihar? \n \n  \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL)  \n  \n(a) & (b):   The Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) has been implementing Central \nSector Umbrella Scheme - Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY) since 2016 -\n17 for overall growth and development of food processing sector including setting up of Mega \nFood Parks & Agro Processing Clusters(Mini Food Parks) across the country including Bihar. \nUnder corresponding component schemes of PMKSY, MoFPI provides credit linked financial \nassistance (capital subsidy) in the form of Grants-in-Aid to entrepreneurs for setting up of Mega \nFood Parks and  Agro Processing Clusters. Also, a Special Food Processing Fund of Rs. 2000 \ncrore was set up with NABARD in November 2014 to provide affordable credit for investments \nin setting up Mega Food Parks as well as processing units in the MFPs. In 2019, the coverage \nof the fund was extended to setting up of Agro Processing Clusters and individual \nmanufacturing units within them. \n  \nThe status of Mega Food Parks and Agro Processing Clusters, assisted  under PMKSY \nin Bihar, is placed at Annexure. \n \n \n***** \n  \n\n  \n \nANNEXURE  \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) AND (b) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 338 FOR ANSWER ON 30 TH NOVEMBER, 2021 ON \nSETTING UP OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRY PARKS IN BIHAR.   \n  \n Details of Mega Food Parks assisted under PMKSY in   Bihar:   \nSr. \nNo \n. \nProject \nName District \nDate of \nFinal \nApproval \n  \nProject \nCost \n(INR \nin \nCr) \n  \nGrant \napproved \n(INR in \nCr) \n  \nGrant \nreleased \n(INR in \nCr) \nStatus \n1. \nPristine  \nMega Food \nPark Pvt.  \nLtd.,  \nKhagaria 06.08.2014 \n120.13 41.9 34.81 \nUnder \nImplementation \n2. \nBihar  \nIndustrial  \nArea  \nDevelopment \nAuthority \n(BIADA),  \nMuzaffarpur \n  \nThe project has received In-principle approval  \n  \n   Details of Agro-Processing Clusters assisted under PMKSY in Bihar:  \n  \nSr. \nNo \n. \nName of the \nProject \nExecution  \nAgency  \nLocation of \nthe Project \nDistrict Date of \napproval  \nProjec \nt cost \n(In  \nRs.Cr.) \nGrant  \nApprove \nd (in Rs.  \nCr.) \nStatus \n1 M/s \nShreekangan \nFood Park \nAgapur, PS  \nMansurchak \n, Sub- \nDivision \nTeghra, Distt.  \nBegusarai,  \nBihar \n Begusarai 15.03.201 \n9 \n30.434 7.9081 Under  \nImplementation \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |339", "t": "Implementation of PMGSY-III", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF YOUTH AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NUMBER *339 \nANSWERED ON 21.12.2021 \nNational Young Leaders Programme \n*339. SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS  be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether the Covid- 19 pandemic caused disruption in the \nactivities under the National Young Leaders Programme; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the measures taken by the Government to keep the \ndevelopment programmes for the youth active during the COVID-19 \nPandemic? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n**** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c ) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.*339 FOR 21 .12.2021 \nREGARDING NATIONAL YOUNG LEADERS PROGRAMME ASKED BY \nHON’BLE MP SHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \n(a & b) Yes, the COVID-19 pandemic caused disruption in the \nactivities under the National Young Leaders Programme(NYLP) . The \nscheme(NYLP) is based on people to people interaction and under \nit, an institutional platform is  created in the form of Neighbourhood \nYouth Parliaments (NYP), where the youth in the age group of 15- 29 \nyears, articulate their views on various issues affecti ng their lives \nand draw attention of the local administration. As per the \nGovernment Advisory , physical gathering was restricted and mass \nbased programs were not possible due to COVID-19 pandemic. \n (c) (i) The COVID-19 restrictions put a limitation on the \nconduct of programs  with physical gathering. T he Ministry in \nparticipation with its autonomous /subordinate bodies, Nehru Yuva \nKendra Sangathan(NYKS) & National Service Scheme(NSS)  \norganized various  programmes including NYLP  for youth through \nvirtual mode during the COVID-19 pandemic in the year 2020-2021 to \nkeep the development programmes for the youth active during the \nCOVID-19 Pandemic. The details of activities undertaken through \nvirtual mode are placed at ANNEXURE-I. \n(ii) Although the COVID-19 restrictions hampered the regular \nprogrammes of the Departm ent, the Ministry through its NYKS & \nNSS volunteers conducted various awareness and education \nactivities on prevention of COVID- 19 pandemic. These volunteers \nshared verified information and messages explaining prevention \n\nmethods such as hand washing, wearing of face mask and \nmaintaining safe social distancing, sanitization and observance of \nlockdown for safety. The IEC Materials on Dos and Don' ts were used \nto make people aware about the pandemic and precaution they \nshould take to minimize the risk of infection. Explanatory videos and \nSocial Media were also the part of Awareness and Education on \npreventive methods. NYKS & NSS volunteers actively took part as \nfrontline workers in National Campaign to counter stigma and \ndiscrimination against COVID- 19. Details are placed at ANNEXURE -\nII. \n**** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c)  (i) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.*339 FOR 21.12.2021 REGARDING \nNATIONAL YOUNG LEADERS PROGRAMME ASKED BY HON’BLE MP \nSHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM: \nDETAILS OF ACTIVITIES UNDERTAKEN THROUGH VIRTUAL MODE \nDURING COVID-19 PANDEMIC: \ni. Harnessing Youth Power to combat COVID-19 \nii. Ek Bharat Shrestha Bharat \niii.  International Yoga Day \niv. Fit India campaign \nv. Observance of Constitution Day and subsequent activities \nvi. Jan Andolan-  Educating and Motivating People to observe \nAppropriate Behaviour Norms to Combat COVID- 19 \nvii. Dissemination of Information on Salient Features of National \nEducation Policy 2020 \nviii. Gandagi Mukt Bharat Abhiyan \nix. Catch the Rain Project in collaboration with National Water \nMission, Ministry of Jal Shakti. \nx. National Youth Parliament Festivals. \nxi. Webinars on Investor Education, Awareness and Fund \nProtection Authority (IPEAFPA) (Ministry of Corporate Affiars) \nxii. Webinars National Human Rights Commission. \nxiii. The 24 th National Youth Festival, 2021 was organized by the \nDepartment in virtual mode. \nxiv. RGNIYD, conducted various lectures & Workshops through \nvirtual mode on crucial  topics as COVID -19 sensitization, \n\ncareer for students, positive engagement for youth, mental \nhealth and emotional well being, prevention of substance \nabuse, gender sensitization, digital skills, sustainable \ndevelopment, entrepreneurship development, etc. \nxv. To keep the youth of the country engaged, the  Department \nresorted to conducting/participating International Youth \nExchange Programmes (IYEP) in virtual format. Apart from \nparticipating in various youth programs/conferences the \nDepartment conducted few important summits such as: \na. 7th BRICS Youth Summit under India’s chairship was \nheld in online platform from 29-31 August, 2021. \nb. 1st IBSA (India -Brazil-South Africa) Youth Summit under \nIndia’s chairship was held in online platform from 26- 28 \nAugust, 2021. \n*** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-II \nANNE"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |34", "t": "Pre-2016 Pensioners", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 34 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29.11.2021 \n \nPre-2016 Pensioners \n34 Shri Natarajan P.R.:  \n \n          Will the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n(a) the number of pre -2016 pensioners of higher educational institutions of \nAvinashilingam Higher Studies University at Coimbatore in Tamil Nadu; \n(b) whether the pension arrears of these penserions have not been paid till date;  \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the steps taken or proposed to be taken by the Government for the settlement of \npension arrear of these pensioners? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (d): The number of pre -2016 pensioners of Avinashilingam Institute for Home \nScience and Higher Education for Women (Deemed to be University), Coimbatore, Tamil \nNadu is 147. The UGC has released an amount of Rs. 800.00 Lakhs as arrears of pension \nto Avinashilingam Institute for Home Science and Higher Education for Women \n(Deemed to be University), Coimbatore, Tamil Nadu during Financial Year 2019-20.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |340", "t": "Original Equipment Manufacturers", "head": "������������������� �\n����������������������������� �\n��������� �\n�������������������������� �\n���������������������� �\n�\n�������������������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ���� ����������� �������������� �������� ���� �������� �����������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ��� ����� �������� ���� ����������� �������� ��� ��������� ������� �������� ����������� ��������\n����������� ���������� ��� ��������� ������� ������ ����� �������� ������������������� ����\n���������������������������������������������\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ���� �������� �� ����� ����������� ����������� �������� ����� �������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n��������������������������������\n������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������ �\n������������������������� �\n�\n������������ �������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���������\n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |341", "t": "Zonal Council Meeting", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 341 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 9, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nZONAL COUNCIL MEETING \n \n341.   SHRI ARVIND DHARMAPURI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government regularly conducts Zonal Council Meetings; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Southern Zonal Council met recently; \n \n(d) if so, the list of Chief Ministers or  representatives of States who attended \nthis meeting and all the previous meetings held during the last three years; \n \n(e) whether the Government keeps the record of  Chief Ministers who have not \nattended the Southern Zonal Council Meetings; and \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof during the aforesaid period? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a) to (c): From 1 st January, 2015 to 28 th February, 2020, four Meetings of \nEastern Zonal Council, three meetings of Central Zonal Council, three \nMeetings of Northern Zonal Council, four meetings of Western Zonal Councils \nand three meetings of Southern Zonal Council were held.  Thereafter, Zonal \nCouncil meetings could not take place due to prevalent Covid -19 situation.  \nRecently, a meeting of Southern Zonal Council has been held on 14 th \nNovember 2021.   \n-2/… \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.341 FOR 30.11.2021 \n \n(d) to (f): The Chief Ministers of Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka and Puducherry; \nMinister for Education, Tamil Nadu; Finance Minister and Minister for Revenue \nand Housing, Kerala; Home Minister, Telangana; Lt. Governors of Puducherry \nand Andaman & Nicobar Islands; Administrator of Lakshadweep; the Minister \nof Health & Medical Education, Karnataka; Minister for Finance, Planning, \nLegislative Affairs & Commercial Taxes, Andhra Pradesh; and Minister for \nPanchayati Raj, Andhra Pradesh attended the Southern Zonal Council meeting \nwhich was held on November 14, 2021 at Tirupati. \n \nMeeting of Southern Zonal Council was previously held on 18.9.2018 at \nBengaluru which was attended by the Chief Ministers of Karnataka and \nPuducherry; Deputy Chief Minister, Tamil Nadu; Minister for Tourism, \nKarnataka; Minister for Social Just ice & Empowerment, Karnataka; Minister \nfor Finance, Andhra Pradesh; Minister for Finance and Coir, Kerala; Minister \nfor Health, Puducherry;  and Lt. Governor of A&N Islands. \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |342", "t": "National Crop Forecast Centre for Yield Estimation", "head": "                                              \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 342\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER, 2021\n \n \nNational Crop \nForecast Centre for Yield Estimation\n \n \n342.\n \nSHRI JAYADEV GALLA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe\n \ndetails of the pilot projects undertaken under the Mahalanobis National Crop \nForecast Centre to develop yield estimation at Gram Panchayat level and the status of \nprojects thereof;\n \n \n(b)\n \nwhether the Government aims to undertake any scheme to assess accurat\ne crop \nyield/loss at insurance unit for quick claims under Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana;\n \n \n(c)\n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(d)\n \nwhether\n \nany data has been collected after the approval of the DGCA for flying of \ndrone to collect the data in the 100 pilot districts for paddy and wheat crops; and\n \n \n(e)\n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \n      \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n       \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) :\n \nThe\n \nGovernment\n \nof\n \nIndia\n \np\nlan\ns\n \nto\n \nmigrate\n \ntowards\n \ntechnology\n \nbased\n \nyield\n \nestimation from the conventional Crop Cutting Experiment (CCE) based yield estimation \napproach. Towards this, few\n \npilot studies have been initiated by taking various \nGovernment and Private Agencies On\n-\nboard. During Kharif 2019, pilot studies were \nconducted through 12 agencies in 64 districts of 15 States for 9 crops envisaging \ninnovative technologies\n \nsuch as High Reso\nlution Satellite data (Optical and microwave), \nUnmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV),\n \nAdvanced\n \nmulti\n-\nparameter\n \ncrop\n \nmodels,\n \nMobile\n \nApplications\n \nfor\n \nField\n \nData\n \nCollection,\n \nArtificial\n \nintelligence/\n \nMachine\n \nlearning\n \napproach,\n \nSensor\n \nNetworks,\n \nInternet\n \nof\n \nThings,\n \nField based digital photographs, Hand held \ninstruments and Scientifically designed Sampling\n \nPlans. These approaches\n \nwere\n \nvalidated in Rabi 2019\n-\n20 in 15 blocks of 6\n \nStates.\n \n\nThe pilot studies were scaled up to 100 districts with the help of\n \n07 agencies for \nPaddy \ncrop\n \nin Kharif \n-\n \n2020, and for Paddy and Wheat in Rabi season of 2020\n-\n21 in 100 \ndistricts of 13\n \nstates. The agencies have submitted the technical reports to Mahalanobis \nNational Crop Forecast Centre (MNCFC).\n \nFurther, the Expression of Interest for Yi\neld estimation of Non\n-\nCereals (other than \npaddy and\n \nwheat) has been initiated. Total 20 agencies/organisations (10 Government \nand 10 Private)\n \nhave\n \nbeen identified. Tentative time\n \nperiod for this study\n \nis Rabi 2021\n-\n22 and Kharif\n \n2022.\n \n(b) & (c) : \nTowards\n \nassessing\n \naccurate\n \ncrop\n \nyield/loss\n \nat\n \ninsurance\n \nunit\n, the \nGovernment\n \nhas\n \ntaken\n \nan\n \ninitiative to develop a protocol for Crop Yield estimation at \nGram\n \nPanchayat\n \nlevel\n \nby\n \nusing\n \nInnovative\n \nTechnologies.\n \nFor\n \nthis,\n \nvarious\n \nTechnology\n \nagencies\n \n(Government & priva\nte both national and international) have been deployed, \nwhich have showcased the various yield estimation approaches such as Multi\n-\nlinear \nregression, machine\n \nlearning,\n \nadvanced\n \nsimulation\n \nmodelling,\n \nlight\n-\nuse\n \nefficiency\n \nmodels,\n \nFood and Agriculture Organiz\nation Evapo\n-\ntranspiration (\nFAO\n-\nET) Model, \nLeast\n \nAbsolute\n \nShrinkage and\n \nSelection\n \nOperator (LASSO), and Machine Learning that \ninvolve advanced analytic\n \ntools, like Google Earth Engine, Python, Internet of Things \n(IoT). In this regard, the agencies have subm\nitted the final work report, the applicability \nand feasibility of which, will be worked out by the Technical Experts Committee.\n \n(d) & (e): \nDuring Kharif 2020, approval of Directorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) \nhas been sought for flying the UAVs in\n \n1\n00 pilot study districts\n \nincluding\n \ndrone\n \nbased\n \nimaging\n \nin\n \n37\n \ndistricts\n \nfor\n \npaddy\n \ncrop.\n \n \n \n***\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |343", "t": "Attacks on Fishermen", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 343 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30th NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \nAttacks on Fishermen \n \n343. PROF. SOUGATA RAY \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has noticed that a large number of fishermen were attacked \nby armed forces of neighbouring countries; \n \n(b) if so, the steps taken to protect the lives of our fishermen; and  \n \n(c) the details of such attacks by Pakistan and Sri Lanka during the last three years? \n \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a)  to (c): Incidences of Indian fishing boats  being attacked by maritime agencies of Sri \nLanka and Pakistan have been reported. The Government of India attach es highest priority \nto the safety and security of Indian fishermen. As soon as reports  of attack on Indian \nfishermen were received, Government of India took up the matter with both these countries. \nThe Government of India has lodged its strong protest s with the Governments of Sri Lanka \nand Pakistan in all such instances and continues to rei terate to the se Governments to \nconsider the issue of fishermen as a humanitarian and livelihood matter.  To effectively \naddress the issues  relating to Indian fishermen, their safety and security between India and \nSri Lanka, over the years, bilateral Joint Working Group (JWG) mechanism and meeting of \nthe Honourable Ministers of Fisheries of India and Sri Lanka ha ve been institutionalized.  \nThe last round of the JWG was held in December, 2020, wherein both the G overnments \ndiscussed the entire gamut of issues related to fishermen.  \n \n Government of India is providing assistance to the fisherman under its schemes.  I n \norder to diversify the fishing activities  of Indian fishermen by providing assistance to \nfishermen of Palk Bay area to enabling them  to take up deep -sea fishing including tuna \nfishing, a subcomponent ‘Assistance to Deep Sea Fishing’ was introduced under the Blue \nRevolution Scheme of the Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry \nand Dairying.  An amount of Rs. 300 crore was released during 2017 -18 towards subsidy to \nthe Government of Tamil Nadu for procurement of deep-sea fishing vessels under this \nscheme and for construction of Fish Landing Centre at Kunthakal in Rameshwaram district of \nTamil Nadu. Training is also provided to fishermen on deep sea fishing and  handling \ntechniques. Blue Revolution Scheme also had another component  to provide f inancial \nassistance for promoting open sea cage culture as alternative livelihood support to coastal \nfishermen and their families for diversification. \n\n  \n \nThe Department of Fisheries is implementing a flagship scheme namely “Pradhan \nMantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) with an investment of Rs. 20,050 crore  for a \nperiod of 5 years  with effect from the financial year 2020-21 to  2024-25 in all States  and \nUnion Territories . Under PMMSY, financial su pport is provided to fishermen for \ncommunication/tracking and Potential Fishing Zone (PFZ) devices, safety kits besides \nlivelihood and nutritional supports as well as insurance. PMMSY also provides for \nmultipurpose support services to fishermen through Sagar Mitras. \n \n PMMSY also provides for financial assistance for promotion of seaweed cultivation, \nopen sea cage culture, seed rearing, bivalve cultivation, recreational fisheries  and sea \nranching to marine fishers for diversification of livelihood.  \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |344", "t": "Payment of Wages to MGNREGS Workers", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 344 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nPAYMENT OF WAGES TO MGNREGS WORKERS \n \n344.  MS. S. JOTHIMANI: \nSHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether there have been delays in the payment of wages to workers under Mahatma \nGandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS), if so, the \ndetails thereof State-wise and district-wise particularly in Uttar Pradesh; \n(b)  the reasons for the delay in payments to MGNREGS workers in various States; \n(c)  the details of wage payments that are pending approval from the Central Government \nacross the country, for work done between April and October; \n(d)  the details of wage payments that are pending in the State of Tamil Nadu for work \ndone between April and October; \n(e)  whether the Government has taken measures to expedite the approval of wage \npayments to MGNREGS workers and ensured that the previous payments are \ndistributed; and \n(f)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a),(b),(d)to(f): Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme is a demand \ndriven scheme. Fund release to States/UTs is a continuous   process and Central Government \nis committed to making funds available keeping in view, the demand for work. The Ministry \nseeks additional funds from Ministry of Finance as and when required for meeting the \ndemand for work on the ground.   \nThe first installment of the first tranche is released in the first half of April after \nadjusting unspent balance available with the States a nd considering the pending liabilities, if \nany. If the proposal for 2 nd Tranche is submitted on and after 1 st October, then the Audit \nReport and Audited Utilization Certificate of the previous financial year are required. The \nfunds can only be released aft er receipt of Audited Utilization Certificate and Audit report of \nprevious financial year submitted by the State. As on date 98.99% FTOs have been generated \nin stipulated time period. No wage payment is pending as on date in the State of Tamil Nadu \nfor the months of April to October.  \n(c): State/UT-wise status of pending Fund Transfer Orders (FTOs) till October, 2021, under \nMahatma Gandhi NREGA is given at Annexure.   \n  \n****** \n \n\nAnnexure referred in reply to part (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.344 dated 30.11.2021 \n  \nState/UT-wise status of pending FTOs till October, 2021, under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGA \nSl. No. States/UTs \nPending FTOs (Rs. in lakh) \nSC ST Others Total \n1 Andhra Pradesh  13467.03   15692.98 29160.00 \n2 Bihar      29722.25 29722.25 \n3 Himachal Pradesh  3760.30   11328.31 15088.60 \n4 Jharkhand      21122.10 21122.10 \n5 Karnataka    3241.23   3241.23 \n6 Mizoram      54.37 54.37 \n7 Nagaland     2277.99 2277.99 \n8 Punjab  11298.36 15.52   11313.88 \n9 West Bengal    10869.13 103475.59 114344.72 \n10 Ladakh     54.23 54.23 \n  Total 28525.69 14125.88 183727.80 226379.37 \n  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |345", "t": "Creation of Assets under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 345 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nCREATION OF ASSETS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n345.  SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of assets that have been created  under the MGNREGS scheme in Uttar \nPradesh \n(b)  whether the Government evaluates the value generated from those assets; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)  whether the quality of assets created under the  scheme are monitored and evaluated \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)      Mahatma Gandhi NREGS is a demand driven wage employment scheme with bottom \nup planning. The works are identified, approved and prioritized out of shelf of work by the \nGram Panchayat, recommended by Gram Sabha in each State/UT as per the provision of the \nAct. Accordingly, the works are approved and taken up by Panchayat or other implementing \nagencies as per the demand.  The planning of works is done as per the estimated person days \nto be generated for the work in a financial year at the time of asses sment of Labour Budget \n(LB). The details of category -wise completed and ongoing works   along with \nexpenditure under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS in the State of Uttar Pradesh   in the current \nfinancial year 2021-22 (as on 26.11.2021) is given at Annexure. \n  \n(b) to (d)   The responsibility of implementation of Mahatma Gandhi NREGA is vested with \nthe Government of States/UTs. The Ministry has a comprehensive system of monitoring and \nreview mechanism for Mahatma Gandhi NREGS. Some of the important elements of the \nabove framework are listed below: \n \n(i). The Ministry regularly reviews the performance of the implementation of Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS in States/UTs through various fora viz., Mid -Term Review, Labour \nBudget meetings, Labour Budget Revision meetings, Programme Rev iew meetings. \nCentral Employment Guarantee Council and State Employment Guarantee Councils \nperiodically monitor implementation of the programme. \n \n(ii). National Level Monitors, Common Review Missions and Officers of the Ministry \nvisit States/UTs at regular interval to review implementation of the programme. \nAfter the field visits, the findings/shortcomings and recommendations are shared \nwith the States/UTs for appropriate action at their end. \n \n\n \n(iii). Auditing Standards for Social Audit have been issued and States/UTs have been \nadvised to establish independent Social Audit Units, conduct Social Audit as per \nAudit of Scheme Rules, 2011 and training of vill age resource persons for \nconducting Social Audit etc. Internal Audit Teams of the Department also conduct \nregular audit. \n \n(iv). Steps have been taken to strengthen transparency and accountability which include \ngeo-tagging of assets, Direct Benefit Transfer  (DBT), National electronic Fund \nManagement System (NeFMS), Aadhar Based Payment System (ABPS), Software \nfor Estimate Calculation using rural rates for Employment (SECURE) and \nappointment of Ombudsperson in every district of States/UTs. \n  \n(v).  Steps have been taken for establishment of State Tech nical Cell at various level fo \nqualitative monitoring and supervision of works under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS. \n \n(vi). In order to ensure higher level of monitoring and oversight, National Mobile \nMonitoring System and A rea Officer App have been introduced. In the former, \nattendance of workers on a particular work, where more than 20 workers are \nemployed is taken daily along with a geo -tagged & time stamped photograph of \ntheirs. The latter has been designed to ensure that  field officials do conduct \ninspections in the requisite numbers and look into all the relevant aspects of the \nscheme. \n  \n(vii). In addition Central and State Employment Guarantee Councils, District \nDevelopment Coordination and Monitoring Committees (DISHA)  as also PRIs do \nalso take up the monitoring and oversight of the Programme. \n \n  \n  \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.345 d ue for \nanswer on 30.11.2021 \n  \nDetails of category wise completed and ongoing works under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS in Uttar Pradesh  in FY 2021-22 (as on 26.11.2021) \nName of the category \nCOMPLETED ONGOING \nNumber of \nworks \nExpenditure \n(Rs. In Lakh) \nNumber of \nworks \nExpenditure \n(Rs. In Lakh) \nRural Infrastructure 3641 796.92 52180 40865.77 \nBharat NirmanSewa Kendra 0 0 23 0 \nCoastal Areas 0 0 8 0.04 \nDrought Proofing 4785 155.99 223266 14259.51 \nFisheries 3 0 18 0 \nFlood Control and Protection 12962 1687.73 103895 30048.68 \nFood Grain 0 0 0 0 \nLand Development 60555 6794.68 499203 145477.65 \nMicro Irrigation Works 10115 1055.75 94209 21794.83 \nOther Works 83 0.25 1256 0 \nPlay Ground 4 0 71 0.04 \nRenovation of traditional water bodies 2641 684.02 25083 10436"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |346", "t": "Impersonating Voices of Judges", "head": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 346 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 9, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nIMPERSONATING VOICES OF JUDGES \n \n346.   SHRI A.K.P. CHINRAJ: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Delhi Police has received any  representation or complaint from \nSupreme Court Registrar Office in relation to impersonating voices of judges, if \nso, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether any First Information Report (FIR) or  complaint has been registered \nin Tilak Marg Police Station  in relation 3+6 to impersonating voices of judges; \nand \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a): Delhi Police has reported that no such complaint has been received from \nSupreme Court Registrar during the years 2017 to 2021 (upto 21.11.2021).  \n(b) and (c): Delhi Police has reported that no complaint/FIR has been registered \nin Tilak Marg Police Station during the years 2017 to 2021 (upto 21.11.2021) in \nrelation to impersonating voices of judges.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |347", "t": "DNA Survey of Child Beggars", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 347 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nDNA SURVEY OF CHILD BEGGARS \n \n347. SHRI VISHNU DATT SHARMA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is contemplating the  DNA survey of children \nbegging on streets so as to take  steps to reunite trafficked children with their \nbiological parents; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government is considering to create  a pan India database of \nDNA information for trafficked children and their biological parents; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) to (d): ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects under the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. The responsibility to maintain law and \norder, protection of life of citizens, including investigation and prosecution of \ncrime against children, rests with respective State Governments, who are \ncompetent to deal with such offences under the extant provisions of laws. \n \n \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 347 FOR 30.11.2021 \n Preventing and combating the crime of human trafficking and crime \nagainst children is a continuous and evolving process, which involves various \nfacets of technology and other efforts on the part of various stakeholders. A \nspecific proposal on DNA survey is not under consideration of the Ministry of \nHome Affairs. \n However, the Government of India has been supplementing the efforts of \nState Governments by providing them financial assistance to establish and \nstrengthen the infrastructure for preventing and combating the crime of \ntrafficking and by providing them guidance and  advice through various \nadvisories and Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) issued from time to time. \n  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |348", "t": "Cyber Crime Awareness", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 348 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCYBER CRIME AWARENESS \n \n348. SHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note that the  younger generation are \nmore vulnerable to cyber crimes; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to include  cyber crime awareness as \npart of curriculum of the  students apart from making stringent laws to control \nthe crime; and` \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) to (d):  With the enhanced use of cyber space, vulnerability of citizens \nincluding younger generation to cyber crimes has also increased.  The Central \nGovernment has taken the following measures to create cyber crime \nawareness: \n \n \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 348 FOR 30.11.2021 \n(i) Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has launched a „Cyber Security \nHandbook‟ to ensure safe and healthy digital habits among students. This \nmodule covers topics in cyber safety, such as cyber bullying, including social \nexclusion, intimidation, defa mation, and emotional harassment, online sexual \nabuse, online attack and frauds, and online enticement. The „Cyber Security \nHandbook‟ can be accessed at the following link:  \n[http://cbseacademic.nic.in/web_material/Manuals/Cyber_Safety_Manual.pdf] \n[http://cbseacadmic.nic.in/web_material/Manuals/ Cyber_Safety_Manual.pdf] \n(ii) Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT -In) under Ministry of \nElectronics & Information Technology has prepared guidelines to handle the \nsecurity concerns about various v ideo conferencing apps, which can be found \nat the following  link: \n[https://www.cert-in.org.in/s2cMainServlet?pageid=PUBWEL01]  \n[ https://www.cert-in.org.in/s2cMainServlet?pageid=PUBWEL01] \n(iii) The National Commission for Protection of Child Rights (NCPCR) has also \nissued advisory to all secretaries of school education departments of all  the \nStates to ensure safety of students in online classes. \n \n\n--3-- \nLS.S.Q.NO. 348 FOR 30.11.2021 \n(iv) PRAGYATA guidelines for school heads and teachers describe the need \nassessment, planning and steps to implement digital education while ensuring \ncyber safety and privacy measures. The guidelines can be accessed at the link \ngiven below: \n[https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/pragyata-guidelines_0.pdf ] \n[https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/pragyata-guidelines_0.pdf] \n(v) To enhance awareness about Cyber Crimes, an easy -to- understand \n'Handbook  for Adolescents/Students' booklet has been published by the \nMinistry of Home  Affairs and this booklet is available at \nwww.cybercrime.gov.in and https://mha.gov.in/documents/downloads.   \n(vi) A Twitter handle namely “@CyberDost” has been created to spread cyber \nawareness, on which messages are being posted regularly. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |349", "t": "PM-KISAN Beneficiaries", "head": "O.I.H\n \nGOVERNEMNT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPART\nMENT OF AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 349\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30\nth\n \nNOVEMBER ,2021\n \n \nPM\n-\nKISAN BENEFICIARIES \n \n \n349. SHRI MALOOK NAGAR:\n \n \n \nWill \nthe Minister of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare\n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \nthe percentage of farmers deprived of facilities like bank account in the \ncountry and the details thereof, \n \n \n(b)\n \nthe manner in which farmers are benefitte\nd from Pradhan Mantri Kisan \nSammanNidhiYojana (PM\n-\nKISAN);\n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government propose to increase the amount provided \nunder the Scheme keeping in view the interest of farmers, corona pandemic \nand current inflation condition;\n \n \n(d)\n \nif so, the details\n \nof the increased amount and the time by when it is \nproposed to be increased; and \n \n \n(e)\n \nthe number of farmers benefitted from this Scheme in Bijnour \nConstituency and the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n                  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a):\n \nAs per the information available with the Government, there are 144 \ncrore operative Current Account Savings Accounts (CASA) in the country out of \nwhich 43.90 crore are Jandhan accounts which \nalso includes the accounts \nopened by farmers. \n \nContd…./2\n \n\n-\n \n2 \n-\n \n(b):\n \nThe Pradhan Mantri Kisan SammanNidhi (PM\n-\nKISAN) is a Central \nSector Scheme, being implemented since 1\nst\n \nDecember, 2018.  The Scheme \naims at providing financial assistance to landholding fa\nrmer families across the \ncountry, subject to certain exclusion criteria, to enable them to take care of \nexpenses related to agriculture and allied activities as well as domestic needs.  \nUnder the Scheme, an amount of Rs. 6000/\n-\n \nper year is transferred in t\nhree 4\n-\nmonthly installments of Rs.2000/\n-\n  \neach directly into the bank accounts of the \nfarmers. \n \n \nUnder the PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme, the responsibility to identify the beneficiaries \nand uploading their correct and verified data on PM\n-\nKISAN portal lies with the \nresp\nective State/UT Government. Thereafter, these data go through various \nlevels of verification and validation including Aadhaar/PFMS/Income Tax \ndatabase. Consequent upon such verification and validation, the benefits of the \nSchemes are passed to eligible ben\neficiaries directly into their bank accounts \nthrough DBT. \n \n \n(c) & (d):\n \nAs of now, there is no proposal to increase the quantum of \nassistance given under PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme.\n \n(e):\n \nAs on 25.11.2021, a total of 3,78,920 beneficiaries have been given the \nfinancial\n \nbenefits of the Scheme in the district of Bijnour. \n \n******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |35", "t": "Competition Law Review Committee", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 35 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 29TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n[AGRAHAYANA 8, 1943 (SAKA)] \n \nCOMPETITION LAW REVIEW COMMITTEE  \n \nQUESTION \n \n35.  DR. MANOJ RAJORIA:      \n      \n Will the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the efforts being made by the Government for better competition laws for \npromoting better mechanism in corporate sector in view of the sharp growth in Indian \neconomy; \n \n(b)  the alternatives suggested in th is regard by the competition law review \ncommittee constituted under the chairmanship of the Secretary, Ministry of Corporate \nAffairs in October, 2018 in this regard; and \n \n(c)  the details of the points reviewed or being reviewed by the committee?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS \nAND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING; AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nCORPORATE AFFAIRS. \n \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n(a) to (c): The Government has taken necessary steps for carrying out \nAmendments to the Competition Act, 2002 as and when necessary.  The Competition \nCommission of India (‘Commission’) also has taken action to amend the Regulations \nissued by it from time to time as and when necessary to do so.  S ince the Commission \nbecame functional, there has been a significant growth in Indian markets. The \nCommission has been undertaking various initiatives from time to time to ensure \neffective competition and fair play in the market.  These, inter-alia, include conducting \nmarket studies on relevant sectors, undertaking competition assessment of Model \nConcession Agreements in the infrastructure and other public delivery sectors, \nadvocacy outreach initiatives including State  Resource Person Schemes and \nconducting roadshows on competition laws & practices, upgradation of IT \ninfrastructure and increased use of technology in functioning, opening of regional \noffices, introduction of green channel for certain combination notificat ions, \ncollaboration with other international anti-trust authorities etc.  \n \nThe Competition Law Review Committee (‘Committee’) in its report submitted in July \n2019 had recommended, inter -alia, introduction of ‘settlements & commitments’ \nframework, provision for compounding of certain offences, provision for a green \nchannel for certain combinations notifications, merging of DG office with the \nCommission for greater operational and administrative efficiency, enabling provisions \nto introduce necessary threshold s including  a deal value threshold for merger \nnotifications, etc.  The report of the Committee is available at www.mca.gov.in.  \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |350", "t": "Villages Covered under National Rural  Livelihood Mission", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 350 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nVILLAGES COVERED UNDER NATIONAL RURAL LIVELIHOOD MISSION \n \n350.  COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of villages covered by the National  Rural Livelihood Mission, State -wise \nalong with Rajasthan’s district and block distribution; \n(b)  the number of Self Help Groups (SHGs) registered in the national network; \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to introduce  technical and managerial training \nfor women to run businesses digitally; \n(d)  whether the Government has improved the market  linkages of SHGs over the past \nyear, alongwith the future action plans for the same; and \n(e)  whether the Government has any data to boost SHGs in rural economies across States, \nif so, the details thereof, State-wise and district-wise particularly Rajasthan? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) The number of villages covered by the Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana -National Rural \nLivelihood Mission (DAY -NRLM), State -wise is attached at ANNEXURE-I and a \nlist of districts covered under DAY -NRLM with the number of blocks and villages \ncovered in Rajasthan is attached at ANNEXURE-II. \n(b) As on 31 st October, 2021, 72,62,021 Self Help Groups (SHGs) have been promoted \nunder Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana -National Rural Livelihoods Mission (DAY -\nNRLM). \n(c) Start-up Village Entrepreneurship Programme (SVEP), the sub -scheme under the \nDeendayal Antyodaya Yojana -National Rural Livelihoods Mission (DAY -NRLM) \nsupports the members of Self -Help Groups (SHGs) and their family me mbers to set -\nup small enterprises in the non -farm sector. The entrepreneurs are provided various \ntypes of training for running their business including skill training in identified sector, \nmanagerial training etc. Specific training and other support are al so provided to SHGs \nfor on-boarding their products in e-commerce platforms including the Government-E-\nMarketplace. \n(d) DAY-NRLM has taken several initiatives to improve the market linkages of SHGs for \nselling their products which are listed as below: \ni. Saras Fairs at the National and State level for selling SHG products. \n \n\n \nii. Supporting SHGs to register on Government e -Market Place (GeM) and having \na dedicated  store  front called Saras Collection; 329 SHG producers have \nregistered 923 products on GeM. \niii. Memorandum of Understanding with Flipkart Internet Pvt. Ltd to support SHG \nproducts. \n(e) Ministry is implementing Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana – National Rural Livelihoods \nMission (DAY -NRLM) across the country since 2011 in a mission mode with the \nobjective of organizing the rural poor women into Self Help Groups (SHGs), and \ncontinuously nurturing and supporting them to take economic activities till they attain \nappreciable increase in income over a period of time to improve their quality of life \nand come out of abject pove rty. It has a provision for providing Community \nInvestment Support Fund (CISF) to the Self Help Groups (SHGs) in the form of \nRevolving Fund (RF) and Community Investment Fund (CIF) for supporting \nactivities of SHGs. The total amount of Capitalization Suppo rt i.e CIF and RF \nprovided to SHGs State -wise including Rajasthan is attached at ANNEXURE-III.  \nFurther, in order to reduce the effective cost of bank credit to women SHGs, DAY -\nNRLM provides Interest Subvention and additional Interest Subvention to SHGs \nfrom Financial Year 2013 -14.All women SHGs whose members are from the DAY -\nNRLM target group, are eligible for receiving Interest Subvention equal to the \ndifference between the borrowing rate of interest and 7%. The funding for this \nsubvention is being prov ided to the State Rural Livelihoods Missions (SRLMs) by \nthis Ministry. In addition, in respect of 250 backward districts, all women SHGs can \navail loans up to Rs.3.00 Lakh each at 7% rate of interest per annum, with an \nadditional interest subvention of 3% on prompt repayment, reducing the effective \ninterest rate to 4%.  \nSince 2013 -14 women SHGs have cumulatively leveraged a credit of Rs.4.35 Lakh \nCrores from Banks to take up income generating activities that contribute to growth \nof rural economy.  The total a mount disbursed to SHGs State -wise is provided in \nANNEXURE-IV and the total amount disbursed to SHGs in Rajasthan, district -wise \nis provided in  ANNEXURE-V. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n  \n ANNEXURE-I \n \nNumber of Villages covered State-wise under DAY-NRLM \n \nS. No. State/UTs No. of Villages Covered under \nDAY-NRLM \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 170 \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH 14908 \n3 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 1275 \n4 ASSAM 24775 \n5 BIHAR 34783 \n6 CHHATTISGARH 17444 \n7 GOA 282 \n8 GUJARAT 17470 \n9 HARYANA 5539 \n10 HIMACHAL PRADESH 10153 \n11 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 4829 \n12 JHARKHAND 25767 \n13 KARNATAKA"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |351", "t": "Promotion of Rural and Indigenous/Tribal Games", "head": "Page 1 of 3 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 351 \nANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nPromotion of Rural and Indigenous/Tribal Games \n \n351. SHRI JAYANT SINHA: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the games identified under the “promotion  of rural and indigenous/tribal \ngames” component of Khelo India; \n \n(b) the details and the number of annual national \ncompetitions/activities/demonstrations held for rural and indigenous/tribal \ngames since 2018 and the games for which competitions have been held; \n \n(c)  the  number  of  NGOs  and  sports  federations/ associations  to  whom  \nfinancial  support  has  been provided for promotion of these games since \n2018 and the amount given, State-wise; and \n \n(d) the number of top sports persons identified for these games, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) Mallakhamb, Kalaripayattu, Gatka, Thang -Ta, Yogasana and Silambam \nhave been identified for promotion by this Ministry  under the “Promotion of \nrural and indigenous/tribal games” component of the Khelo India Scheme. \n \n(b) Demonstration event s for Mallakhamb, Thang -Ta, Kalaripayattu and \nGatka were held during the 3rd edition of the Khelo India Youth Games, 2020 \nat Guw ahati, Assam. Further, thirty-six sports competitions/events were \norganised in 2019 -20 by this Ministry as a part of the  Ek Bharat S hreshtha \nBharat programme to promote indigenous and tribal games in various \ndisciplines, namely, Gatka, Roll Ball, Tug of War, Kalaripayattu, Thang -Ta, \nKho-Kho, Mallakhamb, Shooting Ball, Sqay, Kabaddi, etc. in different parts \nof the country. \n \n\nPage 2 of 3 \n \n(c) Four Nationa l Sports Federations (NSFs) have been provided financial \nassistance under  the “Promo tion o f rural and indigenous/tribal games” \ncomponent of the Khelo India Scheme  as per the d etails tabulated below. \nState-wise data is not maintained in this regard in this Ministry. \n \nS. No. Name of the Federation (NSF) Funds Released (in ₹ crore) \n2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n(up to \n30.09.2021) \n1 Mallakhamb Federation of India - 2.34 1.47 0.06 \n2 Indian Kalaripayyattu \nAssociation \n- 0.61 0.21 0.50 \n3 National Gatka Association - 0.27 0.23 0.18 \n4 Thang-Ta Federation of India - 0.48 0.30 0.15  \nTotal - 3.70 2.20 0.89 \n \n(d) A to tal of 283 athletes have been identified in the disciplines of \nMallakhamb, Thang -Ta, Kalaripayattu and Gatka  who are being provided \nOut-of-Pocket Allowance (OPA) under the “Promotion of rural and \nindigenous/tribal games” component of the Khelo India Scheme . State-wise \ndetails of the same are at Annexure. \n\nPage 3 of 3 \n \nANNEXURE RE FERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 351 ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 REGARDING \n“PROMOTION OF RURAL AND INDIGENOUS/TRIBAL G AMES” ASKED BY \nSHRI JAYANT SINHA, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nState-wise details of athletes being provided OPA under the “Promotion of \nrural and indigenous/tribal games” component of the Khelo India Scheme \n \nS. \nNo. \nState/UT No. of Athletes \nMallakhamb Kalaripayattu Thang-ta Gatka Total \n1 Andhra Pradesh 0 0 1 0 1 \n2 Assam 0 2 1 0 3 \n3 Chandigarh 0 0 0 7 7 \n4 Chhattisgarh 7 0 0 4 11 \n5 Delhi 0 3 8 4 15 \n6 Goa 1 0 0 0 1 \n7 Gujarat 2 0 1 0 3 \n8 Haryana 0 0 0 3 3 \n9 Jammu & Kashmir 0 1 0 0 1 \n10 Karnataka 2 7 0 0 9 \n11 Kerala 0 56 0 0 56 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 31 0 0 1 32 \n13 Maharashtra 34 1 5 2 42 \n14 Manipur 0 0 31 0 31 \n15 Puducherry 1 3 0 0 4 \n16 Punjab 0 0 0 29 29 \n17 Rajasthan 0 0 0 2 2 \n18 Tamil Nadu 24 5 0 0 29 \n19 Telangana 1 0 0 0 1 \n20 Uttar Pradesh 1 1 0 1 3 \nTotal 104 79 47 53 283 \n \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |352", "t": "Threat to National Security", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 352 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 9, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nTHREAT TO NATIONAL SECURITY \n \n352.   SHRI RITESH PANDEY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether there is any guideline for determining the threat to national security; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the basis and  criteria used to determine that a \nperson, institution, or a group of persons are a threat to national security; and \n \n(c) if not, the reasons, therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n(a) to  (c): Security threat assessment is a continuous process. Based on \ninputs received from various agencies, threat assessment is done and \nappropriate security measures are taken, including appropriate pre -emptive \nand preventive action against individual (s) and institutions. Several \nmeasures have been taken by the Government to strengthen the security \narchitecture, such as strengthening the legal framework by amending the \nUnlawful Activities (Prevention) Act 1967, the National Investigation Agency \nAct 2008, A rms Act 1959, Modernization of Police and the Central Police \nArmed Forces (CAPFs), strengthening of border security grid and coastal \nsecurity, strengthening of the Multi -Agency Centre (MAC) and exchange of \ninformation and intelligence in various bilateral fora.  \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |353", "t": "Agricultural Universities", "head": " \n1 \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 353 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nAGRICULTURAL UNIVERSITIES   \n \n \n353. SHRI BHAGWANT MANN:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has allocated funds to encourage education in \nagricultural universities located in Punjab to further boost the agriculture sector;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government is contemplating setting up a new agricultural university \nin Punjab to give a further boost to agricultural education; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h     (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a)  Yes, Sir. \n(b) Financial support of Rs. 110.30 crore has been provided by the Indian Council \nof Agricultural Research (ICAR) to the Punjab Agricultural University (PAU), Ludhiana \nand Guru Angad Dev Veterinary and Animal Sciences University (GADVASU), \nLudhiana, Punjab during the period from 2017 -18 till date  for strengthening and \ndevelopment of the Universities. The details are at Annexures I. \n(c)   There is no such proposal for setting up of a new agricultural university in \nPunjab by the Government. \n(d) In view of reply to part (c), the question does not arise. \n******* \n \n \n\n \n2 \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure-I \n[Part (b) of Lok Sabha USQ No.353 dated 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021] \n \nSupport provided for strengthening and development of the Agricultural \nUniversities located in Punjab under the scheme “Strengthening and Development of \nHigher Agricultural Education in India” is as under: \n(Rs. in crore) \nS. No. Name of \nthe \nUniversities \n2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22  \n(till date) \nNAHEP Total \n1 \nGADVASU, \nLudhiana \n \n5.94 5.95 7.28 3.69 1.10 25.00 48.96 \n2 \nPAU, \nLudhiana \n \n10.77 7.12 12.36 9.49 1.60 20.00 61.34 \nTotal 16.71 13.07 19.64 13.18 2.70 45.00 110.30 \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |355", "t": "Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan", "head": " \n \n O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 355 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nGARIB KALYAN ROJGAR ABHIYAAN \n \n355.  SHRI SATYADEV PACHAURI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has launched a  comprehensive employment-cum-rural pulic \nworks campaign named ‘Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan (GKRA)’  to empower and \nprovide livelihood opportunities in areas/villages witnessing large number of returnee \nmigrant workers affected by devastating COVID-19; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the number  of beneficiaries, mandays employment \ngenerated and the expenditure incurred under the said scheme till date, State-wise; \n(c)  whether the said scheme has been successful; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the steps  being taken to ensure effective and \ntimely implementation of the said scheme? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) to (d)       The Garib Kalyan Rojgar  Abhiyaan (GKRA) was launched on 20 th June 2020 \nfor a period of 125 days with resource envelop of Rs.50,000 Crore to boost employment and \nlivelihood opportunities for migrant workers returning to villages and similar ly affected \ncitizens in rural area. The Abhiyaan adopted a multipronged strategy of providing immediate \nemployment & livelihood opportunities to the distressed, to saturate the villages with public \ninfrastructure and creation of livelihood assets to boost the income generation activities and \nenhance long term livelihood opportunities by giving focus on 25 works in 116 selected \ndistricts across 6 States namely Bihar, Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh, Odisha, Rajasthan and \nUttar Pradesh. The Abhiyaan achieved employ ment generation of 50.78 Crore Persondays \nwith a total expenditure incurred of Rs. 39,293 Crore. State -wise detail of employment \ngenerated & expenditure incurred is given at Annexure. \n  \nA Committee of Secretaries (CoS) under the Chairpersonship of Cabinet Secretary was set up \nfor review of the Abhiyaan. There was a Central Coordinator for the Abhiyaan to coordinate \nwith the participating Departments/Ministries of the Central Government and with the Nodal \nOfficers of the State Governments. A district wise de dicated Central Nodal Officer (CNO) \nwas responsible for monitoring and facilitation of ground level implementation. At State \nLevel, each State under the Abhiyaan had appointed the Additional Chief Secretary/Principal \nSecretary/Secretary, In -charge of State  Rural Development Department as the State Nodal \nOfficer (SNO) for the Abhiyaan, who was responsible for implementation, monitoring and \noverall outcome of the Abhiyaan within the State. At district Level, a committee of all \nstakeholders was formed under th e Chairpersonship of District Collector/ District Magistrate \n/Deputy Commissioner of the Abhiyaan District for the implementation and monitoring. \n*****  \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.355 \ndue for answer on 30.11.2021 \n  \n  \nState-wise employment generated (in person-days) and total Expenditure \nS. No. State Employment Provided \n(In Persondays) \nTotal Expenditure \n(Rs. In Crore) \n1 Bihar 11,19,48,805 10,992 \n2 Jharkhand 1,32,51,929 1,396 \n3 Madhya Pradesh 9,99,01,931 6,819 \n4 Odisha 2,30,31,641 2,042 \n5 Rajasthan 15,39,17,007 8,715 \n6 Uttar Pradesh 10,58,17,358 9,330 \n  Total 50,78,68,671 39,293 \n  \n  \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |356", "t": "Payment of Insurance Claims under PMFBY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n356\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER, 2021\n \n \nPAYMENT OF INSURANCE CLAIMS UNDER PMFBY\n \n \n356\n. \nSHRI \nRAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI  :\n \n        \nSHRI ACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA :\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n  \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether insurance claims are being paid to farmers on time after crop damage/failure \nu\nnder the Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY), if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether in many States, including Odisha, the payment of insurance claims for crop \nfailure in 2018\n-\n19, 2019\n-\n20 and 2020\n-\n21 is still pending, if so the details thereof; \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to take steps for payment of all these outstanding \nclaims; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the time by which the outstanding claims will be fully paid \nand if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(e) the\n \nsteps being taken by the Government to ensure that farmers are being \ncompensated properly especially during crop loss in cases of drought or deficient \nrainfall?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n(SHRI \nNARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (e) : \nThe admissible claims under the \nPradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana \n(PMFBY)\n \nare generally paid by the \nconcerned \ninsurance companies within two months \nof completion of Crop Cutting Experiments (CCEs)/harvesting period and one month of \nnotification for invoking the risks/perils of prevented sowing, mid\n-\nseason adversity and \npost harvest losses subject to receipt of tot\nal share of premium subsidy from concerned \nGovernment within time. However, settlement of \nfew \nclaims in some States \nincluding \nOdisha \ng\not\n \ndelayed due to reasons like delayed transmission of yield data; late release \nof their share in premium subsidy, yield r\nelated disputes between insurance companies \nand States, non\n-\nreceipt of account details of some farmers for transfer of claims to the \nbank account of eligible farmers and National Electronic Fund Transfer (NEFT) related \nissues, erroneous/incomplete entry of\n \nindividual farmers data on National Crop \nInsurance Portal (NCIP), delay in remittance of farmers share of premium/non\n-\nremittance of farmers share of premium to concerned insurance company etc.   \n \n \n \n\n \nThis Department is regularly monitoring the implementati\non of PMFBY including \ntimely settlement of claims through weekly video conference of stakeholders, one to one \nmeetings with insurance companies/States etc.  Further, penalty provisions for late \nsettlement of claims by insurance companies and late release o\nf funds by State \nGovernments have also been stipulated under the revised/revamped operational \nguidelines of the scheme.  Insurance companies have to pay penal interest @12% per \nannum to the farmers for the period beyond the stipulated period in the PMFBY \nG\nuidelines from the date of receipt of final yield data from the State Government and \ncompletion of crop damage survey.\n \n \nFurther, this Department is regularly reviewing the schemes in consultation with \nState Governments and other stakeholders to streamline \nthe process for early \nsettlement of claims and to provide timely and adequate benefits to the eligible farmers \nunder the scheme.\n \n \nState\n-\nwise details of pending claims including Odisha, for the years 2018\n-\n19, \n2019\n-\n20 and 2020\n-\n21 are \nannexed\n. \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \nState\n-\nWise Status of  Pending Claims under PMFBY for 2018\n-\n19, 2019\n-\n20 and 2020\n-\n21 as on \n25.11.2021\n \n(Rs. in crore)\n \nState/UT Name\n \nStates/UTs\n \nReported \nClaims\n \nPaid \nClaims\n \nClaims \nPending\n \nReason for \nPendency\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \nA & N Islands\n \n0.09\n \n0.01\n \n0.08\n \nPayment \nFailure\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n1,890.05\n \n1,885.76\n \n4.29\n \nPayment Failure\n \nGujarat\n \n2,778.08\n \n2,777.89\n \n0.18\n \nState Subsidy \nPending\n \nHaryana\n \n948.01\n \n947.75\n \n0.27\n \nPayment Failure\n \nJharkhand\n \n684.94\n \n50.98\n \n633.95\n \nState Subsidy \nPending\n \nKarnataka\n \n2,948.56\n \n2,947.99\n \n0.57\n \nPayment Failure\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n3,777.31\n \n3,777.14\n \n0.17\n \nPayment Failure\n \nMaharashtra\n \n6,149.46\n \n6,146.19\n \n3.27\n \nPayment Failure\n \nTelangana\n \n587.35\n \n148.92\n \n438.43\n \nState Subsidy \nPending\n \nWest Bengal\n \n535.52\n \n529.85\n \n5.67\n \nPayment \nFailure; State \nSubsidy \nPending\n \n2018\n-\n19 Total\n \n \n29,341.04\n \n28,254.15\n \n1,086.89\n \n \n2019\n-\n20\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n1,259.01\n \n1,254.03\n \n4.98\n \nPayment Failure\n \nAssam\n \n21.27\n \n-\n \n21.27\n \nState Subsidy \nPending\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n1,314.60\n \n1,296.59\n \n18.02\n \nPayment Failure\n \nGujarat\n \n369.40\n \n111.67\n \n257.73\n \nState Subsidy \nPending\n \nHaryana\n \n933.08\n \n928.36\n \n4.72\n \nPayment Failure\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n67.54\n \n66.61\n \n0.93\n \nPayment Failure\n \nJharkhand\n \n25.46\n \n-\n \n25.46\n \nState Subsidy \nPending\n \nKarnataka\n \n1,572.28\n \n1,349.26\n \n223.02\n \nP"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |357", "t": "Non-payment of Crop Insurance Compensation", "head": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n357\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER, 2021\n \n \nNON\n-\nPAYMENT OF CROP INSURANCE COMPENSATION\n \n \n357. SHRI \nPRATAPRAO PATIL CHIKHLIKAR:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n  \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether\n \nthe Government is aware that the installment of crop insurance \ncompensation for the year 2020\n-\n21 has not been paid to the farmers in Nanded till date; \n \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the officials working in the concerned department are not\n \nserious in this \nregard; and \n \n(d) the time by which the insured compensation amount is likely to be paid to the \nfarmers?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (d) :   As reported by the State Government of Maharashtra total claims of Rs. \n223.35 crore  in Maharashtra including Rs. 7.40 crore for Nanded district for Kharif 2020 \nare pending for settlement by concerned insurance companies under Pradhan Mantri\n \nFasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY). Similarly claims of Rs. 48.36 crore including Rs. 17.98 \ncrore are pending for settlement for Rabi 2020\n-\n21 season.  These claims are pending \nas the State Government has not released its share of premium subsidy of Rs. 942.62 \ncror\ne for Kharif 2020 and Rs. 293.59 crore for Rabi 2020\n-\n21. \n \n \nThe PMFBY is a multi\n-\nagency scheme involving farmers, insurance companies, \nfinancial institutions and Governments both Centre and State, therefore \nthis \nDepartment \nis regularly monitoring the implem\nentation of PMFBY including timely settlement of \nclaims through weekly video conference of stakeholders, one to one meetings with \ninsurance companies/States etc.  Further, penalty provisions for late settlement of \nclaims by insurance companies and late rel\nease of funds by State Governments have \nalso been stipulated under the revised/revamped operational guidelines of the scheme.  \nInsurance companies have to pay penal interest @12% per annum to the farmers for \nthe period beyond the stipulated period in the P\nMFBY Guidelines from the date of \nreceipt of final yield data from the State Government and completion of crop damage \nsurvey.\n  \n \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |358", "t": "Deen Dayal Upadhyay Grameen Kaushal Yojana", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 358 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nDEEN DAYAL UPADHYAY GRAMEEN KAUSHAL YOJANA \n \n358.  SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA: \nSHRI JAYANT SINHA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details and salient features of Deen Dayal  Upadhyay Grameen Kaushal Yojana \n(DDU-GKY) to  promote rural employment through skill development  including the \ndetails of the budgetary allocati on made during each of the last three years and the \ncurrent year along with the progress of the scheme, State/UT-wise; \n(b)  the number of training centres in Jharkhand and  the number of people trained, \nassessed, certified and placed through these centres, district-wise; \n(c)  whether the Government proposes to lift the cap  of 25 per cent on the permissible \nlimit of expenditure  under the DDU -GKY, if so, the details thereof and the  reasons \ntherefor; \n(d)  the number of rural youth benefitted through  this scheme  since its inception, \nState/UT-wise; \n(e)  whether all the villages have been covered in  this scheme, if so, the details thereof  \nincluding the reasons therefor, State/UT-wise; and \n(f)  whether any plan has been devised for the  revival of Rural Skill Develo pment \nProgramme by including more rural youth under the scheme to give  fillip to Make in \nIndia programme? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) (i)    Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU-GKY) is a placement \nlinked skill development program for rural poor youth under NRLM. It has its origin in the \nwage employment linked “Special Projects” for skilling comp onent of the Swarnajayanti \nGram Swarojgar Yojana (SGSY), which was subsequently renamed as Aajeevika Skills when \nSGSY was converted as NRLM. Taking into account the focus on improving quality and \nemployment outcomes in the National Policy on Skill Development, and need to increase the \ncoverage of Skill Development programme in remote r ural areas, the scheme has been \nannounced as DDU -GKY on 25 th September 2014. The skilling program for rural youth has \nnow been refocused and re-priotorized to build the capacity of rural poor youth to address the \nneeds of domestic and global skill requirements. \n ii.      Salient features of DDU-GKY are as follows: \n Market-led, placement-linked training programme for rural youth undertaken in a PPP \nmode. \n\n \n Mandatory assured placement to 70% of the trained candidates. \n Focus on rural youth from poor families in the age group of 15 to 35 years. \n Social inclusion of candidates through mandatory coverage of socially  disadvantaged \ngroups, i.e for SC/ST-50%, Minorities- 15%, and Women 33%) \n Minimum Salary of Rs.6000 per month (after a three month training course). \n Post-placement support to candidates. \n Career progression support to training partners. \n In allocation of skills projects, primacy given to Training Partners who can  train and \nsupport overseas placement and captive placements. \n  \nDetails of the release made during each of the last three years and the current year along with \nthe progress of the scheme, State/UT-wise is given at Annexure-I (Table A&B). \n  \n(b). There are 37 operational Training Centres in Jharkhand, district –wise numb er of   \ntraining centres is given at Annexure-II.  \n  \n(c) DDUGKY is a demand driven programme within the overall allocation of National   Rural \nLivelihood Mission. Since 2015 there is no cap of 25% on the permissible limit of \nexpenditure under DDUGKY. \n \n(d) The number of rural youth benefitted through DDUGKY since its inception, State -wise is \ngiven at Annexure-III \n \n(e) DDUGKY programme covers rural poor youth in the age group of 15 -35 years with focus \non rural youth from poor families belonging to: a) MGNREGA w orker household if any \nperson from the household has completed 15 days of work, b) RSBY household, c) \nAntyodaya Anna Yojana card household, d) BPL PDS card households, e) NRLM -SHG \nhousehold, f) Participatory process of Identification of poor, g) Households  covered under \nauto inclusion parameters of SECC 2011. \nDetails : NA \n  \n(f) Skill training for the rural youth is being driven through the approved action plans under \nDDUGKY & RSETI. The target assigned in the Action Plan details are as under: \n  \nAction Plan Target \nSl. No Scheme Action Plan Target \n1 DDUGKY (2019-23) 1845977 \n2 RSETI (2021-22) 390265 \n  \n  \n   \n****** \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\n \nAnnexure-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF  PART (A) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO 358 IN LOK SABHA ON 30.11.2021. \n  \nTable (A) 3 yrs and current yr Funds release status of DDU -GKY to State Govt.  (as on \n31.10.2021) \n                                                                                                                        (Rs. in lakhs) \nS.N. States 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n1 A&N Islands    3.00 798.00"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |359", "t": "Youth Volunteerism in Karnataka", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.359 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nYouth Volunteerism in Karnataka \n \n359. SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any measures to improve youth \nvolunteerism in Karnataka and engage youth in community and skill \ndevelopment projects, if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government aims to expand the reach of National \nCadet Corps (NCC) as well as Bharat Scouts and Guides programme, \nand mobilise their efforts in assisting and monitoring ground -level \nissues of stubble burning, cleanliness and more, if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n \n(c) whether the Government has taken any measur es to increase the \neffectiveness of the NCC in the current semester -based education \nsystem with mandatory attendance which makes NCC participation \nless plausible, and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports through its organisations \ni.e. Nehru Yuva Kendra Sangathan (NYKS) and National Service \nScheme (NSS) has taken measures to improve youth volunteerism \nacross the country including the state o f Karnataka and engage youth \nin community and skill development projects through various \nprogrammes viz. ‘Aatm a Nirbhar YuWaah Bharat - Skilling and \nHandholding’, ‘Training of Youth on Clean Village -Green Village’, \n\n‘Combating COVID -19: Mass Awakening and Ac tion Campaign’, \n‘Training of Youth on Jal Jagran Abhiyaan, ‘District Youth Convention’, \netc. The above programmes have made significant contribution \ntowards mass literacy, health awareness, environment conservation, \nwomen empowerment, relief and rehabilita tion during natural \ncalamities, etc. \n(b) The NCC cadets carry out various social service and community \ndevelopment activities in order to  contribute towards nation building \nand national developmental efforts in various sectors. The Ministry of \nYouth Affairs and Sports provides financial assistance in the form of \ngrant-in-aid to the Bharat Scouts & Guides for various programmes \nsuch as organization of training camps, skill development \nprogrammes, holding of jamborees, etc.  The activities also include \nprogrammes related to adult literacy, environment conservation, \ncommunity service, health awareness and promotion of hygiene and \nsanitation. \n(c) The NCC training curriculum has been aligned with Choice Based \nCredit System (CBCS) model as per the National E ducation Policy, \n2020 to increase the popularity of NCC amongst youth of the country. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |36", "t": "Assessment of Impact of Air Pollution on Tourism", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *36 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021/ AGRAHAYANA 9, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nNCRB REPORT ON SUICIDES \n \n*36.      SHRI BALUBHAU ALIAS SURESH NARAYAN DHANORKAR: \n    SHRI K. NAVASKANI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware about recent  NCRB Report, 11,716 \nbusinesspersons died by suicide in 2020, registering a 29% jump from 2019; \n \n(b) whether it is true that the majority of businesspersons who committed \nsuicide belonged to the micro small and medium enterprises (MSMEs); and \n \n(c) if so, the steps taken by the Government to check this trend? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n-2- \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN THE REPLY TO THE STARRED QUESTION NO. *36 \nFOR REPLY ON 30 NOVEMBER 2021 \n(a): The National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) compiles and publishes \nstatistical data on accidents and suicides in its publication “Accidents and \nSuicides in India”. As per the published reports, a total of 9052 business  persons \ncommitted suicide in the year 2019 and a total of 11,716 business  persons \ncommitted suicides in the year 2020. \n(b):  Such an inference may not be possible since the NCRB report does not \ncategorize the suicide data separately for business  persons from micro small \nand medium enterprises (MSMEs).  \n(c)   Does not arise in view of reply to part (b). \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |360", "t": "Promotion of Fisheries and Animal Husbandry", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 360 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \nPromotion of Fisheries and Animal Husbandry \n \n360. SHRI GOPAL JEE THAKUR: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING मत्स्यपालन, \nपशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a)    the details of the programmes/schemes currently being implemented by the department for \nthe promotion of fisheries and animal husbandry and for its proper development;  \n \n(b)    the details of the schemes currently being implemented in Darbhanga district of  the State of \nBihar, scheme-wise;  \n \n(c)   whether the Government proposes to set up a training institute in Darbhanga for proper \ntraining in fisheries and animal husbandry; and  \n \n(d)     if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING \n(PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) to ( d):To complement and supplement the efforts of the States/UTs for promotion of fisheries, \nanimal husbandry and dairy sectors, the Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying, \nGovernment of India is im plementing various schemes, across the country including Darbhanga \ndistrict of Bihar, namely (i) Pradhan  Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY), (ii) Fisheries and \nAquaculture Infrastructure Development Fund (FIDF), (iii) Rashtriya  Gokul Mission (RGM), (iv) \nNational Livestock Mission (NLM), (v) National Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD), (vi) \nDairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund (DIDF), (vii) Supporting Dairy Cooperative \nand Farmer Producer Organizations (FPO) en gaged in dairy activities, (viii) Livestock Census and \nintegrated Sample Survey, and (ix) Livestock Health and Disease Control (LHDC), etc. People can \ntake benefit out of these schemes through the State /UT Governments. The Government under the \nPradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) and Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure \nDevelopment Funds (FIDF) is providing support for creation of fisheries infrastructure across the \ncountry including Bihar. To address the infrastructure requirements of the fishe ries sector, \nconcessional finance is provided under FIDF to the Eligible Entities (EEs) including State \nGovernments/Union Territories, cooperatives, individuals and entrepreneurs amongst the others for \ndevelopment of identified fisheries infrastructure. In addition, under the Animal Husbandry \nInfrastructure Development Fund (AHIDF) the government incentivizes infrastructure investments in \ndairy, meat processing and animal feed plants. Department of Fisheries has approved a consolidated \nproposal of Bihar for  development of fisheries in the State including Darbhanga district under the \nCSS component of Pradhan  Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) at a total project outlay of \nRs.273.49 Crores involving central share of Rs. 86.23 crore . Out of Central Share an amount of \nRs.41.84 crore has also been released to the State Government for taking up the project s. As of now, \nthere is no proposal to set up training institute in Darbhanga for proper training in fisheries and \nanimal husbandry.  \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |361", "t": "Digital India Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 361 \nANSWERED ON-30.11.2021 \n \nDIGITAL INDIA PROGRAMME \n  \n†361. SHRI VIJAY BAGHEL: \n  \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the scheme to connect village Panchayats with broadband/internet is being \nimplemented under Digital India Programme;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) the number of village Panchayats connected with broadband/internet including those \nlocated in the State of Chhattisgarh as on date;  \n \n(d) the parameters/criteria being adhered to connect village Panchayats with \nbroadband/internet; and  \n \n(e) the time by which the work of connecting village Panchayats with broadband/internet is \nlikely to be completed? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ  \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a) to (c) Under the Digital India Programme, BharatNet project is being implemented in a \nphased manner to provide last mile broadband connectivity to Gram Panchayats (GPs) in the \ncountry through Wi-Fi or any other suitable broadband technology including Fiber to the Home \n(FTTH). As on 01.11.2021, a total number of 1,66,088 Gram Panchayats have been made Service \nReady in the country, including 8,386 Gram Panchayats in Chhattisgarh.  \n \n(d) All the Gram Panchayats are to be provided broadband connectivity under the BharatNet \nproject. Further, as part of this project, the last mile connectivity is to be provided through Wi-Fi \nor any other suitable broadband technology in all GPs to access broadband/internet services.  The \nscope of BharatNet has been recently extended upto all inhabited villages beyond Gram \nPanchayats.  \n \n(e)   The targeted date for completion of the project is August, 2023. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |362", "t": "Management and Regulation of Cooperative Societies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION No. \n362\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3\n0\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER,\n \n2021\n \n \nMANAGEMENT AND REGULATION OF COOPERATIVE SOCIETIES\n \n \n3\n62\n. \n \nSHRI KHAGEN MURMU\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of COOPERATION (\nसहका\u0000रता\n \nमं\u0000ी\n)\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government is planning to revisit the management and regulation of \ncooperative societies\n;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; and\n \n(c) \n \nif not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of COOPERATION\n \nसहका\u0000रता\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n(SHRI AMIT SHAH)\n \n \n(a) \nto (\nc\n): \nManagement and regulation of cooperative societies is an ongoing exercise \nand initiatives in this direction are taken as per the Government of India (Allocation of \nBusiness) Rules, 1961\n,\n \nas \nnotified in Gazette Notification No\n. 2516 dated 06.07.2021 \nand \nas \nshown in Annexure \n–\n \nI.\n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n–\n \nI\n \n \nGovernment of India (Allocation of Business) Rules, 1961\n \nMinistry Of Cooperation (Sahkarita Mantralaya)\n \n \n1\n.\n \nGeneral Policy in the field of Co\n-\noperation and Co\n-\nordination of \nCo\n-\noperation activities in all sectors\n \nNote:\n \n-\n \nThe Ministries concerned are respon\nsible for Co\n-\noperatives in the \nfields.\n \n2\n.\n \nRealization of vision” from cooperation to prosperity”\n \n3\n.\n \nStrengthening of cooperative movement in the country and deepening its \nreach up \nto the grassroots.\n \n4\n.\n \nPromotion of cooperative\n-\nbased economic development model, including \nthe spirit of responsibility among its members to develop the country.\n \n5\n.\n \nCreation of appropriate policy, legal and institutional framework to help \ncooperatives realize \ntheir potential.\n \n6\n.\n \nMatters relating to National Co\n-\noperative organization\n \n7\n.\n \nNational Co\n-\noperative Development Corporation\n \n8\n.\n \nIncorporation, regulation and winding up of Co\n-\noperative societies with \nobjects not confined to one State including administration of ‘the\n \nMulti\n-\nState Co\n-\noperative Societies Act, 2002 (39 of 2002)’:\n \nProvided that the administrative Ministry or Department shall be ‘the \nCentral Government’ for the purpose of exercising powers under the Multi\n-\nState Co\n-\noperative Societies Act, 2002(39 of 2002), \nfor Co\n-\noperative units \nfunctioning under its control.\n \n9.  \n \nTraining of personnel of co\n-\noperative departments and co\n-\noperative   \ninstitutions (including education of members, office bearers and non\n-\nofficials).        \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |363", "t": "Assessment of KVKs", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 363 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nASSESSMENT OF KVKs \n \n \n 363.  SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has any mechanism to assess the quality of services \noffered by Krishi Vigyan Kendra (KVKs);  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government provides financial assistance to the KVKs; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h                (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b):   The quality of services offered by Krishi Vigyan Kendra (KVKs) is assessed \nregularly by holding meetings of Scientific Advisory Committee of KVKs and also State \nand Zonal Workshops. Besides, there is also a mechanism for assessing KVKs’ work \nby the Quinquennial Review Teams and the Third Party evaluation. \n(c) & (d): The Government provides 100% financial assistance for  functioning                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     \nof KVKs. During the last three years i.e. 2018"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |364", "t": "KVKs in Telangana", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 364 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \nKVKs IN TELANGANA \n \n364. SHRI PASUNOORI DAYAKAR:  \n        SHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA:  \n         SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU:  \n        DR. G. RANJITH REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry is aware that there are only 16 Krishi Vigyan Kendras in Telangana \nwhen there are 31 districts in the State;  \n \n(b) if so, whether the Ministry will consider opening more KVKs in districts where there are \nno KVKs in Telangana;  \n \n(c) whether any impact assessment of KVKs has been done and, if so, the findings of such \nassessment;  \n \n(d) whether each KVK has to cater to 90-100 villages which the KVK is finding difficult;  \n \n(e) if so, whether the Ministry will consider establishing additional KVKs in the country; and  \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h            (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) & (b) Out of 31 districts in Telangana State, Krishi Vigyan Kendras  (KVKs) have \nbeen established in 16 districts and presently there is no provision for opening more, KVKs \nfor Telangana State. \n(c) A Third Party evaluation of Krishi  Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) has been carried out by \nNational Institute of Labour Economics Research and D evelopment (NILERD), an \nautonomous institute under NITI Aayog, in 2015. The major observations and findings of this \nevaluation are as follows: -  \n \ni. It was observed that KVKs are playing proactive role in transferring new technology \nat field level with beneficial impacts.  \nii. The KVKs have an edge in technology transfer over other service providers by virtue \nof having better technical expertise and demonstration abilities.  \niii. About 40 percent farmers reported that they implemented the technology immediately \nafter its dissemination by KVK and that 25 percent did so from the next agricultural \nseason.  \niv. On an average a KVK covers 43 villages and 4300 farmers per year. 80% of villages \ncovered are 10 km away from KVK. \n\n2 \n \nv. 96% farmers’ requests were attended by KVKs. \nvi. 42% technologies adopted by farmers resulted in higher productivity, 33% resulted in \nhigh harvest income and 20% resulted in drudgery reduction. \nvii. About 25% of persons trained started self-employment ventures. \nviii. With the intervention by KVKs, about 80 percent of t he farmers have modified their \nagricultural patterns which were related to diversification of crops and changes in \ncropping pattern, seed planting technique, use of fertilizers and pesticides, changes \nin machinery used and in water use pattern. \n \nFurther, N ILERD conducted categorization study of the KVKs on the basis of \nmandated activities, impact of the activities, allied activities, accolades and other \nparameters in 2018. The study categorized the KVKs as A, B, C and D. Best performing \nKVKs (43%) were rated as ‘A’ followed by 48% KVKs as ‘B’ and 9% as ‘C’ & ‘D’. \n \nBesides, International Food Policy Research Institute (IFPRI) evaluated the KVKs in \n2019. Major findings of the Study are as follows: \n \ni. KVKs’ efforts generated an additional net farm income of Rs. 3568 per hectare. \nii. Cost Benefit ratio is 1:7.8. Thus, the rate of return on expenditure on KVK is very high.  \niii. One farmer trained by a KVK disseminates technology/knowledge to 30 fellow \nfarmers. \n \nRecently in 2020, Indian Society of Agribusiness Professionals,  New Delhi also \nconducted study on impact evaluation of KVKs. Major findings of the study are as follows: \n \ni. On an average, KVK outreach found to be around 90 -100 villages. With its strong \nICT interventions, the outreach to villages increases to even 200 villages per KVK. \nii. There is increase in outreach programs by KVKs from 2012-13 to 2019-20 in number \nof on farm trials by 51%, frontlines demonstrations by 61%, farmers trained by 16% \nand extension personnel trained by 35%. \niii. There is increase in proportion of  farm women in trainings from 30% in 2012 -13 to \n37% in 2019-20. \niv. There is 142% increase in the number of short messages sent from 2012-13 to 2019-\n20. Similarly, there is increase in the number of the farmers covered from the year \n2012-13 to 2019 -20 by 135% with the advent of the digital technologies such as \nWhatsApp group, Facebook group which has increased the outreach by 4 times. \nv. There is 32% increase in seed production and 117% increase in planting material \nproduction by KVKs during the said period. \n(d) KVKs are mandated for frontline extension which act as a bridge between research \norganizations and the main extension system operated by different development \ndepartments of the State Governments. Considering the role and resourc"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |365", "t": "Welfare of Fishermen", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 365 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \nWelfare of Fishermen \n365.  SHRI RAVIKUMAR   D. :  \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  मत्स्यपाऱन, \nपशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has any proposal to give cash dole of Rs. 10,000/ - Rs. to fishermen during \nthe time of fishing ban, if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)  whether the Government has any plan to hike the diesel / kerosene subsidy to fishermen, if so, the \ndetails thereof;  \n(c)  the details of measures taken so far to give good level of higher education to the children of \nfishermen; and  \n(d)  the details of schemes being implemented/likely to be implemented for eradication of middleman in \nfish trade? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a):  The Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY)  a flagship scheme implemented by the \nDepartment of Fisheries, M inistry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying inter-alia provides \nlivelihood and nutritional support for socio-economically backward active traditional fisher’s families during \nthe fishing ban/lean period. Under the PMMSY Governmental financial assistance of @Rs. 3000/ - per \nannum is provided to each enrolled beneficiary and the beneficiary contributes Rs. 1500 /- to this annually .  \nSuch accumulated amount of Rs. 4500/- is disbursed to each enrolled beneficiary by the respective State/UT \nat the rate of Rs. 1500/- per month during the fishing ban/lean period for three months annually.  \n(b): The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying has no such \nplan. \n(c): PMMSY inter-alia lays special focus on training, skill development, skill up-gradation and capacity \nbuilding through trainings, awareness building programmes and exposure visits to stakeholders especially  \nfishers, fish farmers, fish workers, fish vendors, entrepreneurs, officials, fisheries Cooperatives and members \nof FFPOs etc.  T he policies and scheme s/programmes implemented by the Department also accord \nparamount importance for improvement of socio -economic status of the fishers, which pave towards the \nbetter living standard and good education to children of fishermen family as well. \n(d): Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada  Yojana (PMMSY) also provides for setting up of and \noperationalisation of Fish Farmers Producer Organizations (FFPOs) to economically empower the fishers \nand fish farmers, enhance their bargaining power and to enable to establish direct marketing linkages aiming \ntowards eradication of middleman in fish trade. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |366", "t": "Production of Onions", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nLOKSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 3\n66\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n3\n0\nTH\nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nPRODUCTION OF \nONION\nS\n \n \n366\n.\n \nSHRI\n \nSHANMUGA SUNDARAM K.:\n \nSHRIVELUSAMY \nP.:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nऔर\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to \nstate:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe total quantity of onions produced in the country during the last three years and the \ncurrent year;\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether the Government has any proposal to \nban export of onions permanently since \nthe deman\nd is higher than supply in the country\n, if so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the amendment to the Essential Commodities Act, 1955 by imposing stock \nlimit\n \nwill affect onion farmers; and\n \n \n(\nd\n)\n \nif\n \nso, the steps being taken by the Government to augment the income of the farmers \nwho can stock their produce to get better price in future?\n \nANSWER\n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\nऔर\n\u0000कसानक\u0000याणमं\u0000ी\n               \n( SHRI NARENDRA SINGH \nTOMAR)\n \n(a)The quantity of Onions produced in the country, during 2017\n-\n18, 2018\n-\n19, 2019\n-\n20, \nand 2020\n-\n21(Third Advance Estimates) is as under: \n-\n \nOnion Production in the country\n \n(\nin Lakh Tonne\n)\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n(3\nrd\nAdv\nance \n \nEst\nimates\n)\n \n232.62\n \n228.19\n \n260.91\n \n268.30\n \n \n(\nb)\n \nAccording to Department of Consumer Affairs (DoCA) and Department of \nCommerce, there has been no proposal for permanent ban onexport of Onions.\n \n \n(c)&\n \n(d)    The decision to repeal the Essential Commodities (Amendment) Act, 2020 has \nrecently been approved by the Cabinet. \n \n \nUnder the Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH), a Centrally \nSponsored scheme being implemented in all States an\nd Union Territories for the holistic \ndevelopment of horticulture,\nassistance is also envisaged @ 50% of total cost of Rs. 1.75 \nLakh per unit for low cost Onion storage structure having a capacity of 25 Tonne each. \n \n\n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |368", "t": "Impact of COVID-19 on Rural Development", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 368 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nIMPACT OF COVID-19 ON RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n \n368.  SHRIMATI  RITA BAHUGUNA JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has assessed the impact of COVID -19 on the rural \ndevelopment sector in general, and on the income of rural people, in particular; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n(c)  the efforts undertaken by the Government for providing livelihood opportunities to \nthe rural people during COVID -19 ? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n(a) to (c): Measures were taken by the Ministry of Rural Deve lopment for providing relief \nand boosting employment and livelihood opportunities in rural areas during the Corona Virus \npandemic. However, the Ministry has not undertaken any survey on the impact of COVID -19 \non rural development sector. \nMinistry of Financ e, Government of India, on 26th March 2020, announced relief package \nunder Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Yojana (PMGKY) for the poor to help them fight the \nbattle against Corona Virus. The relief package announced includes, inter-alia  \ni. One-time ex-gratia payment of Rs 500 per month for 3 months (April, May and June \n2020) to women Jan Dhan Account holders. In pursuance of this, Ministry of Rural \nDevelopment (MoRD) implemented the scheme of relief package of one -time ex -\ngratia payment of R s 500 per month for 3 months (April, May and June 2020) to \nwomen Jan Dhan Account holders through various Banks. A total of Rs. 30944.44 \ncrores has been released towards direct benefit transfers to 20.64 crore Women \nbeneficiaries of Jan Dhan account holder s under Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana \n(PMJDY) @ Rs. 500/- each for three months.  \nii. Ex-gratia of Rs.1000 in two installments (Rs.500/ - each) to the existing old age, \nwidow and disabled/Divyangjan beneficiaries of the schemes of National Social \nAssistance Programme(NSAP).  A total of Rs. 2814.50 crore as ex-gratia was released \nin two tranches in April and May, 2020 to States/UTs under PMGKY for the existing \n282 lakh beneficiaries of NSAP schemes. \niii. Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (Mahatma  Gandhi \nNREGA) wage rate revised w.e.f. 1.4.2020 with average wage rate increase of Rs.20 \nover the wage rate of 2019-20. \n  \n\nApart from this, Government of India launched the Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan (GKRA) \non 20th June, 2020 for a period of 125 days to boost employment and livelihood opportunities \nfor returnee migrant workers and similarly affected citizens in rural area, in the wake of \nCOVID-19 pandemic. The objectives of the Abhiyaan were to provide immediate \nemployment & livelihood opportunities to th e distressed, to saturate the villages with public \ninfrastructure and creation of livelihood assets to boost the income generation activities and \nenhance long term livelihood opportunities by giving focus on 25 works in 116 selected \ndistricts across 6 Stat es of Bihar, Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh, Odisha, Rajasthan and Uttar \nPradesh. The Abhiyaan is a convergent effort of ongoing 25 schemes of 12 Ministries/ \nDepartment of Central Government.  \nFour schemes of the MoRD namely, Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Emplo yment \nGuarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS), Pradhan MantriAwaasYojana - Gramin \n(PMAY-G), Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) and Shyama Prasad Mukherjee \nRURBAN Mission (SPMRM) have been identified under the GKRA. The achievement in \nthis regard is as under: \ni. Under GKRA, a total of 50.78 Crore Persondays employment has been generated \nduring the Abhiyaan with a total expenditure of Rs. 392923 crore. \nii. PMAY-G has sanctioned a total of 770522 houses and completed 481210 houses with \na total expenditure of Rs.5618.19 crore. \niii. Under PMGSY, as against the target of Rs. 2902 crore,  an expenditure of Rs. 1529 \ncrore has been incurred as on 30.11.2020. \niv. Under SPMRM, GKRA was implemented in 47 SPMRM clusters across the six (6) \nStates viz. Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Odisha and Jharkhand to \nprovide livelihood opportunities to migrant workers and rural citizens. A total of \n13,886 works with an investment of Rs.789.35 crore under both Critical Gap Funding \n(CGF) and Convergence  were proposed during the  Abhiyaan period, out of which \n13,494 works (97%) were completed with an  expenditure of Rs. 693.55 crore (88%) \nunder GKRA. \n Other major steps taken by the MoRD under various schemes during the outbreak of Covid -\n19 are listed below. All these programmes of MoRD are being implemented expeditiously for \nsocio- economic development in rural areas. \ni. Mahatma Gandhi NREGS which is a demand driven wage employment programme, \nprovides at least one hundred days of guaranteed wage employment in every financial \nyear to every rural household whose adult members vo"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |369", "t": "Wage Payments under MGNREGS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 369 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nWAGE PAYMENTS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n369.  SHRI GAURAV GOGOI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural  Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS) is facing a negative net balance, if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  the details of the pending liabilities till date as  part of the MGNREGS, with separate \nlists of the wage and material components, State-wise; \n(c)  the number of person -days of work demanded  and number of person -days of work \ngenerated in 2019-20 and thus far in 2020-21; \n(d)  whether the Government has instructed that  wage payments under MGNREGS be \nsplit according to caste categories; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor  along with the reasons for retracting \nsuch order? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) & (b)    Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme is a demand \ndriven scheme. There are enough funds with the scheme to fulfill all the liabilities of the \nscheme for the States which have met the entire conditions for release of second tranche of \nfunds. The Ministry has already sought additional resources for the Scheme. \nState/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for wage component under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS as on 25.11.2021 is given at Annexure-I.   \nState/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for materi al component under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS as on 25.11.2021 are given at Annexure-II. \n(c)  Under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS, the employment demanded by persons, employment \noffered to persons and employment provided to persons in the financial year 2019 -20 and FY \n2020-21 is given below: \n2019-20 (in lakhs) 2020-21 (in Lakhs) \nEmployment \ndemanded by \npersons \nEmployment \noffered to \npersons \nEmployment \navailed by \npersons \nEmployment \ndemanded by \npersons \nEmployment \noffered to \npersons \nEmployment \navailed by \npersons \n933.95 930.50 788.45 1332.58 1329.8 1118.78 \n  \nIn the financial year 2019-20, a total number of 265.35 crore persondays have been generated \nand in FY 2020 -21, a total number of 389.17 crore persondays have been generated under \nMahatma Gandhi NREGS. \n (d) & (e)  The category wise (SC, ST and Others) wage payment system, as made applicable \nfrom this current financial year, has been introduced to accurately reflect on ground flow of \nfunds to various population groups. Its further streamlining is being undertaken.  \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nAnnexure-I referred in reply to part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.369 \ndue for answer on 30.11.2021 \n  \nState/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for wage component under Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS as on 25.11.2021 \n  \nSl No. States/UTs \nWage liability \n(Rs. in Lakh) \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 36990.81 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 6759.12 \n3 ASSAM 0.00 \n4 BIHAR 29618.61 \n5 CHHATTISGARH 2195.34 \n6 GOA 0.00 \n7 GUJARAT 0.00 \n8 HARYANA 1500.34 \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 15670.17 \n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 10934.93 \n11 JHARKHAND 20693.82 \n12 KARNATAKA 50888.40 \n13 KERALA 0.00 \n14 LADAKH 54.23 \n15 MADHYA PRADESH 38258.58 \n16 MAHARASHTRA 10689.04 \n17 MANIPUR 0.00 \n18 MEGHALAYA 3395.59 \n19 MIZORAM 6923.41 \n20 NAGALAND 2277.99 \n21 ODISHA 0.00 \n22 PUNJAB 11112.44 \n23 RAJASTHAN 5765.71 \n24 SIKKIM 0.00 \n25 TAMIL NADU 13330.71 \n26 TELANGANA 4798.77 \n27 TRIPURA 0.00 \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 0.00 \n29 UTTARAKHAND 0.00 \n30 WEST BENGAL 124440.01 \n31 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 0.00 \n32 LAKSHADWEEP 0.00 \n33 PUDUCHERRY 33.37 \n34 THE DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI \nAND DAMAN AND DIU 0.00 \n  Total 396331.40 \n  \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-II \n \nAnnexure-II referred in reply to part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.369 due for answer on 30.11.2021 \n  \n  \nState/UT-wise details of pending liabilities for material component under Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS as on 25-Nov-2021 \nSl No. States/UTs \nMaterial liability \n(Rs. in Lakh) \n1 2 3 \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 134068.91 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 4519.9 \n3 ASSAM 6466.28 \n4 BIHAR 54832.56 \n5 CHHATTISGARH 26136.55 \n6 GOA 1.08 \n7 GUJARAT 11394.92 \n8 HARYANA 4594.65 \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 4043.94 \n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 4019.34 \n11 JHARKHAND 20886.94 \n12 KARNATAKA 50854.94 \n13 KERALA 11737.22 \n14 LADAKH 35.5 \n15 MADHYA PRADESH 86182.33 \n16 MAHARASHTRA 8882.39 \n17 MANIPUR 6857.13 \n18 MEGHALAYA 11037.27 \n19 MIZORAM 24.98 \n20 NAGALAND 1099.75 \n21 ODISHA 76585.8 \n22 PUNJAB 15905.06 \n23 RAJASTHAN 83214.15 \n24 SIKKIM 877.85 \n25 TAMIL NADU 38998.24 \n26 TELANGANA 26007.55 \n27 TRIPURA 1593.8 \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 14879.43 \n29 UTTARAKHAND 12028.09 \n30 WEST BENGAL 107689.32 \n31 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 10 \n32 LAKSHADWEEP 0 \n33 PUDUCHERRY 8.33 \n34 THE DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI \nAND DAMAN AND DIU 0 \n  Total 825474.2 \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |37", "t": "Encroachment on Wildlife Sancturaries and Protected Areas", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \n \nLOK \nSABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n37\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nSAHAKAR MITRA \n-\n \nNCDC INTERNSHIP SCHEME\n \n \n \n*\n37\n.\n \nSHRI \nVISHNU DAYAL RAM\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of \nCooperation \nसहका\u0000रता\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to s\ntate: \n \n \n(a)\n \nthe details of the Sahakar Mitra \n–\n \nNational Cooperative Development Corporation \n(NCDC) Internship Scheme;\n \n \n(b)\n \nthe list of all the interns who have benefitted from this scheme till date; and\n \n \n(c)\n \nthe details of the funds invested by the Governm\nent to run this scheme?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF \nCOOPERATION\n \n \nसहका\u0000रता\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI \nAMIT SHAH\n)\n \n \n(a) to (c):   \n    \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRE\nD TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c\n) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n37\n \nDUE FOR REPLY ON \n30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER, 2021\n \n \n \n(a)\n:\n \n \nT\nhe details of the \n“\nSAHAKAR MITRA Scheme on Internship Program” \nof National \nCooperative Development Corporation (NCDC) \nis \nenclosed at Annexure\n-\nA. \n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nthe list of all the interns who have benefitted from this scheme till date is \nenclosed at Annexure\n-\nB.\n \n(\nc)\n:\n \nThe \nsaid scheme\n \nis \nneither a Government of India scheme nor funded by the \nGovernment. It is f\nunded \nfrom\n \nits own \nresources\n \nby the NCDC\n.  \n \n \n \n \n\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n37\n \nANNEXURE\n-\nA\n \nThe “SAHAKAR MITRA Scheme on Internship Program” (SIP) is an arrangement where \nNatio\nnal Cooperative Development Corporation (NCDC)\n \nprovide\ns\n \nshort term (not \nexceeding four months) opportunity to young professionals \nto \nacquire learning \nexperience by applying skills and knowledge in organizational context to facilitate \nprofessional developme\nnt as also to lend the benefits of \ntheir \nservices, talent and ideas \nto the Corporation. The programme \nalso \naims at providing an opportunity to \nprofessional graduates to get experience in areas of functioning of NCDC and related \naspects.\n \nA p\nerson with any o\nf the following qualification\ns\n \nis\n \neligible for consideration:\n-\n \ni\n.\n \nProfessional graduat\nes with minimum qualification of\n \nBachelor’s degree in \nAgri\nculture \n/ Dairy\n \n/ Animal Husbandry\n \n/ Veterinary Sciences\n \n/ Fisheries / \nHorticulture\n \n/ Textiles\n \n/ Handloom / IT\n \n(Inf\normation Technology)\n,\n \nduly \nrecommended by the Head of the Department of the UGC / AICTE / ICAR \nrecognized University / Institution as applicable.\n \nii\n.\n \nProfessional (pursuing or completed) MBA \n(\nAgri\ncultural B\nusiness\n)\n \n/ MBA \n(\nCoop\nerative)\n \n/ M.Com\n \n/ MCA / MBA \n(\nFina\nnce\n) \n/ MBA \n(\nInternational Trade\n)\n \n/ \nMBA \n(\nForestry\n) \n/ MBA \n(\nRural Dev\nelopment)\n \n/ MBA \n(\nProject Management\n)\n \n/ \nInter\nmediate\n \nICAI / \nInter\nmediate\n \nICWA\n,\n \nqualification duly recommended by \nthe Head of the Department of the UGC / AICTE / ICAR recognized University \n/ I\nnstitution as applicable.\n \nThe details and guidelines of the scheme is available\n \nat the portal \nhttp://sip.ncdc.in\n \n \n.\n \n\nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 37\n \nANNEXURE\n-\nB\n \nS.No.\n \nName of Intern\n \nPlace of Internship\n \nDuration\n \n1\n \nMr. Na\ndikuda Ravi Kiran\n \nHyderabad\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n2\n \nMr. M. S. Keerthana\n \nBengaluru\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n3\n \nMs. Shruti Shukla\n \nLucknow\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n4\n \nMr. Bishnu Shankar Mishra\n \nLucknow\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n5\n \nMr. Tushar Virmani\n \nLINAC\n-\nG\nurugram\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n6\n \nMr. Nitin Azad\n \nShimla\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n7\n \nMr. Sattwick Das\n \nBhubaneswar\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n8\n \nMs. Shivani Vishwanath Honrao\n \nPune\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n9\n \nMr. Daulat Singh\n \nJaipur\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n10\n \nMs. Neha Agarwal\n \nJaipur\n \n01.09.2020 to 31.12.2020\n \n11\n \nMr. Ullash Kumar\n \nPatna\n \n19.10.2020 to 18.02.2021\n \n12\n \nMr. Rahul Raj\n \nPatna\n \n19.10.2020 to 18.02.2021\n \n13\n \nMs. Deepti Tripathi\n \nBhopal\n \n01.02.2021 to 31.05.2021\n \n14\n \nMs. Vibha Gadpayle\n \nBhopal\n \n01.02.2021 to 31.\n05.2021\n \n15\n \nMs. Sweta Sahu\n \nBhubaneswar\n \n01.02.2021 to 31.05.2021\n \n16\n \nMr. Ashish Kumar Gouda\n \nBhubaneswar\n \n01.02.2021 to 31.05.2021\n \n17\n \nMr. Sajid Ali\n \nHead Office\n \n01.02.2021 to 31.05.2021\n \n18\n \nMr. Shivam Kumar Gupta\n \nHead Office\n \n01.02.2021 to 31.05.2021\n \n19\n \nMr. Sanjeev Kumar\n \nHead Office\n \n01.02.2021 to 31.05.2021\n \nS.No.\n \nName of Intern\n \nPlace of Internship\n \nDuration\n \n20\n \nMr. Divyansh Mahajan\n \nBhopal\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n21\n \nMs. Ritika Santosh Nimsarkar\n \nBhopal\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n22\n \nMs. Priyam Talukdar\n \nGuwa\nhati\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n23\n \nMr. Manish Sharma\n \nJaipur\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n24\n \nMs. Parvani Sharma\n \nChandigarh\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n25\n \nMs. Pronipra Ghosh\n \nKolkata\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n26\n \nMs. Sohini Roy\n \nKolkata\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n27\n \nM\ns. Simran  Arora\n \nLINAC, Gurugram\n \n14.06.2021 to 14.10.2021\n \n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |370", "t": "Rules and Regulations for OTT Platforms", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 370 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th November, 2021 \n \nRULES AND REGULATIONS FOR OTT PLATFORMS \n \n370. SHRI RAJENDRA DHEDYA GAVIT  \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) Whether the existing rules and regulations do not prevent the \ntransmission of objectionable content broadcast by Over -the-Top \n(OTT) platforms, if so, the details thereof; \n(b) Whether the rich cultural traditions and beliefs of the country are \nbeing harmed by these contents, if so, the details thereof ; \n(c) Whether the contents available on such platforms can have \nnegative impact on the mindset of the adolescents, if so the deta ils \nthereof ; \n(d) Whether OTT platforms now come under the purview of the \nGovernment as per the notification issued earlier by the Government; \nand if so , the details thereof ; and \n(e) Whether the Government has any specific legislation that regulates \nOTT platforms to ensure that peoples’ sentiments are taken care of, if \nso , the details thereof ? \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS  \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR): \n(a) to (e): - Government has notified Information Technology \n(Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules, 2021, \non 25th February, 2021 under Information Technology Act, 2000. A \ncopy of the Rules is available on the website of the Ministry i.e. \nwww.mib.gov.in.  \nPart-III of the Rules, inter - alia, provide for Code of Ethics to be \nfollowed by publishers of news & current affairs on digital media and \npublishers of online curated content (OTT Platforms).   T he Code of \nEthics for OTT platforms specifies general principles to be followed by \nthe platforms for carriage of content, classification of content into five \nage based categories, display of such classification, and restricting \naccess to children of higher  rated content. Schedule to the Rules lays \ndown guiding principles for content classification.   \nThe Rules also provide for a three -level Grievance Redressal \nMechanism relating to the violation of Code of Ethics by the \npublishers.    \nAs per the Allocation of Business Rules (as  amended on 9.11.2020) \nthe subject relating to content of news and current affairs and audio -\nvisual content on online platforms is under the mandate of Ministry of \nInformation & Broadcasting. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |371", "t": "Shortage of Fodder to Livestock", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION  NO. 371 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \nSHORTAGE OF FODDER TO LIVESTOCK \n371    SHRI THIRUMAAVALAVAN THOL  \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the shortage of dry fodder, green fodder and \nconcentrate feed for the Indian livestock; \n (b) if so, the details thereof including shortage and the adverse impact on the dairy \nproduct may be provided;  \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to bridge the shortage gap in a time -bound \nperiod; and \n (d) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF  FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n (SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) & (b) The Thirty Fourth Report on Standing Committee on Agriculture hasestimated   \nthat the country has 530 million tons, 880million tons and 96 million ton s requirement of \ndry fodder, green fodder and concentrate, in  the year  2020 against the availability o f \n408million tons, 596million tons and 61 million tons having a  deficit of 23% dry fodder, \n32% green fodder and 36% concentrate, on dry matter basis. By the year 2025, the deficit \nof dry fodder, green fodder and concentrate would be 23%, 40% and 38%, respectively.  \nThe shortage of fodder may affect the milk production and thereby the dairy product. \n \n (c) & (d) Union Government in conjunction with the State  Governments and Union \nTerritories has already taken necessary steps to bridge the gap of fodder through various \nongoing Schemes viz., National Livestock Mission with a Sub Mission on Fodder and \nFeed Development and Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana.Under thes e schemes, financial \nassistance is provided to all the States and Union Territories for various fodder \ndevelopment activities planned by them.  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |372", "t": "Setting up of New FM and Community Radio Stations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.372 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \n“Setting up of New FM and Community Radio Stations” \n372. SHRI RAJMOHAN UNNITHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has any policy relating to FM stations and Community \nRadio stations in each district in the country, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has any proposal to start an FM and Community \nRadio station in Kasaragod (Kerala) district if the land is made available /allocated and \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of the kilowatts capacity of the Kannur FM and Community station; \n \n(d) whether there is any proposal to increase the kilowatts capacity, if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n \n(e) whether the Government is preparing any auction for FM station under the \nprivate sector and if so, the details thereof; \n  \n(f)  the criteria for a private party to set up a new FM and Community Radio \nstation; and  \n \n(g) Whether there is any proposal/intent from any private players to start an FM \nand Community Radio station in the Kasaragod district and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND MINISTER OF \nYOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n********** \na:     U nder the Policy Guidelines on Expansion of FM radio broadcasting service \nthrough private agencies (Phase -III), the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting \ngrants FM Radio license to private companies registered in Indi a under the \nCompanies Act, 2013. The Private FM Radio Phase -III Policy being city centric \nprovides for setting up of FM radio stations in all cities having population above one \nlakh, unless it is getting covered from an adjacent city. Further, 11 cities in Border \n\nAreas of UT of Jammu & Kashmi r, UT of Ladakh and North East States having \npopulation less than 1 lakh have also been approved for setting up FM radio stations.   \n \n The Government has also laid a Policy Guidelines for setting up of Community \nRadio Stations (CRS) in India.  Setting up of CRS is a voluntary activity by the not-for-\nprofit organization, fulfilling the eligibility conditions as prescribed in the policy.  \n \n The Policy Guidelines on both Private FM Radio (Ph -III) and CRS are available \non Ministry’s website www.mib.gov.in \n \n In addition, Prasar Bharati has informed that it is having 52 3 FM transmitters \nlocated at 483 All India Radio (AIR) Stations across the country.  \n \nb:   Prasar Bharati is having a 100W AIR FM Relay Centre  in Kasaragod district in \nthe State of Kerala. There is no proposal under consideration for setting up of CRS in \nKasaragod (Kerala) district. \n \nc & d:    Prasar Bharati has one 10 KW AIR FM transmitter at Kannur which was \nupgraded from 6 kW to 10 KW in the year 2018. One CRS is being operated by \nAcademic & Technical Education Development Society in Kannur, Kerala having \npermitted transmitter power of 100 Watts (ERP). There is no proposal to further \nincrease the kilowatts capacity of this CRS. \n \ne :    Under FM Phase-III, Government has approved to auction 683 channels in 236 \ncities.   \n \nf:   Under the Private FM Radio (Ph -III) Policy only companies registered in India \nunder the Companies Act and meeting net worth criteria and other conditions as \nenumerated in t he policy shall be eligible for bidding and obtaining permission for FM \nradio channels.  \n      As per the CRS Policy Guidelines, not -for-profit community -based organizations, \nwhich satisfy basic principles enumerated as per the Policy Guidelines, are eligi ble for \napplying for Community Radio licenses.  \ng :  Presently, no application, from any private organization/institution for setting up \nCommunity Radio Station in Kasaragod district, is under consideration. \n \n******* \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |373", "t": "Storage in Rural Areas", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n373\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n30\nth\n \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nSTORAGE IN RURAL AREAS\n \n \n373\n. \n \nDR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN:\n \n \nWill the\n \nMinister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS\n \nWELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n  \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether the Government has implemented\n \nAgricultural Marketing Infrastructure \nscheme under which\n \nassistance has been provided for construction and\n \nrenovation of \ngodowns, etc. in rural areas of all States\n \nincluding Bihar to enhance the storage capacity \nfor\n \nagriculture products;\n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons\n \ntherefor;\n \n(c) the details of the action taken by the Government\n \nduring\n \nthe last three years for \ncreating more storage\n \nfacilities in Bihar particularly in the district of Gopalganj;\n \n(d) whether the Government has proposed to\n \nconstruct sufficient storage facilities in Bihar \nincluding\n \nin Gopalganj district considering the deman\nds of farmers;and\n \n(e\n) \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS\n \nWELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH\n \nTOMAR)\n \n(a) & (b):\n \nYes Sir. For creation of Agricultural Marketing infrastructure including \nScientific \nStorage, the Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare, Government of India is \nimplementing sub\n-\nscheme “Agricultural Marketing Infrastructure (AMI)” under Integrated \nScheme for Agricultural Marketing (ISAM) across the country including rural areas of all \ns\ntates including Bihar.\n \n \n \nUnder the scheme, assistance is available to Individuals, Farmers, Group of \nfarmers/growers, Agri\n-\npreneurs, Registered Farmer Produce Organizations (FPOs), \nCooperatives, and state agencies etc. It is a credit linked scheme wherein \nback ended \ncapital subsidy @ 25%(for general category) and 33.33%(for women/SC/ST and Tribal & \nHilly areas) is available based on the eligible category of beneficiary.\n \n \n \nUnder the AMI Scheme, since 2001 and up to 30.09.2021, a total of 40,985 \nstorage infra\nstructure projects (Godowns) with a  capacity of 708.67 Lakh MT have been \n\nprovided in the country, including 1086 projects in the State of Bihar with a capacity of \n7.06 Lakh MT.\n \n \n \n(c) to (e):\n \n \nDuring the last three years, a total of 87 storage infrastructu\nre projects with \ncapacity of 1.92 Lakh MT have been assisted in Bihar State under AMI Scheme. Further, \nsince 2001, a total of 72 projects with capacity of 8075 MT capacity were assisted in \nGopalganj District alone.\n \nHowever, during the last three years, the\nre were no applicants \nunder the scheme. \n \n \nFurther, a Central Sector Scheme, ‘Agriculture Infrastructure Fund’ was launched \nby the Government on 9\nth\n \nAugust 2020 which provides interest subvention and credit \nguarantee support for creation of post\n-\nharvest man\nagement infrastructure and community \nfarming assets, including storage facilities. Till date, a total of Rs. 13.8 Crore loan amount \nhas been sanctioned for storage projects in Bihar under the above scheme.\n  \n \n \n*****\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |374", "t": "Promotion of Fisheries", "head": " \nO.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRYAND DAIRYING  \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 374 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nPromotion of Fisheries \n \n374.  SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH: \nSHRI NAYAB SINGH: \nDR. RAM SHANKAR KATHERIA: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING मत्स्यपालन, \nपशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has formulated/proposed  to formulate any scheme to double the \nproduction of fish and income of fishermen, including the States of  Madhya Pradesh and Uttar \nPradesh; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) the details of the steps taken under Atamnirbhar Bharat Mission to provide training and financial \nassistance for fisheries to the fishermen during current and last three years, State/UT-wise; and  \n \n(d)  the details of the funds issued fo r promotion of  fisheries in the States of Madhya Pradesh and \nUttar Pradesh during each of the last three years?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a) to (d):  The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of F isheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying has \nlaunched a flagship scheme namely “Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana  (PMMSY)” as a part \nof the Atma Nirbhar Bharat relief package with a highest ever investment of Rs. 20050 crore in \nfisheries sector. The PMMSY is implemented over a period of 5 years with effect from the financial \nyear 2020 -21 to 2024 -25 in all States/Union Territories including Uttar Pradesh and Madhya \nPradesh for sustainable development of fisheries and aquaculture sector . The scheme intends to \naddress critical gaps in fish production and productivity, quality, technology, post -harvest \ninfrastructure and management, modernization and strengthening of value chain, traceability, \nestablishing a robust fisheries management framework and fishers’ w elfare including enhancement \nof fish production and income of fishermen.  PMMSY inter-alia lays special focus on training, skill \ndevelopment, skill up -gradation and capacity building through trainings, awareness building \nprogrammes and exposure visits to s takeholders especially fishers, fish farmers, fish workers, fish \nvendors, entrepreneurs,  officials, fisheries Cooperatives and members of Fish Farmer Producer \nOrganization. An amount of Rs . 100 crore is earmarked in PMMSY for Training, Awareness, \nExposure and Capacity Building and is being utilised to impart training through National Fisheries \nDevelopment Board, ICAR institutes, Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs), other organisations and State \nDepartments. A total of 3774 outreach events covering about 20.78 lakh  beneficiaries have been \ncompleted so far.   \n     \nState/UT-wise details of the central funds released to the States/UTs including Madhya  \nPradesh and Uttar Pradesh during the last three years  and current financial year for development of \nfisheries and aquaculture are furnished at Annexure-I.  \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I \nStatement referred to part (c) and (d) in reply to the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 374 due for answer on 30 th November, 2021  regarding promotion of fisheries : \nState/UT-wise details of Central funds released for development of fisheries during last \nthree years and current year \n \n(Rs. in lakhs) \n \nS. N State/UT 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22* \n(i) (ii) (iii) (iv) (v) (vi) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 129.09 51.21 200.00 497.00 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1302.31 2556.20 5554.12 5920.12 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 374.68 24.77 790.61 0.00 \n4 Assam 524.14 280.01 2676.56 4941.01 \n5 Bihar 4078.69 1557.15 2932.12 1252.00 \n6 Chhattisgarh 1045.69 3293.90 3606.74 1149.58 \n7 Daman & Diu 0.00 0.00 178.90 0.00 \n8 Goa 63.82 0.00 596.74 0.00 \n9 Gujarat 1420.80 1000.00 893.70 4698.00 \n10 Haryana 605.90 696.47 953.94 853.62 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 1046.13 629.14 1467.28 85.37 \n12 Jammu & Kashmir 439.54 788.63 4207.20 1126.36 \n13 Jharkhand 925.40 0.00 1618.56 1009.60 \n14 Karnataka 5847.28 2330.61 6235.30 5112.11 \n15 Kerala 5093.01 9357.15 800.00 4289.74 \n16 Lakshadweep 39.90 29.30 1344.12 75.00 \n17 Madhya Pradesh 711.39 1859.47 2379.16 4879.98 \n18 Maharashtra 2861.17 1000.00 3657.42 2141.00 \n19 Manipur 382.13 50.68 1097.97 0.00 \n20 Meghalaya 2598.94 2387.45 790.65 0.00 \n21 Mizoram 403.75 735.99 986.77 215.58 \n22 Nagaland 194.67 2396.17 807.07 0.00 \n23 NCT Delhi 0.00 0.00 144.30 0.00 \n24 Odisha 1022.13 543.68 3995.82 5918.76 \n25 Puducherry 1448.00 461.37 990.50 0.00 \n26 Punjab 581.34 2063.76 567.84 0.00 \n27 Rajasthan 70.20 266.20 210.82 90.00 \n28 Sikkim 416.52 1019.65 313.20 4.80 \n29 Tamil Nadu 7155.91 1801.00 1938.61 2550.00 \n30 Telangana 1574.80 154.00 779.34 1299.00 \n31 Tripura 2004.18 788.91 737.01 1179.79 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 3050.06 3816.63 7009.62 972.00 \n33 Uttarakhand 471.08 683.67 912.61 917.40 \n34 West Bengal 185.40 0.00 0.00 0.00 \n35 Ladakh 0.00 0.00 37.50 0.00 \n Total  48068.03 42623.17 61412.10 51177.82 \n   \n*till date \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |375", "t": "Atrocities against Dalits", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 375 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nATROCITIES AGAINST DALITS \n \n375.  KUNWAR DANISH ALI: \nSHRI ANUMULA REVANTH REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that the atrocitis against  Dalits have increased in the \ncountry particularly in the State of Telangana and Uttar Pradesh; \n \n(b) if so, the number of cases of violence against  Dalits reported in the country \nduring the last three years  and recent year along with the details thereof, \nregionwise and district-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has issued any  instructions to stop the atrocities \nagainst the Dalits in the country; especially in Uttar Pradesh; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) & (b): The National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) compiles and publishes \nstatistical data on crimes in its publication “Crime in India”. The published \nreports are available till the year 2020. The details of the number of cases \nregistered   under  crime/ atrocities   against   Scheduled   Castes  (SCs)  in  the  \n\n--2— \nLS.US.Q.NO. 375 FOR 30.11.2021 \ncountry during the last three years are at Annexure -I. While, region-wise data is \nnot maintained by NCRB, details of district -wise cases registered for crime / \natrocities against SCs are available at website of NCRB (https://ncrb.gov.in) \n(c) to (e): “Police” and “Public Order” are State subjects under the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. The responsibility of maintaining law and \norder, protection of life and property of the citizens including SCs, rest w ith the \nrespective State Governments and the State Governments are competent to \ndeal with such offences under the extant provisions of laws. However, Ministry \nof Home Affairs have been issuing advisories to States/Union Territories from \ntime to time to dea l with crimes against SCs, which are available at \nwww.mha.gov.in. \n***** \n  \n\nLS.US.Q.NO. 375 FOR 30.11.2021 \nAnnexure-I \nState/UT-wise Cases Registered (CR) under Crime against Scheduled Castes (SCs) during 2018-2020 \n \nSL State/UT 2018 2019 2020 \nCR CR CR \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1836 2071 1950 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0 0 0 \n3 Assam 8 21 28 \n4 Bihar 7061 6544 7368 \n5 Chhattisgarh 264 341 316 \n6 Goa 5 3 2 \n7 Gujarat 1426 1416 1326 \n8 Haryana 961 1086 1210 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 130 189 251 \n10 Jharkhand 537 651 666 \n11 Karnataka 1325 1504 1398 \n12 Kerala 887 858 846 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 4753 5300 6899 \n14 Maharashtra 1974 2150 2569 \n15 Manipur 0 0 0 \n16 Meghalaya 0 0 0 \n17 Mizoram 0 0 0 \n18 Nagaland 0 0 0 \n19 Odisha 1778 1886 2046 \n20 Punjab 168 166 165 \n21 Rajasthan 4607 6794 7017 \n22 Sikkim 5 4 0 \n23 Tamil Nadu 1413 1144 1274 \n24 Telangana 1507 1690 1959 \n25 Tripura 1 0 2 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 11924 11829 12714 \n27 Uttarakhand 58 84 87 \n28 West Bengal 119 145 109 \n  TOTAL STATE(S) 42747 45876 50202 \n29 A&N Islands 0 0 0 \n30 Chandigarh 1 1 3 \n31 D&N Haveli and Daman & Diu+ 1 2 1 \n32 Delhi 36 76 69 \n33 Jammu & Kashmir* 1 2 7 \n34 Ladakh - - 0 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 0 0 \n36 Puducherry 7 4 9 \n  TOTAL UT(S) 46 85 89 \n  TOTAL (ALL INDIA) 42793 45961 50291 \n \nSource: Crime in India \nNote : '+' Combined data of erstwhile D&N Haveli UT and Daman & Diu UT for 2018 & 2019  \n*' Data of erstwhile Jammu & Kashmir State including Ladakh for 2018 & 2019  \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |376", "t": "Subsidy for PMKSY-PDMC", "head": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.376\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30\nTH \nNOVEMBER, 2021\n \n \nSUBSIDY FOR PMKSY\n-\nPDMC\n \n \n376.\n \nSHRI SUMEDHANAND SARASWATI:\n \n \nSHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether\n \nthe Government provides subsidy to small and marginal farmers to set up \ndrip and sprinkler irrigation system under Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana\n-\nPer Drop \nMore Crop (PMKSY\n-\nPDMC); \n \n \n(b)\n \nif so, the percentage of subsidy provided to farmers along wit\nh the details thereof;\n \n \n(c)\n \nthe efforts made by the Government to cover economic sectors coming under micro \nirrigation and to promote them;\n \n \n(d)\n \nthe benefits of drip and sprinkler irrigation system set up under PDMC; and \n \n \n(e)\n \nthe progress made in this sch\neme so far along with the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n      \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) to (c) : The Government provides financial assistance/subsidy to small and marginal \nfarmers @ 55% and to other farmers @45% for installation of Drip and Sprinkler Irrigation \nsystems under Per Drop More Crop component of Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee \nYojana \n(PMKSY\n-\nPDMC). In addition, some States provide additional incentives/top up subsidy for \nencouraging farmers to adopt Micro Irrigation. \n \nFor larger adoption of Micro Irrigation systems by the farmers under the PMKSY\n-\n \nPDMC scheme, 25% higher unit cost\n \nhave been taken into calculation of subsidy for the \nNorth Eastern and Himalayan States and 15% higher for States having lower adoption of \nMicro Irrigation. \n \n \n\n \nWith the objective of facilitating the States in mobilising\n \nresources for expanding \ncoverage of micro irrigation, a Micro Irrigation Fund (MIF) with  corpus of Rs. 5000 crore \nwas created with National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) during \n2018\n-\n19. States may access MIF for special and innovati\nve projects and also for additional \nincentive to encourage farmers to install Micro Irrigation systems.  To continue the efforts, \nduring the current year, a Budget Announcement was made to double the initial corpus of \nMIF from Rs. 5,000 crores to Rs. 10,00\n0 crore.\n \n(d) : The PMKSY\n-\nPDMC focuses on enhancing water use efficiency at farm level through \nMicro Irrigation. The Micro Irrigation helps in water saving as well as reduced fertilizer usage \nthrough fertigation, labour expenses, other input costs and overa\nll income enhancement of \nfarmers. Recent evaluation studies of the scheme have reiterated that Micro Irrigation is \nrelevant in achieving national priorities such as improving on\n-\nfarm water use efficiency, \nenhancing crop productivity, generating employment \nopportunities etc.  \n \n(e):\n \nAn area of 59.37 lakh ha has been covered under Micro Irrigation  in the country \nthrough PMKSY\n-\nPDMC from 2015\n-\n16 to till date with release of Central Assistance of Rs.\n \n15210.99 crore. The State wise details of coverage of area  an\nd amount released \nso far \nis \nAnnexed.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \nAnnexure to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 376 for answer on 30.11.2021\n \nCentral Assistance released and area covered under Micro Irrigation under PMKSY\n-\n \nPDMC \nfrom 2015\n-\n16 to 2021\n-\n22 (as on\n \n22.11.2021)\n \nS. \nNo.\n \nState\n \nCentral Assistance released\n \n(Rs. in Crore)\n \nArea covered \nunder Micro \nIrrigation (Ha)\n \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n2104.16\n \n743992\n \n2\n \nBihar\n \n112.21\n \n19517\n \n3\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n240.64\n \n106658\n \n4\n \nGoa\n \n2.80\n \n868\n \n5\n \nGujarat\n \n1612.55\n \n847863\n \n6\n \nHaryana\n \n218.19\n \n65136\n \n7\n \nHimachal Pra.\n \n116.85\n \n10486\n \n8\n \nJharkhand\n \n175.64\n \n23758\n \n9\n \nJ&K\n \n58.07\n \n1090\n \n10\n \nKarnataka\n \n2309.15\n \n1246354\n \n11\n \nKerala\n \n42.53\n \n3166\n \n12\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n792.40\n \n237711\n \n13\n \nMaharashtra\n \n1760.46\n \n655177\n \n14\n \nOdisha\n \n231.40\n \n42725\n \n15\n \nPunjab\n \n53.18\n \n6540\n \n16\n \nRajasthan\n \n922.82\n \n371569\n \n17\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n2036.83\n \n893256\n \n18\n \nTelangana\n \n679.32\n \n254034\n \n19\n \nUttarakhand\n \n176.80\n \n25153\n \n20\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n521.79\n \n268995\n \n21\n \nWest Bengal\n \n176.70\n \n52152\n \n22\n \nArun. Pradesh\n \n88.40\n \n6898\n \n23\n \nAssam\n \n122.03\n \n21847\n \n24\n \nManipur\n \n157.36\n \n12851\n \n25\n \nMeghalaya\n \n31.73\n \n-\n \n26\n \nMizoram\n \n104.47\n \n3265\n \n27\n \nNagaland\n \n169.64\n \n9810\n \n28\n \nSikkim\n \n136.24\n \n6297\n \n29\n \nTripura\n \n51.50\n \n-\n \n30\n \nA&D Island\n \n0.70\n \n-\n \n31\n \nPuducherry\n \n2.03\n \n-\n \n32\n \nLadakh\n \n2.40\n \n-\n \n \n \nGrand Total\n \n15210.99\n \n5937168\n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |378", "t": "Development of Roads under PMGSY", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 378 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF ROADS UNDER PMGSY \n \n378.  SHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to expedite  the development of roads under \nPradhan Mantri Gram  Sadak Yojana(PMGSY), if so, the details of action taken  \nthereon; \n(b)  the details of sanction given for roads in Kollam  Lok Sabha constituency under \nPMGSY; \n(c)  the details regarding delay in execution of development of roads under PMGSY; \n(d)  whether the Government consider the  recommendation of State Government for the \nselection of roads under PMGSY, if so, the details thereof; \n(e)  whether the Government proposes to give  sanction to Kuzhiyam - Thalayathu- \nThannivila Mevar  Elavila Moolapana Alamkode Temple Anapuzhakkal road  of \nChadayamangalam block in Kollam District; \n(f)  if so, the details of action taken thereon, if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(g)  whether the Government proposes to implement  the works as per the \nrecommendations of the State  Government considering the rain and other \ngeographical peculiarities of State, if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a)     As per the programme guidelines of Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY), \nthe execution of works under the scheme is the responsibility of the State Government \nconcerned. The time-lines for completion of various categories of work falling in different \ngeographical locations are laid down in the programme guidelines and states are expected to \ncomply with these provisions. In order to ensure that the sanctioned projects are compl eted \nwithin the time -line, the Ministry has amended the programme guidelines to ensure that all \nworks sanctioned under scheme commence on ground within 72 days from the date of \nsanction. \n  \nThe implementation of all sanctioned works is being monitored throu gh online programme \nMonitoring Information System named Online Management, Monitoring and Accounting \nSystem (OMMAS) on real time basis.  In addition, the progress is regularly reviewed by the \nMinistry by way of Regional Review Meetings (RRMs), Performance R eview Committee \n(PRC) Meetings, Pre -Empowered/Empowered Committee Meetings with the States. At \nDistrict level, the District Development Coordination and Monitoring Committee (DISHA) \nheaded by Hon’ble Member of Parliament (LS) monitors the implementation of  various \nschemes of Government of India including PMGSY.  Besides above, special review \n\nmeetings/monthly review meetings are also held by Secretary/ Additional Secretary/Joint \nSecretary, Ministry of Rural Development with Chief Secretaries/Principal Secreta ries of the \nStates to take stock of the progress of the scheme and remove the bottlenecks, if any. \n  \nThe Government has fixed a timeline of September 2022 for completing the works under \nPMGSY- I & II, and a timeline of March 2023 for completing the works under Road \nConnectivity Project for Left Wing Extremism Affected Areas   (RCPLWEA). The timeline \nfor completion of PMGSY-III is March 2025. \n  \n(b)    As informed by the State Government, the details of projects sanctioned in Kollam \ndistrict under various interventions of PMGSY are as under:- \n  \nName of Intervention Projects sanctioned \nNo. of roads Road length in \nKm \nPMGSY-I 23 52 \nPMGSY-II 7 32 \nPMGSY-III 12 56 \n  \n(c)      As informed by the State Government, all the projects under PMGSY -I in Kollam \nParliamentary constituency have already been completed. Only 1 road works is pending \nunder PMGSY-II, which is targeted for completion by March, 2022. All 12 road works under \nPMGSY-III in Kollam Parliamentary Constituency have been sanctioned in October, 2021. \n  \n(d)   As per programme guidelines, selection of roads is based on Comprehensive \nUpgradation cum Consolidation Priority List (CUCPL) prepared by the State Government \nand duly approved/vetted by the District Panchayat and State Level Standing Committee. The \nproposals received from the public representatives are also taken into consideration while \nfinalizing the CUCPL and annual project proposals by the State Government.  \n  \n(e) to (f)  The State Government had proposed the road namely MRL16 -Kuzhiyam-Thalayat-\nThannivila-Mevara-Elavila-Moolappanna-Alamkode Kshekthram - Anapuzhakkal in Kollam \nDistrict for sanction under PMGSY-III, which is under examination for sanction. \n  \n(g)     The road works under PMGSY are as per the technical specifications and geometri c \ndesign standards given in the Ministry of Rural Development Specifications for Rural Roads, \nRural Roads Manual of the Indian Road Congress (IRC) (IRC: SP:20) and also, where \nrequired, the Hill Road Manual (IRC: SP:48) and other IRC Codes/ Guidelines whic h take \ninto account different geographical conditio"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |379", "t": "Community Radio Services", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 379 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021) \n \nCOMMUNITY RADIO SERVICES \n \n379. SHRI M. SELVARAJ \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION & BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details and the number of Community Radio Services in the country; \n(b) whether there is any proposal to expand it further; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND MINISTER OF YOUTH \nAFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) At present, there are 338 Community Radio Stations (CRS) operational in the \ncountry. Details of these CRS are available on the Ministr y’s website \nhttps://mib.gov.in/all_broadcasting_documents. \n \n(b) and (c) The Government, has approved a Central Sector Scheme, namely \n“Supporting Community Radio Movement in India”, to support the Community Radio \nsector. The objective of the scheme is to promote growth  of the sector, especially in \nrural areas and North -Eastern Regions, and to strengthen new and existing \nCommunity Radio Stations with resources and capacity. \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |38", "t": "PMJDY Accounts", "head": "�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n������������������� �\n������������������� �\n�������������������������������� �\n��� ������ �\n�� �������������������� ����\n������������������ ���������� ��������������������� �������������� ��\n�\n�������������� �\n�\n����� �������������������� �\n�\n���������������������������������������������������\n�\n����� �������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������\n�\n����� �������� ���� ����������� ��� ��������� ��� ����� ��� ����� ���� ������� ��� ��������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����\n�\n����� ����������������������������\n��\n������ �\n�\n���������������������������������������������� �� �\n����������� ������ ��\n�������\n���� ��� ��������� ��� ������� ��� ��� ���������������������� ����������� ���� ������ ������� ���\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�����\n��"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |380", "t": "Difference between Market Price and MSP", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n380\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n30\nTH\nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN MARKET PRICE AND MSP\n \n380\n.\n \n   \nSHRI \nBENNY BEHANAN \n:\n \n \n            \nSHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH\n:\n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल् \n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nwhether the \nMinistry is aware that the market prices for October 2021 are \nbelow the MSP for eight major crops, including groundnut, Bajra, Jowar, maize, tur, \nArhar, moong, and paddy in Bihar including district Bhagalpur\n;\n \n \n(b)\n \nif so the \nreasons therefor\n;\n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether\n \nthe National Agricultural Cooperative Marketing Federation of India \n(NAFED) was not able to procure adequate quantities of pulses in the Kharif and \nrabi season;\n \n(d)\n \nif so, the reasons therefor\n;\n \n \n(e)\n \nwhether any remedial measures have been taken to address\n \nthis issue; and \n \n \n(f)\n \nif so, the details thereof\n \nand if not, the reasons therefor\n?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल् \n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(\na) &\n \n(\nb\n): \nCrops like tur/Arhar, groundnut, jowar and bajra\n \nare not cultivated \nsubstantially in Bihar. Market price of maize is\n \nalmost\n \nat par with MSP of maize\n \nin \nOctober, 2021.\n \nProcurement of summer moong is not done by the state government since \nsurplus \nwas\n \nnot available in the market. The harvesting o\nf paddy is\n \nstill going on \nin\n \nmany \ndistrict\ns\n \nof Bihar and  the procurement of paddy  has started since 01.11.2021.\n \n(c) \n&\n \n(\nd\n):   \nGovernment\n \nof India implements\n \nPrice Support Scheme (PSS) under \nthe umbrella scheme of PM\n-\nAASHA for procurement \nof Fair\n \nAverage Quality (FAQ) of \npulses\n \n&\n \noilseeds at Minimum Support Price through Central Nodal Agencies as and \nwhen the market price of these commodities falls below the notified MSP and proposals\n \nare \nreceived from the State/UT Government\n. \nNational Agricultura\nl Cooperative Marketing \nFederation of India (NAFED) is one of the Central Nodal Agencies for procurement of \npulses & oilseeds under Price Support Scheme (PSS) at Minimum Support Price \n\n(MSP)\n.\nThe detail of procurement of pulses \nunder PSS at MSP by NAFED \nsinc\ne 2019\n \nduring Kharif\n \nand Rabi \nSeasons is\n \nat \nannexure\n.\n \n(e) & (f) : \nGovernment extends price support for paddy and wheat through the Food \nCorporation of India (FCI) and State Agencies. Under this policy, whatever food grains are \noffered by farmers within the stipulated period & conforming to the specifications \nprescribed b\ny Government are purchased at Minimum Support Price (MSP) by the State \nGovernment agencies including FCI for Central Pool. It aims to service the National Food \nSecurity Act (NFSA) and other welfare schemes of the Government so that subsidized \nfood grains a\nre supplied to the poor and needy, and to build up buffer stocks of food \ngrains to ensure food grain security. \n \nFurther, different types of nutri\n-\ncereals and maize are\n \nalso\n \nprocured by State \nGovernments itself in consultation with FCI to the extent that the concerned State \nGovernment may utilise the same for distribution under Targeted Public Distribution \nSystem (TPDS) as well as Other Welfare Schemes (OWS).\n \nOilseeds\n \n&\n \npulse\ns of Fair Average Quality (FAQ) are procured from registered \nfarmers under Price Support Scheme under Umbrella Scheme of Pradhan Mantri \nAnnadataAaySanraksHan Abhiyan (PM\n-\nAASHA), as per its prescribed guidelines at MSP \nin consultation with the concerned Sta\nte Government\n.\n \nUnder PM\n-\nAASHA, States / UTs \nare offered to choose either Price Support Scheme (PSS) or Price Deficiency Payment \nScheme (PDPS) in a given procurement season with respect to particular oilseeds crop \nfor the entire State. Further, States have \nthe option to roll out Private Procurement and \nStockist Scheme (PPSS) on pilot basis in district / selected APMC(s) of district involving \nthe participation of private stockist for oilseeds. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \nDetails of  \npulses procured at MSP under PSS  by NAFED since 2019\n-\n20 (as on 24.11.2021)\n \nYear\n \nSeason\n \nCommodity\n \nQuantity Procured  \n \n(IN MTs)\n \nMSP Value  (Rs.  IN LAKH)\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \nKHARIF \n \nMOONG\n \n                                          \n140,018.46 \n \n                                       \n98,713.01 \n \nTOOR \n \n                                          \n536,413.25 \n \n                                     \n311,119.69 \n \nURAD \n \n                                                 \n132.31 \n \n                                               \n75.42 \n \nTotal\n \n                                          \n676,564.02 \n \n                                     \n409,908.12 \n \nRABI \n \nGRAM\n \n                                      \n2,143,274.14 \n \n                                  \n1,044,846.14 \n \nMASOOR \n \n           "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |381", "t": "Comprehensive Possibilities in Agriculture", "head": " \n \nO.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICU\nLTURE AND FARMERS WE\nLFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 381\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER, 2021\n \n \nCOMPREHENSIVE POSSIB\nILITIES IN AGRICULTU\nRE\n \n381\n.\n \nSHRI RAMESH \nCHANDER KAUSHIK\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to State:\n \n(a)\nthe details of the schemes being implemented by the Government to identify under \ncomprehensive possibilities in agriculture in other States including in the State of \nHaryana; \n \n(b)\nthe quantum of funds allocated for these schemes during the last eight years;\n \n \n(c)\nwhether the funds was utilised during the fixed time frame; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER\n \nMinister \nof \nAGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH\n \nTOMAR)\n \n(a) to (d) Details of schemes being implemented by the Department of Agriculture \nand Farmers’ Welfare in the country including the state of Haryana and year wise  \nallo