 ������������ ������ �������� �� ��� ���� ���� ��� �� ����������� ������ ��� ��\n������������������������������������������������� �������������������������������� �\n�\n������ ���������������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n����� ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ ��������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������\n������ �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |420", "t": "Welfare Scheme for the Youth in Madhya Pradesh", "head": "  GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.420 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nWelfare Scheme for the Youth in Madhya Pradesh \n \n†420. SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH PATEL: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the schemes being run for the welfare of the youths in \nMadhya Pradesh; \n \n(b) whether the Government has any special scheme regarding sports and \nyouth welfare for the smal l districts in Madhya Pradesh and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n \n(c) the details of the schemes being run with regard to youth welfare at \nPanchayat levels in rural areas; and  \n \n(d) the details of the special schemes being run at district level for the \nwelfare of youth in Madhya Pradesh? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sp orts implements various \nSchemes/Programmes for promotion of youth affairs and sports in the \ncountry as a whole including in the state of Madhya Pradesh including \nsmall Districts  in Madhya Pradesh as per the following details: \n \nDepartment of Youth Affairs: \n \nDepartment of Youth Affairs of the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports \nhas three major schemes for development of Youth in the country  \nnamely 1. Rashtriya Yuva Sashaktikaran Karyakram (RYSK) consisting \nof seven sub schemes namely (i) Nehru Yuva Kendra Sangathan  (ii) \n\nNational Youth Corps, (iii) National Programme for Youth & \nAdolescent Development, (iv) International Cooperation, (v) Youth \nHostels, (vi) Assistance to Scouting & Guiding Organisations , (vii) \nNational Young Leaders Programme.   2. National Service Scheme \n(NSS) and 3. Rajiv Gandhi National Institute of Youth Deve lopment \n(RGNIYD). \n \nDepartment of Sports: \n \n“Sports” being a State subject, the responsibility of development of \nsports, including sanctioning adequate funds, planning strategies for \nimprovement/development of youth and sports activities and \nconstructions of youth hostels and  stadiums, rests with the State/ \nUnion Territories Governments.  The Ministry of Youth Affair & Sports  \nsupplements their efforts in this regard by providing financial \nassistance for promotion of sports activities through National Sports \nFederations, training in Sports Authority of India (SAI) Centres, support \nunder Khelo India Scheme and for youth activities in the country \nthrough its organization namely Nehru Yuva Kendra Sangathan (NYKS) \nand National Service Scheme (NSS). \n \n(c) & (d):  Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports through Nehru Yuva \nKendra Sangathan , an autonomous body under it undert akes \nmultifarious activities th rough a large network of youth club/ Mahilla \nMandal and volunteer s who work  at the rural level. The programmes \nundertaken by these District Yuva kendras are for Youth Club \ndevelopment, awareness education , capacity enhancement , spirit of \nsportsman ship, folk art and culture , Skill upgradation & programmes \npromotion and national unity.  The programmes undertaken by Nehru \nYuva Kendra Sangathan are at Annexure. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nANNEXURE REFER RED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( c) & (d) OF THE L OK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 420 FOR 30.11.2021 BY SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH \nPATEL REGARDING WELFARE SCHEME FOR  THE YOUTH IN MADHYA \nPRADESH \n \nNYKS organizes following Core Progra mmes and Special Programmes at p an Ind ia level \nincluding in the State of Madhya Pradesh for the welfare of youth: \n \nA. Core Programmes:  \n \n1. Aatma Nirbhar YuWaah Bharat - Skilling and Handholding  \n \na) Orientation of Youth \nb) Personal Contact Program and Facilitation Campaign  \nc) Education in Basic Vocations  \nd) Digital Felicitation - prepare a Cadre of Bank Mitras  \ne) Career Guidance and Career Counselling \n \n2. Combating COVID-19: Mass Awakening and Action Campaign  \n3. Establishing Disaster Risk Reduction & Preparedness Teams  \n4. Youth Led Fit India Movement, Youth Wellness and Positive Life Style \n \na) Training of Youth in Wellness, Positive Life Style and Fit India \nb) Sports Material to Youth Clubs  \nc) Block level Sports Meets  \nd) District Level Sports Meets)    \ne) District Level Promotion of Art & Culture \n \n5. Training of Youth on Clean Village- Green Village \n6. Training of Youth on Jal Jagran Abhiyan \n7. Youth Club Development Program – Formation of Action Plan \n8. Observance of Days of National Importance, National Youth Day & Week \n9. District Youth Convention \n10. Celebration of 150th Birth Anniversary of Mahatma Gandhi : \n \na.   Swachhata Awareness Evam Shramdaan (Swachhata Action Plan)  \nb.   Swachhata Pakhwada (1st to 15th August) \n \n11. Awards to Outstanding Youth Clubs \n12. Declamation Contest on Patriotism & Nation Building \n \nB.   Projects with the support of various Ministries and Agencies \n \na) Tribal Youth Exchange Programme with the support of MHA. \nb) Catch the Rain project with support of Ministry of Jal Shakti. \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |421", "t": "National Sports Awards", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 421 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nNational Sports Awards \n  \n421.  DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE:  \nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR. S.:  \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE:  \nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE:  \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA:  \n  \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the details of the number of sportspersons who have been \nshortlisted and given Khel Ratna National Sports Awards for the year \n2021 by the Selection Committee;  \n  \n(b) the details of norms formulated/criteria fixed for selection of \nsportspersons for receiving National Sports Awards;  \n  \n(c) whether the Government has also awarded special honours and \ncash award to sportspersons who have brought glory to the country; \n  \n(d) if so, the amount of cash award announced and given to \nsportspersons during each of the last three years and the current year; \n  \n(e) whether the Government is providing any assistance for livelihood \nto sportsperson after their retirement or at elderly age, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n  \n\n(f) the details of facilities provided to sports persons under Khelo India \nProgramme during each of the last three years and the current year? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n  \n(a) Sir, the details of sportspersons who have been given the Major \nDhyan Chand Khel Ratna Award for the year 2021 are:  \n \nS. No. Name of the sportsperson \n(S/Shri) \nDiscipline \n1.   Neeraj Chopra  Athletics \n2.   Ravi Kumar  Wrestling \n3.   Ms Lovlina Borgohain Boxing \n4.   Sreejesh P. R.  Hockey  \n5.   Ms Avani Lekhara Para Shooting \n6.   Sumit Antil Para Athletics  \n7.   Pramod Bhagat Para Badminton  \n8.   Krishna Nagar Para Badminton  \n9.   Manish Narwal Para Shooting  \n10.   Ms Mithali Raj Cricket  \n11.   Sunil Chhetri Football  \n12.   Manpreet Singh Hockey  \n  \n(b): The details of norms formulated/criteria fixed for selection of \nsportspersons for receiving various National Sports Awards are at \nAnnexure–I.  \n(c) and (d): Yes, the Ministry of Youth Affairs & Sports gives cash \nawards to sportspersons for their winning medals in international \n\nsports events in the form of incentive under the ‘Cash Awards to medal \nwinners in international sports events and their coaches’ scheme.  \n  \nThe amount of cash award given to sportspersons during e ach of the \nlast three years and the current year (as on 26.11.2021) is : \nS. No. Year Number of \nsportspersons \ngiven cash award \nTotal amount of \ncash award (in \ncrore Rs.) \n1 2018-19 307 Rs. 24.86 crore \n2 2019-20 599 Rs. 44.96 crore \n3 2020-21 393 Rs. 15.48 crore \n4 2021-22 74 Rs. 16.41 crore \n  \n(e) Yes, sir. Th is Ministry is providing assistance for livelihood to \nsportspersons after their retirement in the form of monthly pension \nunder the scheme ‘Sports Fund for Pension to Meritorious \nSportspersons’.  \n  \nUnder the scheme, sportspersons who are Indian citizens and \nhave won medals in Olympic Games, Commonwealth Games, Asian \nGames and World Cup / World Championship (in Olympic and Asian \nGames disciplines) and Paralympic Games, are given lifelong monthly \npension ranging from Rs. 12,000/ - to 20,000/-, after they attain the age \nof 30 years or retire from active sports, whichever is later. The pension \npayments are made through Life Insurance Corporation(LIC) of India, \nfor which Ministry purchases annuities for ind ividual pensioners by \nmaking onetime lump sum payment to LIC. \n \nAt present, 821 sportspersons are getting lifelong pension under \nthe scheme. \n  \n(f)      Under the Khelo India Scheme implemented by the Sports \nAuthority of India (SAI), there are various verticals which provide \nelaborate facilities to sportspersons: \n(i) The ‘Talent Search and Development’  verticals of the Scheme is for \nidentification and nurturing sporting talent to attain excellence at \ninternational levels.   \n\n(ii) As of now, 2964 Khelo India At hletes (KIAs) selected in 21 \ndisciplines including para sports are being trained at 237 academies \naccredited across the country , under the  ‘Support to N ational / \nRegional / State Sports A cademies’ vertical of the Scheme.  A sum of \nRs.6.28 lakh per annum per athlete is being provided to these \nacademies as assistance. \n(iii) 28 centres in 27 states  / UTs are being upgraded  to international \nstandard with financial support for manpower, sports equipment , \nsports science etc. under the vertical ‘State Level Khelo India Centres’ \nof the Scheme as ‘Khelo India States Centre of Excellence’.  \n(iv) All centres notified as Khelo India Centres are eligible to receive \nRs.5 lakh per discipline as  one time grant and Rs.5 lakh per discipline \nas recurring grant.  As of now, 453 Khelo India Centres across the \ncountry have been established.  \n(v) Khelo India Scheme also promotes rural  / indigenous and tribal \ngames providin"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |422", "t": "Setting -up of FPIs under PMKSY", "head": " \n  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 422 \nANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER 2021 \n \nSETTING-UP OF FPIS UNDER PMKSY \n  \n422. SHRI JUAL ORAM: \n            SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE: \n            SHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \nSHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU: \nSHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT: \nDR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE: \n  \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n    \n(a) whether the Government has invited proposals from the States for setting up of food \nprocessing units/ industries under Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana (PMKSY) \nespecially in Maharashtra and Odisha; \n  \n(b) if so, the details of proposals received so far from various State  Governments and \nEntrepreneurs for setting up of food processing units; \n  \n(c) whether the Government had conducted any study in Maharashtra for setting up of FPIs, \nif so, the details thereof;  \n  \n(d) if not, the steps taken for setting up of FPIs in Maharashtra along with efforts made to \nfacilitate loans and grants to encourage food processing units in Maharashtra? \n  \n(e) whether the Union Government has approved the proposals under thescheme for \nCreation of Infrastructure for Agro -Processing Clusters of PMKSY, if so, the details \nthereof, State-wise; and \n  \n(f) the details of the assistance provided to the Entrepreneurs for setting up ofthe food \nprocessing units during each of the last three years, particularly in Odisha, State-wise? \n    \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a): Government approved a Central Sector umbrella Scheme, ‘Pradhan Mantri Kisan \nSAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY)’ in 2017 to promote setting up, modernisation and capacity \nenhancement of food processing industries in the country. The PMKSY has compon ent \nschemes viz; (i) Mega Food Parks, (ii) Integrated Cold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure, \n(iii) Infrastructure for Agro -Processing Clusters, (iv) Creation of Backward and Forward \nLinkages, (v) Creation/ Expansion of Food Processing & Preservation  Capacities, (vi) Food \nSafety and Quality Assurance Infrastructure, (vii) Human Resources and Institutions, (viii) \nOperation Greens. The PMKSY is not any region specific and is implemented across the \ncountry including in the states of Maharashtra and Odish a. Ministry of Food Processing \nIndustries has invited proposals against the Expression of Interest (EoI) from time to time for \nsetting up of food processing units/ industries in the Country. \n  \n\n \n  \n(b): Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) had been implementing the Scheme \nfor Creation/Expansion of Food Processing and Preservation Capacities (CEFPPC) under \nPMKSY to incentivize the setting up of Food Processing units throughout the country \nincluding Maharashtra and Odisha. Ministry have received total 1351 no. of proposal under \nthe Scheme for Creation/Expansion of Food Processing and Preservation Capacities \n(CEFPPC) under PMKSY for setting up of food processing units from all over the country. \nThe list of State-wise no. of proposals received under scheme is attached as Annexure-I. \n  \n(c) & (d): MoFPI has not conducted any study for setting up of FPIs specific to Maharashtra. \nIn order to meet the credit demand of the food processing sector, the food and agro based \nprocessing units and cold chain infrastructure have been brought under the ambit of Prior ity \nSector Lending (PSL) by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) with effect from 23.04.2015. A \nspecial food processing fund of Rs. 2000 crore has been created with National bank for \nAgriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) in 2014-15 for providing affordable credit to \ndesignated Food Parks and Agro Processing Units located in the designated Food parks. \nFurther, under various schemes of the PMKSY, subsidy is provided to the investors in food \nprocessing sector to enhance their credit worthiness for bank loans t o set up various food \nprocessing industries. \n  \n(e): Ministry has approved 68 agro processing clusters so far under scheme of Creation of \nInfrastructure for Agro -Processing clusters of PMKSY. The State -wise details of approved \nagro processing clusters is attached as Annexure-II. \n  \n(f): The State -wise (includ ing Odisha) details of the assistance provided to the \nEntrepreneurs so far for setting up of the food processing units till date is attached at \nAnnexure-III. \n \n \n*****  \n\n \n  \nAnnexure-I \n  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 422 FOR ANSWER ON 30 TH NOVEMBER, 2021  REGARDING \nSETTING-UP OF FPIs UNDER PMKSY: \n  \nSr. No State Number of proposals received  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 26 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 25 \n3 Assam 51 \n4 Bihar 9 \n5 Chhatisgarh 24 \n6 Goa 2 \n7 Gujarat 104 \n8 Haryana 66 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 45 \n10 Jharkhand 0 \n11 Karnataka 111 \n12 Kerala 44 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 64 \n14 Maharashtra 235 \n15 Manipur 49 \n16 Meghalaya 3 \n17 Mizoram 18 \n18 Nagaland 5 \n19 Odisha 18 \n20 Punjab "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |423", "t": "Cyber Crimes", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 423 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCYBER CRIMES \n \n423.  MS. RAMYA HARIDAS: \nDR. NISHIKANT DUBEY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the cyber  crimes in the country have \nincreased in the past few years; \n \n(b) if so, the number of cases reported during the last five years, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has assessed the effects  of increasing cyber \ncrimes on the economic development of the country; \n \n(d) whether the Union Government has taken any  concrete steps to check such \ncyber crimes in the country; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) to (e): With the enhanced use of cyber space, the number of cyber crimes is \nalso increasing. The National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) compiles and \npublishes statistical data on crimes in its publication “Crime in India”.  The \nlatest published report is  for the year 2020.   As per the data published by \nNational Crime Records Bureau (NCRB), the details of cases, registered under \ncyber crimes, States/UTs wise, during 2016-2020 are at Annexure.  \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 423 FOR 30.11.2021 \n „Police‟ and „Public Order‟ are State subjects as per the Seventh Schedule \nof the Constitution of India. States/UTs are primarily responsible for the \nprevention, detection, investigation and prosecution of crimes including cyber \ncrime through their Law Enforcement Agencies (LEAs) .  The LEAs take legal \naction as per provisions of law against the offenders. To strengthen the \nmechanism to deal with cyber crimes, the Central Government has taken steps \nwhich inter-alia include, spreading awareness about cyber crimes; issuance of \nalerts/advisories; capacity building/training of law enforcement personnel/ \npublic prosecutors/ judicial officers; improving cyber forensic facilities, setting \nup of Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) to provide a framework and \neco-system for LEAs to d eal with the cyber crimes in a comprehensive and \ncoordinated manner; etc.  The Government has launched the National Cyber \nCrime Reporting Portal (www.cybercrime.gov.in), to enable public to report \nincidents pertaining to all types of cyber crimes, with a s pecial focus on cyber \ncrimes against women and children. Citizen Financial Cyber Fraud Reporting \nand Management System has been launched for immediate reporting of \nfinancial frauds and to stop siphoning off funds by the fraudsters.  A toll -free \nnumber 1552 60 has been operationalised to get assistance in lodging online \ncyber complaints.   \n***** \n\n LS.US.Q.NO. 423 FOR 30.11.2021 \n        Annexure \nState/UT-wise Cases Registered (CR) under Cyber Crimes during 2016-2020 \n \n \n \n \nSl.No. State/UT 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 \nCR CR CR CR CR \n1 Andhra Pradesh 616 931 1207 1886 1899 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 4 1 7 8 30 \n3 Assam 696 1120 2022 2231 3530 \n4 Bihar 309 433 374 1050 1512 \n5 Chhattisgarh 90 171 139 175 297 \n6 Goa 31 13 29 15 40 \n7 Gujarat 362 458 702 784 1283 \n8 Haryana 401 504 418 564 656 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 31 56 69 76 98 \n10 Jharkhand 259 720 930 1095 1204 \n11 Karnataka 1101 3174 5839 12020 10741 \n12 Kerala 283 320 340 307 426 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 258 490 740 602 699 \n14 Maharashtra 2380 3604 3511 4967 5496 \n15 Manipur 11 74 29 4 79 \n16 Meghalaya 39 39 74 89 142 \n17 Mizoram 1 10 6 8 13 \n18 Nagaland 2 0 2 2 8 \n19 Odisha 317 824 843 1485 1931 \n20 Punjab 102 176 239 243 378 \n21 Rajasthan 941 1304 1104 1762 1354 \n22 Sikkim 1 1 1 2 0 \n23 Tamil Nadu 144 228 295 385 782 \n24 Telangana 593 1209 1205 2691 5024 \n25 Tripura 8 7 20 20 34 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 2639 4971 6280 11416 11097 \n27 Uttarakhand 62 124 171 100 243 \n28 West Bengal 478 568 335 524 712 \n  TOTAL STATE(S) 12159 21530 26931 44511 49708 \n29 A&N Islands 3 3 7 2 5 \n30 Chandigarh 26 32 30 23 17 \n31 D&N Haveli and Daman & Diu+ 1 1 0 3 3 \n32 Delhi 98 162 189 115 168 \n33 Jammu & Kashmir* 28 63 73 73 120 \n34 Ladakh - - - - 1 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 0 4 4 3 \n36 Puducherry 2 5 14 4 10 \n  TOTAL UT(S) 158 266 317 224 327 \n  TOTAL (ALL INDIA) 12317 21796 27248 44735 50035 \nSource: Crime in India      \nNote : '+' Combined data of erstwhile D&N Haveli UT and Daman & Diu UT for 2016 -2019 \n*' Data of erstwhile Jammu & Kashmir State including Ladakh for 2016-2019 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |424", "t": "Terror Attacks in Manipur", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 424 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nTERROR ATTACKS IN MANIPUR  \n \n424.  SHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA: \nSHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any  cognisance of the recent terror \nattacks in Manipur by  heavily-armed insurgent groups and if so, the details  \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government intelligence reveals any  kind of involvement of \nforeign hand in the insurgent attacks in North-east of the country; and \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a): An extensive operation was conducted in the surrounding areas by Security \nForces to nab the insurgents involved in the recent terror attack on Assam \nRifles convoy on November 13, 2021. Police Station Singhat, District \nChurachandpur (Manipur) had lodged a case  in this regard, Which has been \ntransferred to National Investigation Agency (NIA) for investigation. \n(b)&(c): There is no specific information indicating any kind of involvement of \nforeign hand in the attack. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |425", "t": "Promotion of Agro-forestry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n425\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n30\nth \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nPROMOTION OF AGRO\n-\nFORESTRY\n \n425.\n   \nSHRI \nCHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI\n:\n \n          \nSHRIMATI JASKAUR MEENA:\n \nWill the Minister of \n \nAGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS \n  \nWELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \n \nऔर\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a)\n \n \nthe details of the schemes being implemented by the Government to promote agro forestry \nin the country\n;\n \n(b) \n \nthe details of the States selected for agro forestry;\n \n(c) \n \nthe details of identified areas for their selection under these schemes including Rajasthan;\n \n(d) \n \nthe time by which this scheme is likely to be initiated along with the details thereof;\n \n(e)\n \nthe det\nails of eligible farmers under such scheme;\n \n(f) \n \nthe details of the subsidies, concessions and facilities provided by the Government on ago \nforestry; and\n \n(g) \n \nthe details of the benefits of the agro forestry?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n(a):\n \n   \nSub\n-\nMission on Agroforestry Scheme is being implemented by this Department since \n2016\n-\n17 to encourage tree plantation on farm land \nin a complementary and integrated manner \nwi\nth crops and livestock, \nto help the farmers \nto \nget additional income and make their farming \nsystems more climate resilient and adaptive. \n \n \n(b)&(c):     \nPresently, the scheme is \nbeing \nimplemented in 2\n1\n \nStates viz. Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, \nChhattisgarh, Gujarat, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jharkhand, Ka\nrnataka, Kerala, Madhya \nPradesh\n, Maharashtra, Odisha, Punjab, \nRajasthan\n, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Uttar Pradesh\n, \nMizoram, Manipur, Meghalaya, Nagalan\nd and 2 UT\ns viz. Jammu & Kashmir\n \nand Ladakh\n.\nThe \nextant scheme is being implemented in those\n \nStates/UTs \nthat have liberalized \nfelling and transit \nregulation\ns\n \nfor selected tree species. Other States/UTs can access the benefit of the scheme as \nand when such relaxation \nis notified by them. \n \n\n(d): \n \nThe extant scheme is already ongoing since 2016\n-\n17. \n \n \n(e): \n \nAll the farmers specially\n \nsmall\n \nmarginal\n \nfarmers are eligible for the benefit of the \nscheme.\n \n \n(f): \n \nAssistance to farmers for various interve\nntions under the scheme is\n \nas \nfollowing\n:\n \n \nSl \nNo\n.\n \nComponent/Activities/Sub \nComponent\n \nCost Norms\n \n( Rs in lakh)\n \nPattern of \nAssistance\n \n1.      Nursery Development\n \n \n100% for \nGovernment \nAgencies and 50% \nfor farmers/private \nagencies.\n \n(a)\n \nSmall Nurseries\n \nRs 10 lakh per \nnursery\n \n(b)\n \nBig \nNurseries\n \nRs 16 lakh per \nnursery\n \n(c) \n \nHi \n–\nTech  Nurseries\n \nRs 40 lakh per \nnursery\n \n2.\n \nPeripheral/Boundary Plantation\n \nRs.70 /plant\n \n100% for \nGovernment \nAgencies and 50% \nfor farmers/private \nagencies in four \nyears in the ratio \nof 40:20:20:20\n \n3.\n \nL\now Density \nPlantation (100\n-\n500 \nH\na)\n \nRs 28,000/H\na\n \n4.       High Density Block Plantation\n \n(a)\n \n500   to 1000 plants/Ha\n \nRs 30,000/Ha\n \n(b)\n \n1000   to 1200 plants/Ha\n \nRs 35,000/Ha\n \n(c)\n \n1200   to 1500 plants/Ha\n \nRs 45,000/Ha\n \n(d)\n \n> 1500 plants/Ha\n \nRs 50,000/Ha\n \n5.\n \nCapacity Building and Training\n \nUpto 5% of total allocation to the States\n \n \n(g)\n:\n \nThe Agro\n-\nforestry\n \nscheme\n \nenvisages a lot of quantifiable benefits \n \nlike additional income, protection from \ncrop damage\n, \n \nhigher\n \ncarbon\n \nsequestration along with climate change \n \nadaptation and mitigation. \n \n \n******\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |426", "t": "Sports Infrastructure", "head": "Page 1 of 5 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 \nANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nSports Infrastructure \n \n426. SHRI RAJIV PRATAP RUDY: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the existing sports infrastructure in  the country across all \nsports, State-wise, district-wise including Bihar; \n \n(b) the details and the number of coaches available  in the country, State -\nwise, district-wise and sport-wise including Bihar; \n \n(c) whether the Government plans to expand and  improve existing sports \ninfrastructure under Khelo India  and if so, the details thereof, State -wise \nincluding Bihar  with the estimated cost sanctioned and timelines for  \ncompletion of the projects; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to expand the  scope of sports \ninfrastructure under Corporate Social  Responsibility (CSR) and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and \n \n(e) the details of other steps taken by the  Government to promote Khelo \nIndia initiatives at the rural and local level across the country particularly in \nthe State of Bihar? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{ SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR } \n \n(a) and (b) ‘Sports’ being a State subject, the primary responsibility to \ncreate sports infrast ructure and e ngage sports coaches rests with the \nState/Union Territory Governments. The Union Government supplements \ntheir efforts. The sports infrastructure and coaches in various parts of the \ncountry, including Bihar,  are under the jurisdiction of the respective \nbodies/departments of State/UT Governments. As such, the information \nabout them is not maintained in this Ministry. However, this Ministry has \n\nPage 2 of 5 \n \nsanctioned 267 sports infrastructure projects of various categories in the \ncountry under the Khelo India  Scheme, which includes 11 projects in Bihar , \nas detailed in Annexure – I. Further, the Sports Authority of India  (SAI) has \n918 regular and contractual coaches under its various centres across the \ncountry. Discipline-wise and State-wise details of coaches under SAI are at \nAnnexure – II and Annexure – III, respectively. \n \n(c)  Under the “Utilisation and creation/upgradation of sports infrastructure” \nvertical of the Khelo India Scheme, financial assistance is provided to \nState/UT Governments and other eligible entities for creation/upgradation of \nsports infrastructure of  various categories across the countr y. Khelo India \nScheme is a demand driven scheme  under which projects  are sanctioned \nregularly on the basis of proposals received from various State/UT \nGovernments and other eligible entities  from time to time . The timeline of \ncompletion of projects sanctioned under this Scheme is two to three years.  \nHowever, t he responsibility of executing the projects rests with the \nState/UT Governments.  The State -wise detail s of s ports infrastructure \nsanctioned in the country, including Bihar, under the Khelo India Scheme \nare given at Annexure – I. \n \n(d) The Government encourages the contribution of Corporate Social \nResponsibility (CSR) funds for development of sports infrastr ucture through \nvarious Schemes  of this Ministry . Under the National Sports Development \nFund, which is a Central Sector Scheme, CSR funds are received from public \nas well as private institutions for creation and maintenance of sports \ninfrastructure. So far, Rs. 170 crore of CSR funding has been received from \nvarious organisations. \n \n(e) The Government provides financial assistance for development of sports \nby way of  creation/upgradation of sports infrastructure as well as \nconducting competitions and coaching/training camps under the Khelo India \nScheme across the country throughout the year, including the State of \nBihar. So far, an amount of Rs. 55.49 crore has been sanctioned for creation \nof sports infrastructure in various districts of Bihar  under the Scheme . \nFurther, Rs.  1.16 crore ha ve been released under the vertical “sports for \npeace and development” of the Scheme in Bihar. \n  \n \n\nPage 3 of 5 \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART S (a) , (b) AND (c)  OF LOK \nSABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 ANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \nREGARDING “SPORTS INFRASTRUCTURE” ASKED BY SHRI RAJIV PRATAP \nRUDY, HON’BLE MEMBER OF LOK SABHA \n \nANNEXURE – I \n \nState/UT-wise details of projects sanctioned under Khelo India Scheme \n \nS. \nNo. \nState/UT Amount \nSanctioned \n(In ₹ Cr.) \nAmount \nReleased \n(In ₹ Cr.) \nTotal number of \nprojects \n1 Andaman and Nicobar Islands 5.00 0.00 1 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 29.30 15.68 6 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 157.00 102.90 20 \n4 Assam 43.18 41.68 7 \n5 Bihar 55.49 12.00 11 \n6 Chandigarh 0.00 0.00 0 \n7 Chhattisgarh 21.10 7.03 4 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu 0.00 0.00 0 \n9 Delhi 89.84 35.02 8 \n10 Goa 19.10 2.12 4 \n11 Gujarat 608.37 32.13 5 \n12 Haryana 79.67 46.53 8 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 22.10 3.10 5 \n14 Jammu and Kashmir 28.56 11.57 5 \n15 Jharkhand 2.07 2.07"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |427", "t": "Caste-Based Census", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 427 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCASTE-BASED CENSUS \n \n427.  SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI: \nSHRI MALOOK NAGAR: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has considered  conducting a caste -based census in \n2021-22; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government has formulated any  scheme/policy for caste -based \ncensus in various States including Uttar Pradesh; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(e) the number of caste communities identified across  the country, State -wise; \nand \n \n(f) whether the Governmen t proposes to include  more castes/communities in the \nScheduled Castes category, if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a) to (f): The castes and tribes which are specifically notified as  Scheduled \nCastes (SCs) and Scheduled Tribes (STs) as per the Constitution (Scheduled \nCastes)  Order  1950  and  the   Constitution  (Scheduled Tribes)  Order,   1950, as \n \n\n-2- \nLS.USQ.NO.427 FOR 30.11.2021 \namended from time to time, are enumerated in decadal Census. The Government \nof India has not enumerated caste wise population other than SCs and STs in \nCensus since independence.  The Census Schedule is designed in consultation \nwith various stakeholders including Central Ministries.  The intent of th e \nGovernment for conducting Census 2021 was notified in the Gazette of India on \n28th March, 2019.  However,  due to outbreak of Covid -19, the Census activities \nhave been postponed.  \n \n***** \n            \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |428", "t": "Cases of Cyber Crime related to Financial Fraud", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 428 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCASES OF CYBER CRIME RELATED TO FINANCIAL FRAUD \n \n428. SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether a sharp increase has been reported  in cyber crime related to \nfinancial frauds in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the number of such cases reported during  the last five years in the \ncountry, year-wise along with the type of frauds; \n \n(c) the percentage of such cases and the action taken against the culprits; \n \n(d) the measures and mechanism being adopted by  the Government to curb \nsuch cases; and  \n \n(e) the details and number of fake and fraud cal l centres which were busted \nduring the said period? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) to (e): With the enhanced use of internet, the number of cyber crimes \nincluding financial frauds is also increasing. The National Crime Records \nBureau (NCRB) compiles and publishes statistical data on crimes in its \npublication “Crime in India”. The latest published report is for the year 2020. As \nper  data  published  by  NCRB,   the  details  of cases registered,  action taken  \n \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 428 FOR 30.11.2021 \nagainst the culprits etc., under cyber crimes, States/UTs wise and category \nwise during 2016-2020 are at Annexure. \n„Police‟ and „Public Order* are State subjects as per the Seventh Schedule \nof the Constitution of India. States/UTs are primarily responsible  for the \nprevention, detection, investigation and prosecution of crimes through their \nLaw Enforcement Agencies (LEAs). The LEAs take legal action as per \nprovisions of law against the offenders.  \nTo  strengthen  the  mechanism  to  deal  with  cyber  crimes  in  a \ncomprehensive  and  coordinated  manner,  the  Central  Government  has \ntaken  steps  for  spreading  awareness  about  cyber  crimes;  issuance  of  \nalerts/ advisories; capacity building /training of   law  enforcement personnel/ \nprosecutors/judicial officers; improving cyber forensic facilities; etc. Ministry of \nHome Affairs has established Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) to \nprovide a framework and eco -system for LEAs to deal  with the cyber crimes in \na comprehensive and coordinated manner.  \nThe Government has launched the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal \n(www.cybercrime.gov.in), to enable public to report incidents pertaining to all \ntypes of cyber crimes, with a special focus on cyber crimes against women and \nchildren. Cyber  crime  incidents  reported  on  this  portal, their conversion into  \n\n--3-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 428 FOR 30.11.2021 \nFIRs and subsequent action thereon are handled by the State/UT law \nenforcement agency concerne d as per the provisions of the law. Citizen \nFinancial Cyber Fraud Reporting and Management System has been launched \nfor immediate reporting of financial frauds and to stop siphoning off funds by \nthe fraudsters.  A toll -free number 155260 has been operationa lized to get \nassistance in lodging online cyber complaints. \nThe details and number of fake and fraud call centres are not maintained \ncentrally. \n***** \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \nL.S.USQ.NO.428 FOR 30.11.2021 \nCrime Head-wise Cases Registered (CR), Cases Chargesheeted (CCS), Cases Convicted (CON), Persons Arrested (PAR), Persons Chargesheeted (PCS) and Persons Convicted (PCV) \nunder Cyber Crimes during 2016-2017 \nSL Crime Head 2016 2017 \nCR CCS CON PAR PCS PCV CR CCS CON PAR PCS PCV \n1 Tampering computer source documents 78 32 1 66 43 1 233 54 2 99 72 2 \n2 Computer Related Offences 6818 2018 134 4674 2579 172 10108 2091 50 4169 3319 59 \n3 Cyber Terrorism 12 6 0 7 7 0 13 1 0 11 1 0 \n4 Publication/transmission of obscene / sexually explicit act in electronic form  957 409 10 829 484 12 1768 642 18 1443 1087 25 \n5 Interception or Monitoring or decryption of Information 0 0 0 1 0 0 4 1 1 2 1 1 \n6 Un-authorized access/attempt to  access to protected computer system  0 3 0 3 3 0 2 1 0 1 1 0 \n7 Abetment to Commit Offences - - - - - - 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n8 Attempt to Commit Offences - - - - - - 4 0 0 0 0 0 \n9 Other Sections of IT Act 748 242 14 384 298 17 1503 379 44 658 522 37 \nA Total Offences under I.T. Act 8613 2710 159 5964 3414 202 13635 3169 115 6383 5003 124 \n10 Abetment of Suicide (Online) - - - - - - 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n11 Cyber Stalking/Bullying of Women/Children - - - - - - 542 233 6 397 296 6 \n12 Data theft 86 22 0 34 26 0 307 36 0 155 37 0 \n13 Fraud - - - - - - 3466 614 9 1971 1027 11 \n14 Cheating 2329 355 4 853 569 6 1896 301 2 806 621 2 \n15 Forgery 81 16 0 64 37 0 99 24 0 180 75 0 \n16 Defamation/Morphing - - - - - - 12 4 0 17 17 0 \n17 Fake Profile - - - - - - 86 31 0 90 52 0 \n18 Counterfeiting 10 12 0 17 19 0 1 1 0 9 1 0 \n19 Cyber Blackmailing/Threatening - - - - - - 311 103 2 214 127 2 \n20 Fake News on Social Media - - - - - - 170 51 1 115 85 1 \n21 Other Offences 1012 45"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |429", "t": "Implementation of Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY)", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 429 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nImplementation of Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) \n \n \n429. DR. HEENA GAVIT: \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL: \n DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL: \n  SHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \n  DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \n \n \n        Will the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री sbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of increase in fish production after the implementation of the Pradhan Mantri  \nMatsya SampadaYojana (PMKSY) in line with the objectives of the scheme, State-wise; \n \n(b) the details of employment generated by way of implementation of the scheme as on date, \nState-wise; \n \n(c) the number of fishermen and fish farmers whose incomes have doubled in line with the \nscheme objectives and the details thereof, State-wise;  \n \n(d) the details of potential growth clusters identified for deve lopment under the scheme, \nState-wise, and the criteria employed for such identification; \n \n(e) the details of steps taken to create robust fisheries management and regulatory framework \nin line with the scheme objectives; and \n \n(f) the monitoring mechanism to keep a check on whether the ambitious goals of the scheme \nare being met or not? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n(PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n(a) to (c): The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying \nis implementing a flagship scheme namely “Pradhan MantriMatsyaSampadaYojana \n(PMMSY)” with  highest ever estimate investment of ₹20050 crore in fisheries sector for a \nperiod of 5 years with effect from the financial year 2020 -21. The scheme intends to address \ncritical gaps in fish production and productivity, quality, technology, post-harvest infrastructure \nand management, modernization and strengthening of value chain, traceability, establishing a \nrobust fisheries management framework and fishers’ welfare.The activities supported under the \nPMMSY inter-aliaaim towards increase in employment and enhancement  of income of fishers \nand fish farmers in the country. The state-wise details of beneficiaries covered during the year \n2020-21under Pradhan MantriMatsyaSampadaYojana is furnished at Annexure-I.  \n\n \n \n(d):PMMSYalso focuseson creation of f isheries clusters with robust backward and forward \nlinkages.Some of the major activities identified for development under cluster based \napproachin PMMSY are;  (i) brackish -water aquaculture, (ii) mariculture including seaweed \ncultivation, (iii) development of resource specific deep -sea fisheries, (iv) cold water fisheries, \n(iv) development of aquaculture in saline/alkaline areas and (v) integrat ed development of \nreservoirs.  \n(e):The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying has \ntaken several steps  forrobust fisheries management; they include imposing of uniform fishing \nban in the Indian Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) beyond te rritorial waters in East and West \nCoasts for a period of 61 days annually, ban on destructive fishing methods, d iscouraging \njuvenile fishing,  promotion of river and sea ranching, diversification of fisheries activities, use \nof by-catch and juvenile fish e xcluders, turtle exclusion devices, providing need-based support \nto States/UTs for formulation and implementation of fisheries management pla ns,  promotion \nof deep sea fishing and measures to protect the aquatic ecology. \n(f):  The overall implementation of  the PMMSY is monitored through various institutional \narrangements envisagedfor the purpose under the PMMSY at central and state level ssuch as \nCentral Apex Committee (CAC) , Project Monitoring Unit (PMU) , Project Appraisal \nCommittee, Project Monitoring and Evaluation Unit (PMEU)  and State Level Approval & \nMonitoring Committee (SLAMC) . A dedicated portal and  a Management Information System \n(MIS) have been devised for this purpose.  \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \nStatement referred to in reply to the LokSabha Unstarred QuestionNo. 429put in by Dr. \nHeenaGavit, Shri UnmeshBhaiyyasahebPatil , Dr.SujayRadhakrishnaVikhePatil, Shri \nDhairyasheelSambhajirao Mane , Dr. ShrikantEknathShinde Honourable Members of \nParliament for answer on 30 th November, 2021regarding Implementation of Pradhan \nMantriMatsyaSampadaYojana (PMMSY) \n \n \nSl. \nNo. \nState/UT TotalBeneficiaries under PMMSY \n2020-21 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 9614 \n2 Andaman &Nicobar  123 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 163 \n4 Assam 5662 \n5 Bihar 2809 \n6 Chhattisgarh 10118 \n7 Daman & Diu and Dadra & Nagar Haveli 30 \n8 Delhi  29 \n9 Goa 251 \n10 Gujarat 7516 \n11 Haryana 709 \n12 Himachal Pradesh  954 \n13 Jammu & Kashmir 17941 \n14 Jharkhand 1174 \n15 Karnataka 27636 \n16 Kerala 1010 \n17 Ladakh 60 \n18 Lakshadweep  692 \n19 Madhya Pradesh 16995 \n20 Maharashtra 7580 \n21 Manip"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |43", "t": "Gig Workers", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 43 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29.11.2021 \n \nGIG WORKERS \n \n43.  SHRI PRADYUT BORDOLOI: \n  \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)whether the Government has State -wise data on the number of gig workers \nin India, if no, whether the Government intends to collect such data and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(b)the status of implementation of the Code on Social Security 2020, \nspecifying details of  the provisions that have been implemented as of \nNovember 2021 and details of provisions that are pending implementation; \n(c)the status of implementation of the Social Security Fund and other social \nsecurity measures for gig and platform workers as laid ou t in the Code on \nSocial Security, 2020; and \n(d)whether the Government intends to bring gig workers under the minimum \nwage cover as determined by states or determine a separate, national \nminimum wage for gig workers and if so, details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c): The provisions relating to gig and platform workers have been \nintroduced first time in the Code on Social Security, 2020. ‘Labour’ is in the \nconcurrent list and under the Labour  Codes rules are required to be framed by \nthe Central Government as well as  by the State Governments. The Central \nGovernment is pursuing the State Governments to frame rules under all the \nLabour Codes.  T he Central Government has pre -published the Code on  Social \nSecurity (Central) Rules, 2020 and the Code on Social Security (Employee’s \nCompensation) (Central) Rules, 2021 for public consultations. The   provisions \nrelating to gig workers and platform workers have not come into force. \n \n(d):  The Code on Wage s, 2019 envisages that all workers whether in \norganised sector or unorganised sector will be entitled for minimum wages. It, \ninter-alia, provides for fixation of minimum wages on time work basis for \nvarious wage period, namely on hourly, daily or monthly basis. \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |430", "t": "Natural Calamities in Uttarakhand", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 430 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nNATURAL CALAMITIES IN UTTARAKHAND \n \n430.  SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \nSHRI SUBRAT PATHAK: \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that  Uttarakhand and other parts of the \ncountry recorded high rainfall in the month of October, 2021; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons for the same; \n \n(c) the details of the total number of casualties of  tourists and locals due of this \nuntimely rain and flood; \n \n(d) whether the Government has sent any team to the rain-ravaged areas of these \nStates to assess the  damage caused due to incessant rain and if so, the  details \nand the findings of the team; \n \n(e) whether the Government has declared any  financial assistance to the kith and \nkin of locals and visiting tourists who lost their life and property in the flood; and \n \n(f) whether the Government has announced any  financial package for the State to \ncounter the damage done due to rain and flood and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 430 FOR 30.11.2021 \n(a) to (d): As per information received from India Meteorological Department \n(IMD), interaction between the western disturbance and strong lower level \neasterlies associated over South Madhya Pradesh led to extremely heavy rainfall \nactivity over Uttarakhand and over west Uttar Pradesh on 17 th and 18 th October, \n2021. \nThe State Government of Uttarakhand has reported loss of 72 human lives due to \nabnormal hi gh rainfall between October, 17 and 19, 2021. The Government of \nIndia has constituted an Inter -Ministerial Central Team (IMCT) for on the spot \nassessment of damage due to heavy rains in the month of October, 2021 in the \nstate of Uttarakhand, which visited the State on 22 -24, October,2021. On receipt \nof IMCT report, additional financial assistance is  considered, as per the \nestablished procedure.  \n(e) & (f) : The primary responsibility for disaster management rests with the \nState Governments concerned. The State Governments undertake relief measures \nin the wake of natural disasters from the State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF), \nalready placed at their disposal, in accordance with Government of India’s \napproved items and norms.  Additional financial assistance is provided from \nNational Disaster Response Fund (NDRF), as per laid down procedure in case of \ndisaster of ‘severe nature’, which includes an assessment based on the visit of an  \n\n--3-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 430 FOR 30.11.2021 \nIMCT. The financial  assistance  from  SDRF/NDRF is provided by way of relief and \nnot for compensation of loss suffered/claimed. Distribution of the relief , as per \nGovernment of India approved items and norms of expenditure from SDRF, to the \naffected people on the ground, is the responsibility of the State Government \nconcerned.  \nThe Government of India has already released, in advance, both the installments \nof Central Share of SDRF for the year 2021-22 amounting to Rs. 17,747.20 crore to \nthe all states, including Rs. 749.60 crore, to the State of Uttarakhand.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |431", "t": "e-Governance of Panchayats", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 431 \nANSWERED ON-30.11.2021 \n \nE-GOVERNANCE OF PANCHAYATS \n  \n431. SHRI RAVI KISHAN: \n  \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the extent of implementation of e -governance in the Panchayats across the country, at \npresent;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has provided financial assistance to the States for the purpose and \nif so, the details thereof for the last three years and the current year along with the \nutilisation thereof;  \n \n(c) whether power shortage in rural areas is a major hindrance in the proper working of e -\ngovernance of panchayats;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) the other alternative measures adopted or are likely to be adopted by the Government to \novercome the problem of power shortage in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ  \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a) Under Digital India Programme, Ministry is implementing e -Panchayat Mission Mode \nProject, in the country to revamp the functioning of Panchayats and to make them more \ntransparent, accountable and effective. Ministry launched eGramSwaraj a simplified work -based \nAccounting application to add resses various aspects of Panchayat functioning viz. planning, \naccounting, budgeting including online payments through eGramSwaraj -PFMS Interface for \nservices delivered.  Currently, 2,58,694 Panchayati Raj Institutions have prepared Panchayat \nDevelopment P lans for Financial Year 2021 -22 and 2,75,252 Panchayati Raj Institutions have \nadopted eGramSwaraj for the purpose of accounting.   Further, 2,53,523 Panchayati Raj \nInstitutions have onboarded eGramSwaraj-PFMS Interface for carrying out online transactions.  \n \n\n(b) No direct financial assistant is provided to States / UTs for implementation of e-governance \nin the Panchayats under e-Panchayat Mission Mode Project.  Ministry of Panchayati Raj (MoPR) \nhas been providing programmatic support for strengthening of Pancha yati Raj Institutions (PRIs) \nand capacity building of Elected Representatives and functionaries of PRIs to improve their \nfunctioning and effectiveness and make them self-reliant. \n(c) to (e) Panchayat being a State subject, providing basic infrastructure fa cilities including \ncomputers, electric power supply etc. is primarily the responsibility of concerned State \nGovernment / UT Administration for proper working of e-governance of panchayats. \n \n*** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |432", "t": "Modernization of Agriculture", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICU\nLTURE AND FARMERS WE\nLFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED\n \nQUESTION NO. \n432\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON TH\nE \n30\nTH\n \nNOVEMBER\n, 2021\n \n \nMODERNIZATION OF AGR\nICULTURE\n \n \n432\n. \n \n    \nSHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKH\nIL K\nHADSE:\n \n   \n        \n \nSHRI MANOJ KOTAK:\n \n \n  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes and\n \naims to modernize agriculture and empower \nmarginal\n \nfarmers;\n \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c) whether agricultural exports have set a record in\n \nrecent years;\n \n \n(d\n) if so, the details of the agricultural exports\n \nduring the last five years; and\n \n \n(e) the details of the agricultural commodities which\n \nare in demand in the international \nmarket?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\n\u0000ी\n     \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH \nTOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a)&\n(\nb\n):   \n \nThe Government has taken various steps to\n \nmodernizing agriculture\n \nfor \nsustainable growth in agriculture sector. Some of the interventions in this direction are :\n \n \n \n(\ni\n)\n \n  \nCreation of a\n \nnetwork of 72\n2\n \nKrishi Vig\nyan Kendras (KVKs) for dissemination of \nknowledge and information about modern technology etc.\n \n(\nii\n)\n \n  \nInitiatives under Agriculture Technology Management Agency (ATMA) Scheme like \nExtension Reforms, Mass Media Support to Agricultural Extension, Kisan Call Centr\nes, \nAgri\n-\nClinics and Agri\n-\nBusiness Centres, Exhibitions/ Fairs etc.\n \n(\niii\n)\n \n   \nSub Mission on Agricultural Mechanization (SMAM)\n \n \n(\niv\n)\n \n  \nPromotion of Agricultural Mechanization for In\n-\nSitu Management of Crop Residue in \nthe States of Punjab, Haryana, Uttar Pradesh and NC\nT of Delhi\n \n \n(\nv\n)\n \n  \nNational Agriculture e\n-\nMarket platform (e\n-\nNAM) has been established\n \n \n(\nvi\n)\n \n  \nSoil Health Card (SHC) Scheme\n \n \n(\nvii\n)\n \nPer Drop More Crop (PDMC)\n \n \n(\nviii\n)\n \n \nMission for Integrated Development of Horticulture \n \n\n(\nix\n)\n \n   \nPromotion of 10,000 FPOs.\n \n \n(c)\n&(d)\n \n \nAgriculture exports e\nxcluding allied products\n \n(Marine, Meat etc\n.\n) have set \nrecord in 2020\n-\n21 with\n \n \ntotal agri export of\n \n \nRs. 213513.38 Crore which has been highest \nsince last two decades.\n \nThe export details of principle agriculture commodities in last 5 \nyears is \nannexed\n.\n \n \n \n(e)\n \n \n \nThe top 10 agriculture commodities which are in demand in the international \nmarket are Rice (other than basmati), rice\n-\nbasmati, spices, sugar, cotton, oil\n-\nmeals, castor \noil, fresh fruits, tea and fresh vegetables.\n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \n \nIndia's export of principa\nl agri and allied 2016\n-\n17 and 2020\n-\n21\n \n( Value in Rs Crore)\nS. \nNo.\n \nCommodity\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \n2017\n-\n18\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n1\n \nRICE(OTHER THAN BASMOTI)\n \n16929.88\n23437.23\n21171.17\n14400.32\n35557.03\n2\n \nRICE \n-\nBASMOTI\n \n21512.91\n26870.67\n32804.30\n31026.33\n29847.70\n3\n \nSPICES\n \n19111.25\n20084.91\n23217.77\n25642.04\n29529.39\n4\n \nSUGAR\n \n8659.54\n5225.60\n9523.14\n13981.56\n20668.57\n5\n \nCOTTON RAW INCLD. WASTE\n \n10907.32\n12200.05\n14627.55\n7539.53\n13968.38\n6\n \nOIL MEALS\n \