ION NO. 433 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nNAXALS ENCOUNTER IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n433.  SHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \nSHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether Naxals were killed in an encounter in  Gadchiroli district, \nMaharashtra recently, if so, the details thereof during the last five years and the \ncurrent year, till date and the reasons therefor, State wise; \n \n(b) the number of naxals/police killed, injured in such encounters State-wise; \n \n(c) the number of fake encounters reported/along with the action taken thereon; \nand  \n \n(d) the corrective steps being taken by the  Government to stop such fake \nencounters? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a)& (b): 27 Left Wing Extremists (LWEs), including Milind  Teltumbde, Member, \nCentral Committee of CPI (Maoist), were killed in an exchange of fire (EoF)with \nPolice under the jurisdiction of Police Station Korchi, district Gadchiroli, \nMaharashtra on 13.11.2021. Following the EoF, the Police recovered 29 \nweapons, including AK -47 Rifle –06, Underbarrel Grenade Launcher –01, SLR \nRifle–09, INSAS Rifle–01, and .303 Rifle–03. \n \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 433 FOR 30.11.2021 \nThe number of LWEs killed during the exchange of fire with security \nforces and security forces killed, injured during the last 05 years and the \ncurrent year, State-wise, is given at Annexure I. \n(c)& (d):No case  of fake encounter in the LWE affected States  has been \nreported. \nA strong constitutional and legal mechanism exists,  which include \nNational Human Rights Commission (NHRC) and State Human Rights \nCommission (SHRC), to prevent and deal with the case of fake encounter. NHRC \nhas issued guidelines for conducting magisterial enquiries in case of death of a \nperson in police action. The Hon’ble Supreme Court of India has also laid down  \na detailed procedure to be followed in investigation of a case of death of a \nperson during police encounter for thorough, effective and independent \ninvestigation. These mechanisms and rulings are in addition to the provisions \ngiven in the criminal laws for registration and fair investigation of an offence. \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nLSUQ No. 433 for 30.11.2021 \nSecurity Forces martyred/injured and LWEs killed from 2016 to 15.11.2021 \n    Security Forces martyred \nS.No. State 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 \n(Upto15.11.2021) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n2 Bihar 11 0 1 1 0 0 \n3 Chhattisgarh 11 16 10 4 22 24 \n4 Jharkhand 2 2 3 2 1 1 \n5 Madhya Pradesh 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n6 Maharashtra 0 1 0 0 2 0 \n7 Odisha 1 0 0 1 2 0 \n8 Telangana 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n9 West Bengal 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 Kerala 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n  Total 25 19 14 8 27 25 \n \n    LWEs Killed \nS.No. State 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 \n(Upto15.11.2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 3 2 0 7 0 6 \n2 Bihar 12 7 2 7 5 6 \n3 Chhattisgarh 135 75 125 78 42 37 \n4 Jharkhand 20 12 26 26 13 5 \n5 Madhya Pradesh 0 0 0 2 3 3 \n6 Maharashtra 9 17 52 8 9 49 \n7 Odisha 40 8 19 8 17 6 \n8 Telangana 0 9 1 2 10 1 \n9 West Bengal 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 Kerala 2 0 0 5 1 0 \n  Total 221 130 225 143 100 113 \n \n  \n\nLSUQ No. 433 for 30.11.2021 \n \n  \n  Security Forces injured \nS.No. State 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 \n(Upto15.11.2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1 0 1 1 1 0 \n2 Bihar 8 0 4 1 2 0 \n3 Chhattisgarh 98 78 117 36 85 72 \n4 Jharkhand 16 14 11 25 6 8 \n5 Madhya Pradesh 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n6 Maharashtra 21 40 15 15 9 10 \n7 Odisha 1 21 3 4 4 3 \n8 Telangana 0 0 1 0 1 0 \n9 West Bengal 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 Kerala 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n  Total 145 153 152 82 108 93 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |435", "t": "Job Card Holders under MGNREGS", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 435 \n  ANSWERED ON 30/11/2021 \n \nJOB CARD HOLDERS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n435.  SHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any details of the job card holders under the MGNREGS \nand wages fixed to them, State/UT-wise and gender-wise; \n(b)  whether it is a fact that many workers are being paid less/not being paid fully in some \nStates; \n(c)  if so, the details of such cases reported so far and the action taken so far; and \n(d)  the details of the funds released under this scheme during each of the last three years \nand the current year, State/UT-wise along with the steps being taken to pay the wages \nto card holders timely? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a) to (c):    The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (Mahatma \nGandhi NREGA), 2005, is an Act to provide f or the enhancement of livelihood security of \nthe household in rural areas of the country by providing at least one hundred days of \nguaranteed wage employment in a financial year to every household whose adult members \nvolunteer to do unskilled manual work. States/UTs-wise number of households having Job \ncards is given at Annexure-I.  \n  \nAs per section 6(1) of Mahatma Gandhi NREGA 2005 (Mahatma Gandhi NREGA), the \nCentral Government may, by notification, specify the wage rate for unskilled work for its \nbeneficiaries. Accordingly, the Ministry of Rural Development notifies Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGA wage rate every year for States/UTs. The wage rate is made applicable from  1st \nApril of each financial year.   The State/UT wise wage rates for unskilled workers und er \nMahatma Gandhi NREGA for the Financial Year 2021-22, are at Annexure-II. \n  \nAs per the Para 17 of Schedule -I of the Act, “The State Government shall link the \nwages, without any gender bias, with the quantity of work done and it shall be paid according \nto the rural schedule of rates fixed after time and motion studies for different types of work \nand different seasons and revised periodically”. This is being inbuilt into the design of the \nscheme and being scrupulously observed. \n  \n(d):      State/UT-wise details of Central fund released under Mahatma Gandhi NREGA in last \nthree financial years and current financial year 2021 -22 (as on 25.11.2021) is given at \nAnnexure-III. \n  \n \n\n \nDirect wage payments to the accounts of beneficiaries are being made through National  \nElectronic Fund management System (NeFMS) using DBT protocol. To monitor the progress \nof payment made to wage seekers, a payment dashboard has been development. Also regular \nreview with Stakeholders (Banks, PFMS, and States) is taking place. \nFurther, in o rder to monitor the timely payment of wages, this Ministry reviews the \nprogress of States, via Labour Budget Meeting, Labour Budget Revision Meeting, Mid -Term \nReview Meeting and other interactions with the States/UTs.  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure I \n  \nAnnexure-I referred in reply to parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.435 dated 30.11.2021 \n(Figure in lakh) \nSl. No. States/UTs \nNumber of households having Job \ncards under Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGA (as on 26.11.2021) \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 97.81 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 2.77 \n3 ASSAM 54.89 \n4 BIHAR 189.32 \n5 CHHATTISGARH 40.59 \n6 GOA 0.34 \n7 GUJARAT 42.64 \n8 HARYANA 12.00 \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 13.86 \n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 12.40 \n11 JHARKHAND 59.64 \n12 KARNATAKA 75.25 \n13 KERALA 40.07 \n14 LADAKH 0.37 \n15 MADHYA PRADESH 77.42 \n16 MAHARASHTRA 94.01 \n17 MANIPUR 5.90 \n18 MEGHALAYA 6.24 \n19 MIZORAM 2.09 \n20 NAGALAND 4.49 \n21 ODISHA 76.86 \n22 PUNJAB 19.54 \n23 RAJASTHAN 114.99 \n24 SIKKIM 0.87 \n25 TAMIL NADU 89.74 \n26 TELANGANA 56.61 \n27 TRIPURA 6.52 \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 210.72 \n29 UTTARAKHAND 11.78 \n30 WEST BENGAL 139.95 \n31 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR 0.33 \n32 LAKSHADWEEP 0.08 \n33 PUDUCHERRY 0.73 \n34 \nTHE DADRA AND NAGAR \nHAVELI AND DAMAN AND DIU 0.04 \n  Total 1560.84 \n(as per MIS)  \n \n  \n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure-II \n  \nAnnexure-II referred in reply to parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.435 \ndated 30.11.2021 \n  \n  \nState/UT wise wage rates for unskilled workers under \nMahatma Gandhi NREGA as per Gazette Notification for the \nFY 2021-22 \n  \n No. Name of the States/UTs \nFinancial Year \n2021-22 \n(in Rs.) \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH  245 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH  212 \n3 ASSAM  224 \n4 BIHAR  198 \n5 CHHATTISGARH  193 \n6 GOA 294 \n7 GUJARAT  229 \n8 HARYANA  315 \n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH  - NON SCHEDULED AREA 203 \n9a HIMACHAL PRADESH  - SCHEDULED AREA 254 \n10 JAMMU & KASHMIR  214 \n11 LADAKH 214 \n12 JHARKHAND  198 \n13 KARNATAKA 289 \n14 KERALA  291 \n15 MADHYA PRADESH  193 \n16 MAHARASHTRA  248 \n17 MANIPUR  251 \n18 MEGHALAYA  226 \n19 MIZORAM  233 \n20 NAGALAND  212 \n21 ODISHA 215 \n22 PUNJAB  269 \n23 RAJ"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |436", "t": "Dairy Entrepreneurship Development Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.436 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER 2021 \n \n \nDAIRY ENTREPRENEURSHIP DEVELOPMENT SCHEME  \n \n436:  SHRI KANUMURU RAGHU RAMA KRISHNA RAJU:  \n SHRI Y.S. AVINASH REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING  \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the attention of the Government is drawn to the Dairy Entrepreneurship Development \nScheme aimed at promotion of Entrepreneurship Development and Employment Generation \nProgramme for development of animal husbandry and dairy industry in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the number of entrepreneurs who availed the opportunities in the last three years particularly \nin Andhra Pradesh;  \n(d) the quantum of financial assistance being given under this scheme and the details thereof; and  \n(e) the status of the programme as on date? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) & (b) Dairy Entrepreneurship Development Scheme (DEDS) was implemented by Ministry of \nFisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying, Government of India through National Bank for \nAgriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) from September 2010 to March 2020.  \n(c) & (d)  State-wise number of beneficiaries and back -ended subsidy disbursed  by NABARD \nunder DEDS including in the State of Andhra Pradesh during the last three years are given at \nAnnexure. \n (e) The scheme has been discontinued from April 2020. \n \n**** \n \n  \n\nAnnexure  \nDetails of State wise number of beneficiaries and back-ended subsidy disbursed by \nNABARD under DEDS during last three years (2018-19 to 2020-21) \n  \nS. No. Name of States/UTs No. of \nbeneficiaries \nSubsidy amount \nreleased (Rs in lakh) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 8 2.00 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 5880 3947.57 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 94 146.66 \n4 Assam 1908 1663.19 \n5 Bihar 1622 777.98 \n6 Chandigarh 1 0.47 \n7 Chhattisgarh 109 107.90 \n8 Daman and Diu 0 0.00 \n9 Goa 10 7.19 \n10 Gujarat 3898 5318.69 \n11 Haryana 1135 873.99 \n12 Himachal Pradesh 665 632.10 \n13 Jammu And Kashmir 3324 1834.72 \n14 Jharkhand 713 744.99 \n15 Karnataka 7095 3041.10 \n16 Kerala 1781 973.11 \n17 Lakshdweep 4 1.86 \n18 Madhya Pradesh 3855 2496.48 \n19 Maharashtra 7380 3792.30 \n20 Manipur 509 287.44 \n21 Meghalaya 28 23.51 \n22 Mizoram 255 201.39 \n23 Nagaland 289 271.86 \n24 Odisha 2490 1077.21 \n25 Puducherry 293 52.05 \n26 Punjab 4714 2711.38 \n27 Rajasthan 8811 5629.65 \n28 Sikkim 187 163.15 \n29 Tamil Nadu 9512 2602.66 \n30 Telangana 2979 2452.86 \n31 Tripura 493 288.19 \n32 Uttarakhand 2551 1402.90 \n33 Uttar Pradesh 15505 9566.56 \n34 West Bengal 2518 1993.68 \n Total  90616 55086.76 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |437", "t": "FAME India Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 437 \nANSWERED ON 30.11.2021 \n \nFAME INDIA SCHEME \n \n437. SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \nSHRI MAHENDRA SINGH SOLANKY: \nDR. KRISHNA PAL SINGH YADAV: \nSHRIMATI SANDHYA RAY: \nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA: \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government had assessed the  progress and impact made by the Faster \nAdoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid & Electric Vehicles in India  (FAME-India) Scheme \nin Phase 1; \n \n(b) if so, the findings of the same; \n \n(c) the rationale behind extension of Phase two of  FAME Scheme by two years, till 31 st  \nMarch 2024; \n \n(d) whether the COVID -19 pandemic had hampered  the implementation of the scheme by   \nlowering the demand for electric vehicles; and \n \n(e) if so, the proposed new targets set by the  Government and incentives provided for the \nsame? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n(a) & (b):  Yes Sir, the evaluation of Phase -I of FAME Scheme was done by an independent \nconsultant. The main findings of the validation of outcome report as submitted by consultant \nare given below: \n \n(i) The Scheme has increased awareness which is in itself a notable achievement. \n \n(ii) Overall outcomes of key  parameters of Fuel saving and CO2 reduction are \nsignificantly below the target for FAME;  \n \n(iii) Industry players have been cautious about developing capabilities players have chosen \nto operate adjacent to their core capabilities.  \n \n(iv) Subsidy structure needs to be revised based on the power train technology (to \nincentivize cleaner technologies) and to establish parity across technologies. \n \n \n \nContd….2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \n \n \n(c):  Yes, Sir. The second phase of FAME -India Scheme has been extended for further two \nyears to Achieve the FAME II targets within the fund allocation.  \n \n(d) & (e): Yes, Sir. COVID -19 pandemic disrupted the domestic/ global supply chains and \nthe demand of electric vehicles. \n \nThe phase-II of FAME India Scheme focusses on supporting electr ification of public \n& shared transportation and aims to support, through subsidies, approx. 7000 e -Buses, 5 lakh \ne-3 Wheelers, 55000 e -4 Wheeler Passenger Cars and 10 lakh e -2 Wheelers for a period of 5 \nyears w.e.f. 01st April, 2019 with a total budgetary support of Rs. 10,000 crores.  In addition, \ncreation of charging infrastructure is also supported to address range anxiety among users of \nelectric vehicles. \n \nFurther, the demand incentive under Phase II of FAME India Sche me has been \nincreased to Rs. 15,000/KWh from Rs. 10,000/KWh with an increase in cap from 20% to 40% \nof the cost of vehicle from 11 th June, 2021, thus enabling cost of Electric two wheelers at par \nwith that of ICE two wheeler vehicles. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |438", "t": "Implementation of CAA and NRC", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 438 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30th NOVEMBER, 2021/AGRAHAYANA 9,1943 (SAKA) \n \nIMPLEMENTATION OF CAA AND NRC \n \n438. SHRI HIBI EDEN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the number of persons who have applied for  Indian citizenship during the last \nfive years, year-wise and country-wise; \n \n(b) the number of persons who have been granted  Indian citizenship during the \nsaid period, year-wise; \n \n(c) whe ther the Government has any data showing  the number of Indians \ncurrently living in foreign countries, if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; \n \n(d) the number of Indians who gave up Indian  citizenship for the citizenship of \nanother country during the said period, year-wise; and \n \n(e) whether the Government is considering the  implementation of Citizenship \nAmendment Act (CAA) and National Register of Citizens (NRC), if so, the  progress \nmade in this regard and the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \na) The details are at Annexure – I.  \nb) The details are at Annexure – II.  \n \n \n\n--2-- \nLS.US.Q.NO. 438 FOR 30.11.2021 \nc) As per the information available with the Ministry of External Affairs, a total \nnumber of 1,33,83,718 Indian nationals are living in foreign countries.  \nd) As per the information available with the Ministry of External Affairs, the \ndetails are as follows :-   \n \ne) The Citizenship (Amendment) Act, 2019 (CAA) was notified on 12.12.2019 and \nhas come into force w.e.f. 10.01.2020. The persons covered under the CAA may \napply for citizenship after the rules are notified under the CAA.  \nTill now, the Government has not t aken any decision to prepare National Register \nof Indian Citizens (NRIC) at the national level. \n******* \n  \nYear No. of Indians who gave up Indian Citizenship and \ntook citizenship of other countries \n2017 1,33,049 \n2018 1,34,561 \n2019 1,44,017 \n2020 85,248 \n2021 \n(as on 30.09.2021) \n1,11,287 \n\nANNEXURE – I /1 \nto Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 438 for 30.11.2021 \n \nThe number of persons who have applied for Indian citizenship during the last 5 years, year-wise and \ncountry-wise (as per data generated from online citizenship module) \nCountry 2016 2017 2018 2019 2020 Total \nAfghanistan 152 167 57 364 55 795 \nAlgeria 1 0 0 0 0 1 \nAustralia 3 2 6 9 4 24 \nAustria 0 0 0 2 0 2 \nAzerbaijan 0 0 0 0 1 1 \nBahrain 0 0 0 1 0 1 \nBangladesh 54 46 19 37 28 184 \nBarbados 0 0 0 1 0 1 \nBelarus 0 1 1 0 0 2 \nBelgium 0 1 0 1 0 2 \nBelize 1 0 0 0 3 4 \nBhutan 0 0 1 3 1 5 \nBrazil 0 2 0 2 2 6 \nCanada 7 5 8 16 8 44 \nChile 1 0 0 0 0 1 \nChina 2 2 0 3 2 9 \nColumbia 0 1 0 0 0 1 \nCroatia 0 0 0 1 0 1 \nCzech Republic 1 0 0 0 0 1 \nDenmark 0 0 0 1 0 1 \nEritrea 0 1 0 0 0 1 \nEthiopia 0 0 0 1 1 2 \nFiji