esh; and  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof alongwith  the Corporations, Agencies and organizations engaged/ involved \nin achieving meaningful growth of the Handicrafts Sector in Srikalahasti and weavers from Venkatagiri \nConstituency in Andhra Padesh? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) & (b): Yes Sir, the Office of Development Commissioner (Handicrafts) implements various \nschemes for promotion and development of handicraft sector in the State of Andhra Pradesh including \nSrikalahastiand Venkatagiri constituency. The details of interventions/activiti es like organising \nmarketing events, skill upgradation training, design development workshops, distribution of modern tool \nkits and organising seminar etc by the Office of Development Commissioner (Handicrafts)in Andhra \nPradesh during last five years are as under: \n \nFinancial \nYear \nNo. of intervention \nexecuted \nAmount Released \n(Rs. in lakhs) \nNo. of beneficiary \ncovered directly \n2016-17 36 1619.71 2238 \n2017-18 19 360.10 1901 \n2018-19 14 269.20 9767 \n2019-20 39 184.94 1354 \n2020-21 74 313.89 3775 \nTotal 182 2747.84 19035 \n \nOffice of Development Commissioner (Handlooms) also implements various interventions for \ndevelopment of handloom sector and to promote the marketing channels in domestic as well as \ninternational market.  The details of marketing event / expo organized by the Office of Development \nCommissioner (Handlooms) in Andhra Pradesh during last five years are as under: \n \nFinancial \nYear \nNo. of event events \nexecuted \nAmount Released \n(Rs. In lakh) \nNo. of beneficiary \ncovered (directly and \nindirectly) \n2016-17 5 52.00 11,000 \n2017-18 10 50.49 23,000 \n2018-19 2 41.50 5,000 \n2019-20 7 47.00 15,000 \n2020-21 6 58.40 10,000 \nTotal 30 249.39 64,000 \n\n \n \nFurther, the following major agencies/organisation have  been engaged for the promotion of handicrafts \nand weavers in the State of Andhra Pradesh including Srikalahasti and Venkatagiri constituency: \n \n M/s State Handicrafts Development co-corporation, Vijayawada, Andhra Pradesh. \n M/s Shilparamam Arts, Crafts and Cultural Society, Vijayawada, Andhra Pradesh. \n M/s Society for Weavers’ Welfare in Andhra Pradesh (SWWAP), Ratnagiri, Andhra Pradesh \n District Rural Development Agency, Chittoor, Andhra Pradesh. \n M/s Chittoor Handicrafts Producer Company Limited, Chittoor. \n National Handloom Development Corporation, Greater Noida, Uttar Pradesh.  \n \n*** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |633", "t": "Industrially Backward Districts", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 633. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 1ST DECEMBER, 2021. \n \nINDUSTRIALLY BACKWARD DISTRICTS \n \n633.  SHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                    वाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the Government has identified such  districts/regions in Rajasthan \nwhich are industrially backward; \n \n(b)  if so, the district-wise details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has formulated any policy, investment plan or scheme \nand created friendly environment for setting up industries on the basis of  local \nresources and industrial development of such backward districts of the regions; \nand \n \n(d)  the details of steps taken for industrial  development of backward \ndistricts/regions in Rajasthan? \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्रालय में राज्य मंत्री (श्री सोम प्रकाश)   \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI SOM PARKASH) \n \n(a) & (b):        State Governments decide the Industrially Backward Districts in their \nStates. Government of Rajasthan has informed that they have notified \nthe Rajasthan Investment Promotion Scheme, 2019 (RIPS-2019) in the \nState on 17 th December, 2019 to promote industries and employment \ncreation. The scheme has notified certain areas as “Most Backward & \nBackward Areas” and additional benefits are available for these areas in \nthe Scheme. \n  \nMost Backward and Backward Areas for the purpose of the above \nscheme as notified by the State Government are:- \n  \n1.        Revenue districts (excluding municipal areas as notified under \nRajasthan Municipalities Act, 2009), namely:-  \ni. Banswara \nii. Baran \niii. Barmer \n\niv. Dholpur \nv. Dungarpur \nvi. Jaisalmer \nvii. Jhalawar \nviii. Karauli \n \n2.       Tribal sub plan area as determined by the Tribal Areas \nDevelopment Department, Government of Rajasthan from time \nto time. \n  \n(c) & (d):     Rajasthan Goverment launched Rajasthan Investment Promotion \nScheme 2019 (RIPS-2019) in the State on 17 th December, 2019 and it \nis operative till 31 st March, 2026. The Scheme is applicable to all \nmanufacturing enterprises. The General benefits in the scheme are”- \n        \nS.No. Benefit Provided RIPS-2019 \n1. Investment \nsubsidy for seven \nyears \n75% of SGST \n2. Employment \nsubsidy for seven \nyears \nEmployment Generation subsidy in the \nform of reimbursement of 50% of \nemployer’s contribution towards \nemployees EPF and ESI. \n  \nAnd 75% for – \ni. Employees belonging to Women, \nSchedule Caste (SC), Schedule \nTribe (ST), Person with disability \n(PwD); and \n  \nii. For all employees if the enterprise is \nproviding more than 75% direct \nemployment to persons domiciled in \nRajasthan. \n3. Exemption from \nElectricity Duty \nfor seven years \n100% \n4. Exemption from \nLand Ta x for \nseven years \n100% \n5. Exemption from \nMandi Fee for \nseven years \n100% \n6. Exemption from \npayment of \nStamp Duty  \n100% \n7. Exemption from \npayment of \nconversion \ncharges \n100% \n\n  \nAdditional Benefits applicable to enterprises in Most Backward & \nBackward Areas:  \n  \nAn eligible enterprise, other than a cement manufacturing enterprise, \nmaking investment in a Most Backward & Backward Areas shall be \ngranted the following additional benefits, in addition to the other benefits \nmentioned in other clauses of the scheme. \n \ni. Applicable Investment Subsidy for an additional three years, \nsubject to the maximum of ten years; \nii. Applicable Employment Generation Subsidy for an additional \nthree years, subject to the maximum of ten years; \niii. Applicable Exemption from payment of Electricity Duty for an \nadditional three years, subject to the maximum of ten years; and \niv. 1% additional interest subsidy, if an enterprise is eligible for \ninterest subsidy under the scheme. \nv. Capital Subsidy and Interest Subsidy as per Dr. B.R. Ambedkar \nPackage. \n  \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |634", "t": "FUNDS FOR CCTV", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.634 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \nFUNDS FOR CCTV \n  \n†634. SHRI RAMESH CHANDER KAUSHIK: \n  \n       Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of railway station and other locations identified for \ninstallation of CCTV cameras under Nirbhaya Fund for the safety of \nwomen throughout the country alongwith the State -wise and UT -\nwise details thereof; and  \n(b) the details of the funds all ocated and sanctioned for the said \nproject state-wise and UT-wise?  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND ELECTRONICS & \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) & (b) 983 Railway Stations of Indian Railways have been \nidentified for installation of CCTV cameras under Nirbhaya fund for \nthe safety of women throughout the country.  State -wise/UT-wise \ndetails are appended.  \n \nThe project has been sanctioned at the total cost of ₹ 500 crore & \n₹ 298 crore has been allocated, so far.  \n***** \n  \n\n  \nAPPENDIX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) AND (b) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.634 BY SHRI RAMESH CHANDER \nKAUSHIK TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 01.12.2021 \nREGARDING FUNDS FOR CCTV \n \n(a) and (b) State-wise/UT-wise Railway Stations identified for \ninstallation of CCTV c ameras under Nirbhaya Fund  is as \nfollows:- \nStates No. of Stations \nAndhra Pradesh 59 \nAssam 8 \nBihar 53 \nChattisgarh 13 \nDelhi 4 \nGujarat 34 \nHaryana 16 \nHimachal Pradesh 1 \nJammu & Kashmir 1 \nJharkhand 14 \nKarnataka 32 \nKerala 24 \nMadhya Pradesh 41 \nMaharashtra 128 \nOdisha 19 \nPuducherry 1 \nPunjab 10 \nRajasthan 39 \nTamilnadu 111 \nTelangana 19 \nTripura 1 \nUttar Pradesh 76 \nUttrakhand 9 \nWest Bengal 269 \nTotal 983 \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |635", "t": "Rising Prices of Essential Commodities", "head": " \n \nORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 635 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \n \nRISING PRICES OF ESSENTIAL COMMODITIES \n \n \n635. SHRIMATI NUSRAT JAHAN RUHI:  \n(OIH) \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware about the rising prices in essential commodities in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the details of action the Government is going to take in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k jkT; ea=h  \n(श्री अश्विनी कु मार चौबे) \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (c) :  Government monitors the wholesale and retail prices of 22 essential food commodities on daily \nbasis from the 179 price monitoring centres set up by the State Governments/UT Administrations. The \ndaily prices and price trends are analysed on real-time basis for requisite policy interventions.  \n \nVarious measures are taken from time to time by Government to augment domestic availability and \nstabilize prices of essential food commodities. These steps, inter -alia, include releases from the buffer to \ncool down prices, imposition of stock limits, monitoring of stocks declared by entities to prevent hoarding, \nand changes in trade policy instruments like import duty, import quota, minimum export price and export \nrestrictions.  \n \nTo incentivise farmers for inc reasing production, the Government promulgates appropriate \nMinimum Support Prices and also implements schemes such as Mission for Integrated Development of \nHorticulture (MIDH), National Food Security Mission (NFSM), Operation Greens etc. with the objective  \nof increasing agricultural production and productivity, and boosting agri-infrastructure. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |636", "t": "Gold Ore Reserves", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 636 \nANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n  \nGOLD ORE RESERVES \n   \n†636. DR. SANJAY JAISWAL: \n  \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether it is true that Bihar has one of the highest gold ore reserves in the country \nand if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has conducted primary surveys and exploration of the \ngold block in districts of Bihar including West Champaran; \n(c)      if so, the results and details thereof and if not, the reasons thereof; and \n(d) whether the Government has a timeline regarding the same and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF MINES, COAL AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n \n(a): As per National Mineral Inventory, the total resources of primary gold ore in the \ncountry as on 1.4.2015 are estimated at 501.83 million tonnes with 654.74 tonnes of \ngold metal and out of this, Bihar is endowed with 222.885 million tonnes (44%) of ore \ncontaining 37.6 tonnes of metal. These resources of gold in Bihar are categorized \nunderUnited Nations Framework Classification[UNFC] code -333 (128.885 million \ntonnes containing 21.6 tonnes metal) and UNFC code -334 (94 million tonnes \ncontaining 16 tonnes metal) . The entire resource of gold ore in Bihar is located in \nSono area of Jamui district. \n(b) to (d):  Yes, Sir. During the last five years, Geological Survey of India [GSI], an \nattached office of Ministry of Mines, has carried G4 stage ‘reconnaissance survey' for \ngold in parts of West Champaran district and G3 stage ‘preliminary exploration’ in \nGaya district of Bihar following the guidelines of UNFC. In West Champaran district, \nG4 stage exploration was carried out for placer gold in foothills of Siwalik Himalay as, \nbut the average concentration of placer gold in this area is low (0.0061ppm to 1.96 \nppm) and no resource has been estimated.   \n \nMineral exploration/ assessment is a continuous process and a number of \nexploration agencies like GSI, Atomic Minerals Direc torate for Exploration & \nResearch, State Directorates of Geology and Mining, State/Central Undertakings \nand private sector companies are engaged in exploration of mineral deposits in the \ncountry. For mineral surveys in the country, GSI is the nodal agency to formulate \nmineral exploration programmes of various agencies through its Central Geological \nProgramming Board.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |637", "t": "ROB/RUB in Maharashtra", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.637 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \nROB/RUB IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n†637.   SHRI OMPRAKASH BHUPALSINH alias  \n            PAWAN RAJENIMBALKAR: \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the construction of several railway -over-bridges (ROBs) \nand railway-under-bridge(RUBs) in Maharashtra is still pending;   \n(b) if so, details of these pending rail projects route -wise and \nlocation-wise; \n(c) the main reasons for delay in these rail projects; and \n(d) the time by which these pending rail projects are likely to be \ncompleted alongwith the route-wise details thereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n (a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*****  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.637 BY SHRI OMPRAKASH BHUPALSINH \nalias PAWAN RAJENIMBALKAR TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON \n01.12.2021 REGARDING ROB/RUB IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n(a) to (d):  As on Oct’2021, 158 Road over Bridges (ROBs) and 249 Road \nunder Bridges (RUBs) have been sanctioned by Ministry of Railways in \nMaharashtra. Out of 158 ROBs, Railway portion have been completed \nin 13 and work is in progress in othe r 65 ROBs. Remaining 80 works \nare at planning stage like finalization of alignment and preparation of \nestimate etc. Similarly, out of 249 RUBs, 63 works are in progress. \nRemaining 186 works are at planning stage like finalization of \nalignment and preparation of estimate etc. \n \nSection-wise details are appended.  \nConstruction of ROB/RUBs depends upon the consent of State \nGovernment for closure of LCs and cost sharing, wherever applicable, \navailability of encroachment free land for approach roads, technical \nfeasibility, shifting of utilities, allocation of adequate funds by State \nagencies, public support etc. therefore, it is not feasible to fix \ntimelines for completion of these ROB/RUBs. \n***** \n  \n\nAPPENDIX REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO  (d) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 637 BY SHRI OMPRAKASH BHUPALSINH alias PAWAN \nRAJENIMBALKAR TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 01.12.2021 \nREGARDING ROB/RUB IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n(a)  TO  (d): Section wise details of sanctioned ROBs/RU Bs in \nMaharashtra: \nZonal \nRailway \nSection Nos. \nof \nROBs \nSection Nos. \nof \nRUBs \nWestern Churchgate-Virar 6 Churchgate-Virar 0 \nVirar-Surat 11 Virar-Surat 12 \nUdhna-Jalgaon 4 Udhna-Jalgaon 10 \nCentral Amla-Nagpur 9 Amla-Nagpur 12 \nBadnera -Nagpur 9 Amravati -Narkher 1 \nBadnera-Amravati 2 Badnera-Wardha 12 \nBhusaval-Badnera 4 Bhusawal -Badnera 40 \nBhusaval-Khandwa  3 Bhusawal -\nKhandwal 10 \nChatrapati Shivaji Terminus-\nKalyan  3 Chalisgaon -Dhule 12 \nDaund- Solapur 4 Daund -Solapur 13 \nDiva - Vasai  5 Diva-Panvel 1 \nDiva-Panvel 2 Diwa -Roha 2 \nIgatpuri-Bhusawal 19 Igatpuri -Bhusawal 23 \nJasai -Jawaharlal Nehru \nPort Trust  2 Kalyan-Igatpuri 8 \nKalyan-Igatpuri 1 Kurudwadi -Miraj 1 \nKalyan-Lonavala 8 Lonaval -Pune 1 \nMiraj - Kolhapur  1 Nagpur-Wardha 3 \nMiraj-Hubli 1 Pachora -Jamner 1 \nPanvel-Roha  1 Panvel -Jasai 1 \nPune-Daund 7 Pune-Daund 7 \nPune-Lonavala 7 Pune-Kolhapur 4 \nPune-Miraj 8 Pune-Miraj 9 \nSewagram-Ballharshah 2 Sewagam - 20 \n\nBallarshah \nSolapur-Wadi 2 - - \nWardha-Ballharshah 9 - - \nPanvel-Roha 1    \nKazipet-Ballaharsha 1 Manmad-Mudkhed 12 \nManmad-Mudkhed 4 \nMudkhed-\nPimpilkutti 2 \nAkola - Purna 5 Akola-Purna 3 \nMudkhed-Adilabad-\nPimpilkutti 1 Parli-Parbhani 3 \nSouth \nEast \nCentral \nDurg-Nagpur 16 Durg-Nagpur 26 \nTotal  158  249 \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |638", "t": "Train from Bhavnagar Division", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.638 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \n \nTRAIN FROM BHAVNAGAR DIVISION \n \n†638. DR. BHARATIBEN DHIRUBHAI SHIYAL: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the department of Railways has taken decision to normalize \nits services as earlier;  \n(b) if so, the time by when all the train services under Bhavnagar division \nare likely to be resumed;  \n(c) whether new trains are proposed to be operated from Bhavnagar \ndivision;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) the time by which the said trains are likely to be made operational? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n(a)  to (e) Due to Covid -19 Pandemic, Indian Railways had discontinued \nall regular passenger carrying trains w.e.f. 23.03.2020 and only special \ntrains were operated. Further, Indian Railways is keeping a close watch \non the situation and restoring regular train services in a phased manner \ngradually.  Besides, introduction of trains is an ongoing process on \nIndian Railways subject to operational feasibility, traffic demand, \navailability of resources etc. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |639", "t": "Computerisation of Postal Services", "head": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF POSTS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 639 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nCOMPUTERISATION OF POSTAL SERVICES \n \n639.  SHRI KALYAN BANERJEE:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of the proposal for computerisation of entire Postal services and modernisation \nof present buildings of the country;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government has received proposals for shifti ng of Post offices from its \nexisting decades old and ramshackle buildings to new ones; and  \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a)  The details are as under:- \n \ni. All the 25,363 Departmental Post Offices in the country are computerised. The Postal Circle \nwise list including all States and UTs is enclosed as Annexure-I. The Department has also \nupgraded 1,29,157 Branch Post Offices in the rural/backward areas of the country by providing \nSubscriber Identification Module (SIM) based Hand Held Point of Sale Devices,  for carrying \nout online postal and financial services. The Postal Circle wise details including all States and \nUTs is enclosed as Annexure-II. \nii. A central server based integrated, modular and scalable solution for all the operations of \nDepartment of Posts including Banking, Insurance, Mails, Human Resources, and Finance & \nAccounts has been provided. \niii. There is a provision for “Facelifting of Postal Buildings and Assets” in the Scheme of “Estates \nManagement” under which the postal buildings are modernized by carrying out activities like \nimproving the look and feel of the buildings, installation of Solar Power Panels, construction \nof Rain Water harvesting Structures etc. \n \n(b) & (c)  Yes, Sir. Such proposals are received from time to time. Maintenance and renovation \nof postal buildings is a continual process. Routine maintenance of the postal buildings is carried out \nperiodically and ma jor repairs and renovation are carried out as and when required under relevant \nbudgetary provisions. Further, when a postal building outlives its life or its condition deteriorates to \nsuch an extent that it is beyond repairs even before its life or it is d eclared dilapidated/unsafe by either \nPostal Civil Wing or concerned Municipal Authority, the Post Office is shifted to a suitable new \naccommodation. \n\n2 \n \n \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nPostal Circle wise (including all States /UTs) number of Departmental Post Offices computerised in \nthe country (as on 31.3.2021)  \n \nS.No. Name of Postal Circle \nTotal \nDepartmental \nPost Offices \nTotal Departmental \nPost Offices \n(Computerised) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1570 1570 \n2 Assam 623 623 \n3 Bihar 1069 1069 \n4 Chhattisgarh 352 352 \n5 Delhi 379 379 \n6 Gujarat (including UT - Dadra and \nNagar Haveli and Daman & Diu) 1213 1213 \n7 Haryana 506 506 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 471 471 \n9 Jammu & Kashmir (including UT - J&K \nand UT- Ladakh) 267 267 \n10 Jharkhand 467 467 \n11 Karnataka 1695 1695 \n12 Kerala (including UT- Lakshadweep) 1509 1509 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 1014 1014 \n14 Maharashtra (including Goa) 2214 2214 \n15 \nNorth-East (including Arunachal \nPradesh, Manipur, Meghalaya, Tripura, \nMizoram and Nagaland) \n341 341 \n16 Odisha 1209 1209 \n17 Punjab (including UT-Chandigarh) 768 768 \n18 Rajasthan 1313 1313 \n19 Tamil Nadu (including UT-Puducherry) 2838 2838 \n20 Telangana 827 827 \n21 Uttar Pradesh 2552 2552 \n22 Uttarakhand 395 395 \n23 West Bengal (including Sikkim and UT-\nAndaman and Nicobar Islands) 1771 1771 \n  Grand Total 25363 25363 \n  \n\n3 \n \n \n \nAnnexure-II \n \nPostal Circle wise (including all States/UTs) details of Branch Offices modernized under Rural \nICT \n \nS. \nNo. Name of Postal Circle Total Branch \nOffices \nTotal number of \nBranch Offices \nrolled out under \nRural ICT \n1 Andhra Pradesh 9025 8945 \n2 Assam 3383 3374 \n3 Bihar 8040 7942 \n4 Chhattisgarh 3556 3040 \n5 Delhi 132 68 \n6 Gujarat (including UT - Dadra and Nagar \nHaveli and Daman & Diu) 7633 7595 \n7 Haryana 2190 2175 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 2324 2314 \n9 Jammu & Kashmir (including UT - J&K \nand UT- Ladakh) 1433 1427 \n10 Jharkhand 3312 2715 \n11 Karnataka 7929 7898 \n12 Kerala (including UT- Lakshadweep) 3554 3540 \n13 Madhya Pradesh 7264 7216 \n14 Maharashtra (including Goa) 10785 10573 \n15 North-East (including Arunachal Pradesh, \nManipur, Meghalaya, Tripura, Mizoram \nand Nagaland) \n2571 2510 \n16 Odisha 7049 7035 \n17 Punjab (including UT-Chandigarh) 3100 3081 \n18 Rajasthan 8975 8943 \n19 Tamil Nadu (including UT-Puducherry) 9302 9211 \n20 Telangana 4967 4948 \n21 Uttar Pradesh 15114 15034 \n22 Uttarakhand 2327 2310 \n23 West Bengal (including Sikkim and UT-\nAndaman and Nicobar Islands) 7308 7263 \n Grand Total 131273 129157 \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |64", "t": "North East Rural Livelihood Projectg", "head": " \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Development of North Eastern Region \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.  64 \n \nTo be answered on \nMonday, November 29, 2021/Agrahayana 8, 1943(Saka) \n \nQUESTION \nNorth East Rural Livelihood Project \n \n64. SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \n Will the Minister of DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION be pleased \nto state: \n(a)  the details of progress made under North East Rural Livelihood Project (NERLP) \nassisted by World Bank; \n(b)  the number of districts covered under the above said scheme, State-wise; and \n(c)   the details of schemes currently under implementation in Barpeta, Nalbari and \nBongaigaon districts of Assam?   \n \nANSWER \n The Minister of the  Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region \n [Shri G. Kishan Reddy] \n \n(a) and (b)   North East Rural Livelihood Project (NERL P)  was implemented from \n2012 till September, 2019. It was implemented  in 1,645 villages under 58 development \nblocks across 11 districts of 4 States of Mizoram (Aizawl and Lunglei districts), Nagaland \n(Peren and Tuensang districts), Sikkim (South Sikkim and West Sikkim districts) and Tripura \n(West Tripura , North Tripura , Sepahijala, Khowai and Unakoti districts) . The major \nachievements of the project are as under: \n \n\n \ni. 10,462 boys and girls were trained in various job-skills; \nii. 2,92,889 households were covered through formation of 28,154 Self-Help \nGroups (SHGs) and 1212 Village Federations and formation of 1599 Community \nDevelopment Groups (CDGs);  \niii. The project released a Community Investment Fund (CIF) of Rs.319.15 crores to \n28,154 SHGs; \niv. A total number of 5535 SHGs availed bank linkage with a total sanctioned bank \nloan amount of Rs.58.19 crores; and  \nv. 97% of the members of SHGs formed under the project had saving -bank \naccounts with cumulative savings of Rs.60.51 crores. \n \n(c) The Ministry of Development of North Eastern Region is implementing the Non-\nLapsable Central Pool  of Resources (NLCPR) Scheme;  North East Special \nInfrastructure Development Scheme (NESIDS) ; Schemes of NEC (North Eastern \nCouncil) and North East Road Sector Development Schem e (NERSDS) in all the eight \nStates of North Eastern region (including all districts of Assam)  under which specific \nprojects that are prioritised by the State Governments are being implemented.  \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |640", "t": "Criteria for Local Trains", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 640 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \nCRITERIA FOR LOCAL TRAINS \n \n640.   SHRI ARVIND GANPAT SAWANT: \n    \n    Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a)   the details of definition of local railway in urban areas like Mumbai \nand the criteria of distance and time to t ravel required for such trains  \nto be considered as a local train; \n \n(b)   whether the local train from Churchgate to Dahanu or Chhatrapati \nShivaji Maharaj Terminus to Kasara can be treated as local train which \nconsumes nearly a travel time of two and half to three hours; \n \n(c) if so, whether there is a demand for toilet facility for the ladies \ncompartment for such a long distance local train and the remedial  \naction taken by Railways in this regard; and \n \n(d) whether the Ministry had assured for an Elevated train on CSTM to \nPanvel on harbour line and if so, the present status of this project? \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND ELECTRONICS \n& INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a): Ministry of Railway’s consideration for notification as sub -urban \nroutes like Mumbai etc. is based on the pattern of traffic, availability \nof rail infrastructure and the notification issued by State Gove rnments \nfrom time to time identifying areas as suburban in their master plan \netc. As on date, the current list of areas already notified as sub -urban \nincludes Mumbai, Chennai, Kolkata and Secunderabad. \n(b): Yes, Sir. As on date Churchgate to Dahanu & Chhatrapati Shivaji \nMaharaj Terminus to Kasara are part of notified suburban sections. \n\n(c): Local trains are supposed to be covered for short distance in \nsuburban area. In such area toilets of ladies or/and gents have been \nprovided in several stations. Pas sengers are supposed to avail facility \nof toilets at stations. \n(d): No, Sir. The work of Fast elevated corridor between CSMT -Panvel \nalongwith other suburban corridors was proposed under MUTP 3A \nProject. Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) while \nconveying its approval in March 2019, has dropped the work of CSMT -\nPanvel Corridor under MUTP 3A Project. \n \n***** \n \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |641", "t": "Manual of Parliamentary Procedures", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED DIARY NO. – 641 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \nManual of Parliamentary Procedures \n \n641   Shri Benny Behanan \n \nWill the Minister of Parliamentary Affairs be pleased to state: \n \na. Whether the Ministry has received an Office Memorandum from the Ministry of Law \nand Justice dated February 17, 2014 requesting the Ministry to suitably amend the \nManual of Parliamentary Procedures in the Government of India to incorporate public \nconsultation procedures, and if so, the details thereof; \n \nb. Whether the Ministry has amended the Manual of Parliamentary Procedures in the \nGovernment of India in accordance with this memorandum; \n \nc. if so, the details there of and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \nd. whether the Ministry has implemented the provisions for public consultation as \nrequired under Pre Legislative Consultation Policy, 2014; and \n \ne. if so, the details there of and if not the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS AND \nMINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF CULTURE \n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n \n(a), (b) and (c) Yes, the Ministry had received an Office Memorandum No. 11(35)/2013-L1 \ndated 17.02.2014 from Legislative Department (Ministry of Law and Justice) regarding \nissues related to Pre-legislative Consultative Policy (PLCP) with request to take necessary \naction on the Manual for Parliamentary Procedures in the Government of India, to the \nextent required, for suitable modification. Accordingly, the initiatives were taken for  \nmodification of the Manual of Parliamentary Procedures in the Government of India. After \nreceiving suggestions from all Ministries/Departments and both the Secretariats of the \nParliament, the modified 4th Edition of the Manual of Parliamentary Procedures in the  \n\nGovernment of India was published in 2019, in consultation with Ministry of Law and \nJustice which is available on the Ministry’s Website i.e. www.mpa.gov.in. Provisions \nrelated to Pre-Legislative Consultation are given in Para 9.2 (a) to 9.2(d) of the Manual of \nParliamentary Procedures of the Government of India. \n \n(d) and (e)  Yes, Provisions regarding pre -publication of Rules etc. has also been \nincorporated in the Manual at Para 11.2 of the Manual of Parliament ary Procedures of the \nGovernment of India. \n \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |642", "t": "Steel Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STEEL \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.642 \nFOR ANSWER ON 01/12/2021 \n \nSTEEL PRODUCTION \n \n642. SHRI NIHAL CHAND: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the State-wise details of the percentage/ratio of  total production and demand \nof steel in the country during the last two years; \n(b) the details of the names of the countries from  which India imports steel \nincluding the percentage thereof  during the last two years along with the percentage \nit amounts to in terms of the total requirement of the country; and \n(c) the details of the comprehensive steps taken by  the Government to increase \nproduction of steel in the country? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STEEL     (SHRI RAM CHANDRA PRASAD SINGH) \n \n(a)  State-wise details of the percentage of total production and demand of steel in \nthe country during the last two years are given at Annexure-I and Annexure-II \nrespectively. \n \n(b) The country-wise details of imports of finished steel during the last two years  \nalong with percentage thereof is given at Annexure-III. \n \n(c) The Government has taken the following steps to increase prod uction of steel \nin the country:- \n \n(1) Notification of National Steel Policy 2017 which envisages inter -alia \ndomestically meeting the enti re demand of steel and high -grade automotive \nsteel, electrical steel, special steel and alloys for strategic applications \n(2) Domestically Manufactured Iron & Steel products (DMI&SP) Policy for \npromoting procurement of Made in India steel by Government agencies.  \n(3) Notification of Steel Scrap Policy to enhance the availability of domestically \ngenerated scrap.  \n(4) Issuance of Steel Quality Control Order to stop manufacturing and import of \nnon-standardized steel.  \n(5) Notification of Steel Import Monitoring System (SIMS) for advanced \nregistration of steel imports.  \n(6) Notification of Production Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme for Specialty Steel \nwith an outlay of Rs 6,322 crores.  \n(7) Engagement with various stakeholders including industry association and \nleaders of domestic steel industry to address their issues by the concerned \nMinistries/Departments of the Central Government and State Governments.  \n(8)  Engagement with relevant stakeholders including from Railways, Defence, \nPetroleum and Natural Gas, Housing, Civil Aviation, Road  Transport and \nHighways, Agriculture and Rural Development sectors to enhance the steel \nusage and overall demand for steel in the country. \n(9)  Establishment of Project Development Cell in the Ministry to attract and \nfacilitate investment in the steel sector. \n \n******** \n  \n\n   \n \nAnnexure-I \n \nState-wise details of percentage of total Finished Steel Production \nduring last two years: \n \n \n \nState \n2019-20 2020-21  Production \n('000t) \n% \nShare \nProduction \n('000t) \n% \nShare