 664. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 1ST DECEMBER, 2021. \n \nVCIC \n \n664.  SHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \nDR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \nSHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY: \nSHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY: \nSHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                     वाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the Government has identified 3 priority nodes of Chittoor-Srikalahasti, \nKadapa and  Vishakhapatnam under the Visakhapatnam -Chennai Industrial \ncoridor (VCIC); \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the status of the progress of these priority nodes? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्रालय में राज्य मंत्री (श्री सोम प्रकाश)   \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI SOM PARKASH) \n \n(a) to (c): The State Government of Andhra Pradesh has identified three priority \nnodes namely, Chittoor (Srikalahasti), Visakhapatnam and Kadapa   for \ndevelopment under Visakhapatnam -Chennai Industrial corridor(VCIC) \nthe details thereof are as under: \n \n1. Kadapa/Kopparthy node  – Project development activities including \nMarket demand assessment, Site survey, prep aration of base map, \ntechnical assessment & land suitability and   preparation of \npreliminary master plan has been completed by the consultant. \n \n2. Chittoor node – Consultant  appointed for preparation of detailed \nmaster plan, market demand assessment and preliminary \nengineering activities.  \n \n3. Vishakhapatnam node  - Visakhapatnam node is being developed \nby the State Government of Andhra Pradesh on its own.  \n   \n \n******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |665", "t": "Hi Speed Internet", "head": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 665 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nHI SPEED INTERNET \n \n†665.  SHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL:  \nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA:  \nSHRI JASWANT SINGH BHABHOR:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of the efforts being made by the Government to make India as a largest \nconnected nation in the world;  \n \n(b)  the details of the efforts being made to accelerate the work of providing internet facility \nin those States and districts across the country which have less or no access to internet facilities;  \n \n(c)  the details of the measures being taken by the Governme nt to provide hi -speed internet \nservices in the rural and tribal areas of the State of Gujarat; and  \n \n(d)  the details of the progress made under the village broadband connectivity scheme? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) to (d)  Internet connectivity  is provided in the country by the Government and the \nTelecom Service Providers (TSPs) through wireless mobile and fixed wireline connections in \nphased manner. As per data provided by Telecom Service Providers and field units of \nDepartment of Telecommunications, it is estimated that out of 5,97,618 inhabited villages (as per \nCensus 2011) in the country, mobile wireless coverage has been provided in 5,72,551 villages \nincluding 17,331 villages in Gujarat.  \n Further, in its endeavour to provide high speed inte rnet/broadband connectivity, BharatNet \nproject is envisaged in all the Gram Panchayats (GPs) and villages of the country including rural \nand tribal areas of Gujarat. As on 01.11.2021, a total of 1,66,088 Gram Panchayats have been \nmade service ready with broadband infrastructure under BharatNet including 13,888 in Gujarat. \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |666", "t": "Digital Mapping of the Country", "head": "Page 1 of 3 \n \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SCIENCE & TECHNOLOGY \nDEPARTMENT OF SCIENCE & TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 666 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01/12/2021 \n  \nDIGITAL MAPPING OF THE COUNTRY \n  \n666.  SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \n         SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \n         SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \n         SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \n         SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n  \nWill the Minister of SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY ǒव£ान और Ĥौƭोिगकȧ \nमंğी   be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Department of Science and Technology has taken up \nthe task of digital mapping of the entire country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the present status of the project \nfor digital mapping of the entire country by the Survey of India; \n \n(c) the details of funds allocated for the purpose; \n \n(d) the time by which the remaining States are likely to be covered; and \n \n(e) the time by which the project is likely to be completed? \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF \nSCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY AND EARTH SCIENCES \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH) \nǒव£ान और Ĥौƭोिगकȧ तथा पृØवी ǒव£ान मंğालय क े  राÏय मंğी (èवतंğ Ĥभार) \n  (डॉ. ǔजतɅġ िसंह) \n  \n\nPage 2 of 3 \n \n(a) Yes, Sir. \n  \n(b) Survey of India (SoI) is not having any single project to map entire country. \nHowever, it is Department’s endeavor to map entire country with Departmental \nmapping & under various projects taken together. The Present status of the project \nof digitally mapping of entire country by Survey of India is given below: \nI. Departmental Activity to Map entire country [Total Area = 32.87 lac \nSq.km approx. SoI Area of Responsibility (AoR) = 31.67 lac Sq.km \napprox.; General Staff Geographical Section (GSGS) (Military Survey) \nAoR = 1.2 lac Sq.km] \n \n1. Using High Resolution Satellite Imagery (HRSI): Work involves 05 \nstages as under: \nControl Work completed                                               ≈ 227804 Sq.Km \nGeo-referencing completed                                          ≈ 218249 Sq.km \nFeature Extraction completed                                       ≈139973 Sq.km \nGround Validation completed                                        ≈ 11908 Sq.km \nFinal National Topographic Data Base(NTDB)              ≈ 5453 Sq.km \n \nII. Projects: \n \n1. National Hydrology Project (NHP): [Total Area = 8.0 Lac Sq.km] \nUsing High Resolution Satellite Imagery: Work involves 05 stages \nfor which status is given as under: \nControl Work completed                                          ≈ 698457 Sq.Km \nGeo-referencing completed                                     ≈ 696070 Sq.km \nFeature Extraction completed                                  ≈ 329300 Sq.km \nGround Validation completed                                   ≈ 17328 Sq.km \nFinal NTDB                                                               ≈ 0 Sq.km \n  \n2. Using UAV/Drones: Work involves 04 stages for which status is \ngiven as under: \n \nData Acquired using Drone                                         ≈26668 Sq.km \nData Processing                                                          ≈21342 Sq.km \nFeature Extraction                                                       ≈8198 Sq.km \nGround Validation                                                        ≈2929 Sq.km \n3. Using UAV/Drones [for Village Abadi areas only] \nData Acquired using Drone                                            ≈75644 Villages \nData Processing                                                             ≈67403 Villages \nFeature Extraction                                                          ≈62648 Villages \nFinal Map                                                                       ≈7818 Villages \n\nPage 3 of 3 \n \nIII. The details of States in which the project has started are as below: \n1. (i). Departmental Activity to Map entire country using HRSI \n– All States / UTs. \n2. (i). In the States/UT of J&K, HP, Punjab, Uttarakhand, UP, \nBihar, West Bengal, Assam, Odisha, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat & Arunachal \nPradesh. \n2. (ii). Large Scale Mapping project is being carried out in the \nStates of Haryana, Karnataka & Andhra Pradesh. \n2. (iii). SVAMITVA Project is being carried out in the States of \nHaryana, Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Madhya Pradesh, Karnataka, \nMaharashtra, Punjab, Rajasthan and Andhra Pradesh. [Mapping of Village \nAbadi areas only under SVAMITVA project]: Working is under progress and \nMoUs have been signed for Chhattisgarh, Gujrat, Kerala, Odisha, Jharkhand, \nArunanchal Pradesh, Ladakh(UT), Himachal Pradesh, Dadar & Nagar Haveli \n(UT), J&K (UT), Manipur, Assam, Mizoram, Tripura, Puducherry (UT), \nAndaman & Nicobar (UT) and Lakshadweep (UT). \n(c) The details of funds allocated for the purpose: \nProject Total Cost Fund allotted so far \nNational Hydrology Project      300 Crore 141.05 Crore \nNational Mission for Clean Ganga (NMCG)         86.64 Crore 41.00 Crore \nLarge Scale Mapping of Haryana 150 Crore 19.90 Crore \nLarge Scale Mapping of Karnataka          76 Crore 22.00 Crore \nL"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |667", "t": "Mega Textile Parks", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 667 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \n \nMEGA TEXTILE PARKS \n \n \n667.   SHRI RAJAN VICHARE:  \n          SHRI BRIJENDRA SINGH:  \n          SHRI ANURAG SHARMA:  \n          SHRI NIHAL CHAND:  \n          SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.:  \n          SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI:  \n          SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR:  \n          SHRI GAUTHAM SIGAMANI PON:  \n          SHRI SELVAM G.:  \n          SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR: \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government has set up seven Mega Textile Parks under the PM -MITRA scheme and if \nso, details of the main objectives and salient features alongwith the financial allocation and targets set \nfor this purpose;  \n \n(b) whether the plan entails stablishing these parks over a period of 5 years and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether places have been selected in the country including from Haryana and Maharashtra and if so, \nthe details of the locations thereof;  \n \n(d) the number of States which have expressed interest in setting up of these Mega Textile Parks;  \n \n(e) the names of areas across the country which are likely to be given priority and the names of products \nbeing given priority for export under the said scheme;  \n \n(f) the manner in which the scheme will boost employment generation in the next three years alongwith \nthe number of direct and indirect jobs that are to be generated under these parks;  \n \n(g) whether the parks would be developed by a Special Purpose Vehicle  owned by the Central and State \nGovernments and would get two kinds of support and if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(h)the other steps taken by the Government to develop world class industrial infrastructure and to boost \nFDI and local investment in the Textile Sector?  \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH)  \n(a) to (g): With a view to attract investment, boost employment generation and position itself \nstrongly in the global textile market, the Government has approved setting up of 7 (Seven) PM Mega \nIntegrated Textile Region and Apparel (PM MITRA) Parks in Greenfield/Brown field sites including \nplug and play facility with an outlay of Rs. 4445 cr for a period of seven years upto 2027-28.   \n \n\n \nThe salient features are:-  (i) Willing state governments to have ready availability of contiguous \nand encumbrance-free land parcel of 1000+ acres for being eligible. (ii) It is envisaged to be on Public \nPrivate Partnership (PPP) mode.  (iii) there is a provision of Development Capital Support (DCS) \n@30% of the project cost with a maximum support of ₹ 500 Cr and ₹200 Crore per park for Gr eenfield \nand Brownfield PM MITRA Park respectively. The DCS is for creation of core Infrastructure. (iv) \nThere is provision of Rs. 300 Cr. per park for incentivizing the industries to set up their units in the \npark. There is a provision to use 10% of the park’s area for Commercial Development (CD) and revenue \nstream from CD may help maintenance of common assets and facilities.   \nThe selection of sites will be by way of challenge method with weightage of parameters for \nexample- connectivity, power infrastruc ture, water and waste water disposal system, Industry Friendly \nlabour laws, Single Window Clearances, Stable and Conducive industrial/textile policy of the state.  \nStates like Tamil Nadu, Punjab, Odisha, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Rajasthan, Assam, Karnataka, \nMadhya Pradesh and Telangana have expressed interest so far.  The Scheme shall be implemented on \npan-India basis and is intended for holistic development of textile sector. However, emphasis on \nTechnical Textiles, textile machinery and man -made fibre wil l be given. Once completed, the Mega \nTextile Park is intended to generate around 1 Lakh direct and 2 lakh indirect employment per park. The \nproject will be led and owned by Special Purpose Vehicle created for this purpose for each PM MITRA \nPark.  \n(h): In order to develop world class industrial infrastructure and to boost FDI and local investment in \nthe Textile Sector, the government has taken following major initiatives are (i) Production Linked \nIncentive (PLI) Scheme for textile Sector, (ii) National Techn ical Textiles Mission; (iii) Scheme for \nIntegrated Textiles Parks; (iv) Integrated Processing Development Scheme; (v) PowerTex India; (vi) \nComprehensive Powerloom Cluster Development Scheme, etc. \n*** \n  \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |668", "t": "Establishment of New industries", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 668. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 1ST DECEMBER, 2021. \n \nESTABLISHMENT OF NEW INDUSTRIES \n \n668.  SHRI DHARAMBIR SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                   वाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the Government is encouraging the State Governments for setting up \nof new industries through various policy measures; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of the incentives given to various State Governments including \nthe State of Haryana during the last three years and the current financial year; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has received any proposal from the State Government \nof Haryana regarding setting up of new industries during the last three years;  \nand \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्रालय में राज्य मंत्री (श्री सोम प्रकाश)   \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI SOM PARKASH) \n \n(a) to (d): Central Government has adopted a wide range of measures to \npromote and facilitate industrial development across States/UTs. \n \nFor creation and facilitation of business, DPIIT implements a large \nnumber of schemes such  as  reforms in Foreign Direct Investment, \nEase of Doing Business, Reduction of Compliance Burden on \nbusinesses and citizens, setting up of Empowered Group of \nSecretaries (EGoS), Project Development Cells, Production Linked \nIncentive Schemes, Single Window  Investment Clearance Cell (ICC), \nOne District One Product (ODOP), Project Monitoring Group, setting \nup of India Industrial Land Bank (IILB), Industrial Park Rating \nSystem(IPRS) and promoting Intellectual Property Rights.  \n \nFor creation of World class infr astructure, DPIIT implements the PM \nGati Shakti National Master Plan (NMP), National Industrial Corridor \nProgrammes. For promotion of investment, DPIIT implements Scheme \nfor Startups, reimbursement of GST in North Eastern Region and \nHimalayan States. Handholding measures have also been put in place \nfor fast recovery of industrial activity in post COVID period.  \n  \n*******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |669", "t": "Closing of LC-249-C", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.669 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \nCLOSING OF LC-249-C \n \n†669. SHRI ASHOK KUMAR RAWAT: \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has received letter No. 1261/XX/JA -\n2019-20, dated 19.10.2019 by the District Magistrate, Hardoi (UP) \nregarding having no admistrative objection in closing railway level \ncrossing No. 249-C near St. Theresa’s School in Sandila Tehsil, Misrikh \nParliamentary Constituency after the construction of ROB there;   \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has received requests from public \nrepresentatives as well in this regard; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the progress made so far in this regard? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (e): Yes, Sir.  No Objection Certification (NOC) has been received \nfrom the Add. Distt. Magistrate, Hardoi  for closure of level crossing \n(LC) No. 249 -C near St. Theresa’s school in Sandila Tehsil after \nconstruction of Road Over Bridge (ROB). \n\nRepresentation has also been received requesting Railways to \nconstruct the ROB in lieu of LC No. 249 -C.   After joint survey with \nState Authorities, the conceptual profile sketch of ROB has been \napproved by  Uttar Pradesh State Bridge Corporation limited, on \n02.08.2021. Accordingly, ROB in lieu of  LC No. 249/C has been \nsanctioned by Railways on 23.11.2021. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |67", "t": "Preservation and Protection of Cultural Places of Ernakulam", "head": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 67 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02ND DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n Average Price of Petroleum Products  \n \n*67. SHRI GIRISH CHANDRA:  \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी    \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of average prices of petrol, diesel, kerosene and LPG as on 30th June during each \nof the last three years and the current year; and \n(b) the main components of the price fixation mechanism of petroleum products in the country \nvis-à-vis share of amount of revenue between the Union and State Governments during the last \nthree years and the current year with year-wise details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkÑfrd xSl ea=h \n (\u0000ी  हरद\u0000प \u0000संह पुर\u0000) \n \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n \n(a) & (b): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 67 \nasked by Shri Girish Chandra to be answered on 02 nd December, 2021 regarding “Average \nPrice of Petroleum Products”. \n \n(a) & (b): Prices of petrol and diesel are market-determined with effect from 26.06.2010 and \n19.10.2014 respectively. Since then, the Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) have \nbeen taking appropriate decision on pricing of petrol and diesel on the basis of international \nproduct prices and other market conditions. The OMCs have increased and decreased the prices \nof petrol and diesel according to changes in international prices and rupee dollar exchange rate. \nThe Average price of Indian Basket of Crude oil has risen approximately 313% from April, 2020 \nto October, 2021 ($19.90/bbl to $82.11/bbl). \nPrices of LPG in the country are based on Saudi Contract Price (CP), the benchmark for \ninternational prices of LPG. However, for domestic LPG  the Government continues to modulate \nthe effective price to consumer to insulate the common man from rise in international prices. \nSaudi CP has risen approximately 238% from April, 2020 to October, 2021 (236 USD to 797 \nUSD). \nThe average retail selling price of petrol, diesel and domestic LPG at Delhi and PDS kerosene at \nMumbai during last three years and current year as on 30th June is given as under: \nYear Petrol Diesel PDS Kerosene Domestic LPG RSP \n  Rs./Litre Rs./14.2 Kg cylinder \n2018-19 75.37 68.22 27.05 768.12 \n2019-20 72.69 65.78 33.73 694.73 \n2020-21 80.84 73.58 25.39 695.15 \n2021-22 (till 30th June) 93.10 83.67 37.70 809.00 \n \n \n \nThe contribution to Central and State exchequer during last three years and current year, year-\nwise is given below:                     \n(Rs./Crores) \nYear \nTotal Contribution of \nPetroleum Sector to \nExchequer \nShare of Central \nExchequer \nShare of State \nExchequer \n2018-19 5,75,632 60% 40% \n2019-20 5,55,370 60% 40% \n2020-21 6,72,719 68% 32% \n(Apr-Sep) 2021-22 (P) 3,49,923 62% 38% \n Based on data from 16 major oil & gas companies \nx-x-x-x-x "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |670", "t": "Consumer Protection", "head": " \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 670 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \n \nCONSUMER PROTECTION \n \n \n670. SHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: SHRI KODIKUNNIL SURESH:  \n \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has undertaken consultations with experts and industry stakeholders on the \nConsumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof including the persons and/or entities consulted with, and if not, the reasons \ntherefor; and  \n(c)  the timeline by which the Government will notify and implement the amendments to the Rules? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k jkT; ea=h  \n(श्री अश्विनी कु मार चौबे) \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (c) : The Department has consulted with stakeholders, such as Industry Bodies, State Governments, \nCentral Government Ministries/ Departments and Voluntary Consumer Organisations before framing the \nConsumer Protection (E -commerce) Rules, 2020 and notifying them on 23 -07-2020. Subsequently, draft \namendments to the Rules were uploaded on the Department’s website for wider consultation, in June this \nyear. Comments from the stakeholders have been received on the same and detailed interacti ons have been \nheld thereafter on the draft amendments. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |671", "t": "Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development Scheme", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 671 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \n \nCOMPREHENSIVE HANDLOOM CLUSTER DEVELOPMENT SCHEME \n \n \n671.  DR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR. S.:  \n         DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE:  \n         SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI:  \n         SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \n         SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR:  \n         DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE:  \n         SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE:  \n         SHRI GAUTHAM SIGAMANI PON:  \n         SHRI SELVAM G.:  \n         SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR:  \n         SHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA: \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government is implementing Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development Scheme \n(CHCDS);  \n \n(b) if so, the current status of its implementation in the country alongwith  financial allocation for the \nscheme;  \n \n(c) the number of mega handloom clusters that is being implemented in different States including \nMaharashtra;  \n \n(d) the number of green and brown field projects that have been selected under the scheme during each \nof the last three years and the current year State/ UT-wise;  \n \n(e) the details of the facilities and incentives being offered under the scheme; and  \n \n(f) whether any proposals for development of mega handloom clusters in Maharashtra under the \nCHCDS are pending for approval and if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereof?  \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) & (b): Comprehensive Handloom Cluster Development Scheme (CHCDS) has been subsumed \nunder National Handloom Development Programme for its implementation during 2021 -22 to 2025-26 \nfor integrated & holistic development of mega handloom cluster, having atleast 10,000  handlooms with \nCentral financial assistance upto Rs. 30.00 crore in a period of 5 years.  \n \n(c) & (d): As announced in the different Annual Budgets, 08 Mega Handloom Clusters viz. \nVaranasi (Uttar Pradesh), Sivasagar (Assam), Virudhunagar (Tamil Nadu), Murshidabad (West Bengal), \nPrakasam & Guntur districts (Andhra Pradesh), Godda & neighbouring districts (Jharkhand) Bhagalpur \n(Bihar) and Trichy (Tamil Nadu) are under implementation.  \n\n \n \nDuring the last three years and current year, one green project, ”Retail Outlet” at Kaziranga \nunder Sivasagar mega handloom cluster has been taken up in 2018-19.  \n \n(e): In the mega handloom clusters, financial assistance is provided for need based interventions like \nHathkargha Samvardhan Sahayata, product development/diversification, market development, export & \npublicity, development of technical/common infrastructure, setting up of Value Addition units like \nGarmenting unit, Processing unit, Printing unit etc. 80% of the project cost is borne by the Govt. of \nIndia, while 20% of project cost along with land is provided by the State Govt./Implementing Agency. \n \n(f): No such proposal is pending in the Ministry of Textiles. \n  \n*** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |672", "t": "Niryat Bandhu Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 672 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01st DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nNIRYAT BANDHU SCHEME \n672.  DR. HEENA GAVIT: \n  DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL: \n  DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL:  \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वािण᭔य एवं उ᳒ोग मंᮢी) be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether progress has been made so far in achieving the objectives under Niryat \nBandhu Scheme;  \n(b) the details of funds allocated, sanctioned and disbursed during the past five years and \nthe current year for the purpose, State/Union Territory wise, specially for \nMaharashtra;  \n(c) the details of the entrepreneurs benefitted during the last five years, State/Union \nTerritory wise; and  \n(d) the future action plan of the Government for further encouraging the entrepreneurs \nof India if so, the details thereof and if not, the reason therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a): The Directorate General of Foreign Trade (DGFT) is implementing the Niryat Bandhu \nScheme for mentoring new and potential exporter on foreign trade through counseling, training \nand outreach programmes in partnership with various stakeholders. The Scheme has benefitted \naround 1.15 Lakh Entrepreneurs since its inception in 2013.  \n \n(b) & (c): Niryat Bandhu Scheme has been allocated Rs. 5.45 Crore in last 5 years (2016-17 \nto 2020-21). An amount of Rs. 4.04 Crore has been utilized during this period and 65,184 \n\nentrepreneurs have benefitted from the training and outreach activities undertaken under the \nScheme. Fund allocation & utilisation for the last 5 years is as under. \nFinancial Year  Allocation (Rupees in Lakhs) Expenditure (Rupees in Lakhs) \n2016-2017 100 98.52 \n2017-2018 100 96.97 \n2018-2019 200 99 \n2019-2020 95 73.05 \n2020-2021 50 36.92 \nTOTAL 545 404.46 \n \nFor the current year 2021-22 Rs. 4.48 Crores have been allocated under the Scheme. \nThe details of fund allocation, utilization and entrepreneurs benefitted State/Union Territory wise \nunder the Niryat Bandhu Scheme is at Annexure I. \n(d): Niryat Bandhu Scheme has been extended for period of five years from 2021-22 to 2025-\n26 with enhanced objectives and components. \n  \n\nAnnexure I \nStatement referred to in reply of part (b) and (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred question no. 1276 \nfor answer on 01st December 2021   \nThe details of fund allocation, utilization and entrepreneurs benefitted State/Union Territory and \nFinancial Year wise under the Niryat Bandhu Scheme are as follows: \nTable 1: For FY 2016-17 \nSr. \nNo. \nStates/UTs DGFT Regional \nAuthority (RA) \nhaving jurisdiction \nof State(s)/UTs \nFY 2016-17  \nFund \nSanctioned \nto RA (In \nRs.) \nFund Utilized \n(In Rs.) \nNumber \nof \nEntrepren\neurs \nBenefitted \n1 Andhra Pradesh RA Visakhapatnam  86,000   86,000   458  \n2 Assam, Meghalaya, Arunachal \nPradesh, Mizoram, Nagaland and \nManipur \nRA Guwahati  40,000   990  NA \n3 UT of Delhi, Uttarakhand, UP \n(Districts under CLA Jurisdiction) \nand Haryana(Gurugram and \nFaridabad) \nRA Delhi  2,56,000   2,56,000   775  \n4 Gujarat RA Vadodara  85,065   85,065   1,261  \n5 RA Ahmedabad  1,86,000   1,86,000  NA \n6 RA Rajkot  2,21,000   2,21,000   1,350  \n7 RA Surat  1,36,000   1,36,000   1,945  \n8 Haryana RA Panipat  1,71,000   1,71,000   240  \n9 Karnataka RA Bangalore  1,86,000  1,860,00  380  \n10 Kerala and UT of Lakshadweep RA Kochi  2,37,000   2,37,000   378  \n11 Madhya Pradesh RA Bhopal/Indore  1,86,000   1,86,000   882  \n12 Maharashtra, Goa, Chattisgarh and \nUT of Dadra and Nagar Haveli \nand Daman and Diu  \nRA Nagpur  86,000   86,000   218  \n13 RA Pune  1,86,000   93,000   600  \n14 RA Mumbai  1,36,000   1,36,000   420  \n15 Punjab, Uttarakhand and \nHimachal Pradesh \nRA Ludhiana  86,000   86,000   720  \n\n16 Rajasthan RA Jaipur  1,71,000   1,71,000   1,207  \n17 Tamil Nadu & UT of Puducherry RA Coimbatore  1,36,000   1,28,017   456  \n18 RA Chennai  1,86,000   1,86,000   1,223  \n19 Telangana RA Hyderabad  1,36,000   77,395   390  \n20 UT of Jammu & Kashmir and UT \nof Ladakh \nRA Jammu & RA \nSrinagar \n 71,000   70,323   252  \n21 Uttar Pradesh RA Kanpur  1,36,000   1,36,000   569  \n22 RA Varanasi  1,36,000   1,34,295   347  \n23 Persons trained through online \nMassive Online Open Course \n(MOOC) by DGFT in \ncoordination with IIFT  \nDGFT Hqr  38,40,000   38,40,000  353  \n  TOTAL  70,96,065   67,09,085   14,071 \n \nTable 2: For FY 2017-18 \nSr. \nNo. \nStates/UTs DGFT Regional \nAuthority (RA) \nhaving \njurisdiction of \nState(s)/UTs \nFY 2017-18 \nFund \nSanctione\nd to RA \n(In Rs.) \nFund \nUtilized \n(In Rs.) \nNumber \nof \nEntrepre\nneurs \nBenefitte\nd \n1 Andhra Pradesh RA \nVisakhapatnam \n 96,000   76,511   387  \n2 Assam, Meghalaya, \nArunachal Pradesh, \nMizoram, Nagaland and \nManipur \nRA Guwahati  1,43,000   1,43,000  NA \n3 UT of Delhi, Uttarakhand, "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |673", "t": "Per Capita Income", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 673 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n  \nPER CAPITA INCOME \n  \n673. SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL: \n \nWill the Minister of STATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION \nbe pleased to State: \n  \n(a) the per capita income noted during the last three years in the \ncountry, State-wise;  \n  \n(b) whether there has been huge difference in per capita income of \nvarious States during the last five years;  \n  \n(c) if so, the details thereof, State-wise including Assam;  \n  \n(d) the urban and rural per capita income differentials in the country \nduring each of the last three years and the current year; and  \n  \n(e) the corrective steps taken/being taken by the Government to bridge \nthe gap?  \n \n ANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF \nSTATISTICS AND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF \nSTATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING \nAND MINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS (RAO \nINDERJIT SINGH) \n  \n(a): Per Capita Net National Income of the country from 2018 -19 to \n2020-21 at current and constant (2011-12) prices is as under: \n  \nPer Capita Net National Income (In Rs.) \nYears At Current Prices At Constant (2011-12) Prices \n2018-19 1,25,883 92,241 \n2019-20 1,34,186 94,566 \n2020-21 1,28,829 86,659 \n\n   \nState/Union Territory wise per capita Net State Domestic Product at \ncurrent and constant prices are available up to 2019 -20. The said \ninformation from 2015-16 to 2019-20 is given in Annexure. \n  \n(b): As per the latest information received from State Governments, \nState/ Union Territory -wise range of Per Capita Income, measured in \nterms of Net State Domestic Product (NSDP) at current and co nstant \n(2011-12) prices for the last five years, is as under: \n                                                                                              (In Rs.) \nYear Range at Current Prices Range at Constant Prices \n2015-16 30,404 – 3,34,576 24,064 – 2,78,601 \n2016-17 34,045 – 3,78,953 25,455 – 3,05,875 \n2017-18 36,850 – 4,11,740 26,719 – 3,08,296 \n2018-19 40,715 – 4,23,716 29,092 – 3,37,031 \n2019-20 45,071 – 4,35,959 30,621 – 3,03,687 \n  \n(c): State wise details including those of Assam are as given in \nAnnexure. \n  \n(d): This Ministry compiles estimates of rural and urban income, in \nterms of Net Value Added (NVA), only in the base year of the Gross \nDomestic Product (GDP) series, which is currently 2011 -12. The per \ncapita NVA for the year 2011 -12 for rural and urban area is Rs.40,925 \nand Rs.98,435 respectively.  \n  \n(e): Government has been focusing on inclusive growth as reflected in \nits commitment of Sabka Saath, Sabka Vikas and has taken various \nsteps to address the rural urban disparity. 'Gaon, Gareeb aur Kisan' \nhas been at the centre of government policies.   Government has been \nimplementing a number of targeted programmes to improve the per \ncapita income of both rural and urban India for overall balanced \ndevelopment in the country.  \n  \nGovernment has also i mplemented the Aspirational Districts \nProgramme aimed at comprehensive development of backward areas \nin six areas: (i) health & nutrition, (ii) education, (iii) agriculture & \nwater resources, (iv) financial inclusion, (v) skill development, and (vi) \nbasic infrastructure. Several measures have also been taken to \nincrease growth and consumption in both rural and urban India under \nthe Aatmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyan, including Pradhan Mantri Garib \nKalyan Yojana. \n  \n\nThe Union Budget 2021 -22 has also announced a numb er of measures \nto support broad -based and inclusive economic development. Key \nmeasures include ensuring Minimum Support Price (MSP) at minimum \n1.5 times the cost of production across all commodities, extension of \nSWAMITVA Scheme to all States/UTs, enhancin g agricultural credit \nand infrastructure funds, investments to develop modern fishing \nharbours and fish landing centres, One Nation One Ration Card, Rs. \n15,700 crore budget allocation to MSME Sector, etc. \n \n******* \n  \n\n  Annexure \nStatement attached to Lok SabhaUnstarred Question No. 673 on ‘Per Capita Income’ to be \nanswered on 01.12.2021. \nPER CAPITA NET STATE DOMESTIC PRODUCT AT CURRENT PRICES;  \nBASE YEAR 2011-12. As on 02.08.2021 \n            (In Rs.) \nS. No. State\\UT 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 \n(1) (2) (3) (4) (5) (6) (7) \n1 Andhra Pradesh     1,08,002   1,20,676   1,38,299   1,52,286   1,68,480 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh    1,16,985   1,24,129   1,38,836   1,54,652   1,69,742 \n3 Assam       60,817       66,330      75,151      81,034      86,801 \n4 Bihar       30,404       34,045      36,850      40,715      45,071 \n5 Chhattisgarh       72,991       83,285      89,690      98,254   1,05,089 \n6 Goa    3,34,576   3,78,953   4,11,740   4,23,716   4,35,959 \n7 Gujarat    1,39,254   1,56,295   1,76,961   1,97,457   2,13,936 \n8 Haryana    1,64,963   1,84,982   2,10,592   2,26,409   2,47,628 \n9 Himachal Pradesh    1,35,"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |674", "t": "New Ration Card of ONORC", "head": "ORIGINAL IN H!ND!\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLTC DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLTG DISTRIBUTIOI{\nLO1( SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.674\nTO BE AitSwERED ON {sr DECEMBER,2O2{\nNEW RATION CARD OF ONORC\nt674, SHRI MALOOK NAGAR:\nSHR! GOPAL GHINNAYA SHETTY:\nSHRIMATI SUMALATHA AMBAREESH:\nSHRI D.K.SURESH:\nSHR! NALIN KUMAR KATEEL:\nWill the Minister of GOilSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND pUBLtC\nDISTRIBUTIoN 5crirfrr erri, urc.a flt{ Trffi6, frr{q d* be pteased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the centnl Government has directed the state Governments\nto issue ration card to the eligible beneficiaries on new standard fomat as\na part of rOne Nation One Ration One Gard, scheme;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, the criteria for getting the benefit of the\nscheme and present status of imptementation of scheme, State-wise;\n(c) whether beneficiaries of the said scheme can avail the facitity ofprocuring ration ,rom any fair price shop in the country under the National\nFood Security Act([FSA);\n(d) if so, the details thereofl and\n(e) the details total number of eligibte ration card holders/beneficiaries\nreceived the benefits of ONORG scheme tilt date?\nAHSWER\nilIilISTER OF STATE FOR MI]'ISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMEI{T AilD\nGONSUMER AFFA|RS, FOOD & pUBLtC DISTRTBUTION\n(sADHVt NTRANJAN JYOTT)\n......2t-\n\n-2.\n(a) & (b): As pail of the Targeted public Distribution System (TPDS)\nreforms, states/urs have been advised that whenever they plan to issue new\nration cards under the National Food security Act (!{FSA), they may conform\nto a standard sel of data fields in the ration cards in bi-linguat format with an\nintent to gradually standardize the ration cards across the country. However,\nas such there is no immediate requarement to issue new ration cards under\nNFSA for the purpose of ration card portabitity under the one ation oneRation Gard (oNoRc) ptan. To take the benefit of ration card portability\nunder ol{oRG plan, any aadhaar seeded NFSA beneficiary may tift theentitled foodgrains for self or on behalf of the complete household from any\nFair Price shop (FPs) in the country by using their same/exlsting ration cardwith biometric authentication on an electronic poant of sale (epos) deylce.\nso far, the oNoRC plan is enabled in 34 states/urs covering about 75 Grore\nbeneficiaries (94.3\"/o NFSA populataon) in the country. state/ur wise status\nof implementation is at Annexure-|.\n(c) & (d): Under the technotogy driven oNoRG plan NFSA beneficiaries are\nempowered to lift their entitled foodgrains from any Fps of their choice In\nthe country by using their same/existing ration cards with biometric\nauthenticataon on an ePoS device at any of the FpS.\n(e): since inception of oNoRG ptan in August 2oi9 to J{ovember 2o21, a\ncumulatiye total of about 49.6 crore portable transactaons have been\nrecorded in the ol{oRG implemented states/urs so far. However, as any\nmember of an eligible ration card may lift the monthly entitted foodgrains on\nbehalf of some or all members of the family in a single transaction, theactual cumulative number of ration card holders/beneficiaries having\nbenefited from oNoRG facility would be much higher than the total\ncumulative number of portability transactions recorded ti date.\n\nAnnexure-l\nANI{EXURE REFERRED To til REpLy TO pARTs (a)&(b) OF THEU.ISTARRED QUEST'O,{ 110.674 FOR AilSWER OiI OI.I2.2O2' IN THE LOKSABHA.\nEtate/ur wise imptementation status of one Nation ons Rataon Garer (oNoRC)plan so fat:\ns1, State/UT lmplementation Status lmplernented\nFrom\n1 Andaman & Nicobar lslands lmplemented Dec-2OAndhra Pradesh lm lemented\n3 Arunachal Pradesh lmplcmented Oct.204 Assam\n5 Bihar lm lemented Ma 06 Chandigarh lmplemented Nov-207 Ghhattisgarh\nI Dadra & NH arrd Daman & Diu lmplemented May-20Delh i lmplemented Jul-21Goa lmplemented Jan-2O11 Gujarat lmplemented\n12 na lmplemented Oct.{9t3 Himachal Pradesh lmplemented May-2014 Jammu & Kashmir lm emented Aug-20{5 Jharkhand lmplemented Jan-2016 Kamataka lm emented Oct-{ 917 Kerala lmptemented Oct-i9{8 Ladakh lmplemented Sep-20{9 Lakshadweep lmplemented Sep-2020 Madhya Prades;lr lmplemented\n21 Maharashtra lmplemented {9Au22 Manipur !mplemented Aug-2OMeghalaya lmplemented Dec-2O24 M izoram lmplemented Jun.2025 Nagaland lmplemented Aug-2026 Odisha lmplemented Jun-2027 Puducherry lm emented\n2a Punjab lm lemented May.2029 Rajasthan lmplemented Oct-{930 Sikkim lmplemented Jun-203t Tami! Nadu lmplemented Oct-2032 Telan lmplemented Aug-i933 Tripura lmplemented Jan-2034 Uttar Pradesh ementedI Au o35 Uttarakhand lmplemented May-2036 West Be al lmplemented Aug-21Total 34\nlz Aug-{ 9\nls\nlo\nAug.l9\nJan-2O\n23\nNov-20"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |675", "t": "Polluter Pays Principle", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 675 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01st DECEMBER, 2021 \n \n \nPOLLUTER PAYS PRINCIPLE \n 675.  SHRI KURUVA GORANTLA MADHAV:  \nSHRI TALARI RANGAIAH: \nSHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \nSHRI ADALA PRABHAKARA REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वािण᭔य एवं उ᳒ोग मंᮢी) be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has introduced the Polluter Pays Principle in the text of the \nnegotiations at WTO for the fisheries sector; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) the steps taken by India to safeguard the interest of domestic fisheries sector against \nthe heavily subsidized high sea fishing by developed countries? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n \n(a) to (c): Yes Sir. On 23 September, 2021 India has introduced a document at the WTO \ncontaining ‘Polluter Pays’ principle for fisheries subsidies negotiations. It proposes a moratorium \non subsidies by distant water fishing nations for fishing and fishing related activities beyond their \nExclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) and also seeks reduction commitments on distant water fishing \ncapacity by these nations.  \nIn these negotiations, India has also sought policy space to support low income, resource-poor or \nlivelihood fishing or fishing related activities up to our EEZ, to develop and modernize our \nfisheries sector and for expanding our fishing activities in the high seas.  \n \n  \n***** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |676", "t": "BharatNet in UP", "head": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 676 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nBHARATNET IN UP \n \n676.  SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA:  \nSHRI MAHENDRA SINGH SOLANKY:  \nSHRIMATI SANDHYA RAY:  \nSHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY:  \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of current penetration percentage of Mobile Internet in Rural and Urban areas \nof the State of Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan;  \n \n(b)  the details of current status of the BharatNet Scheme and the number of beneficiaries \ncovered under the above said scheme;  \n \n(c)  the timeline by which Uttar Pradesh as expected to achieve 100% internet coverage;  \n \n(d)  the status of the PM WANI Scheme and the expected number of beneficiaries in the \nState? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n \n(a) As per the Indian Telecom Service Performance Indicators published by Telecom \nRegulatory Authority of India (TRAI) on 21.10.2021, the Service Area wise number of Internet \nSubscribers per 100 population  (including mobile internet subscribers) in rural and urban areas \nof Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan at the end of June 2021 are as below: \nService Area No. of Internet subscribers per 100 population \nRural Urban Total \nUttar Pradesh (East) 28.74 89.40 43.50 \nUttar Pradesh (West) \nMadhya Pradesh 28.73 100.56 49.01 \nRajasthan 38.07 109.64 56.95 \nNote: Data/Information for Uttar Pradesh includes Uttarakhand and Madhya Pradesh includes \nChhattisgarh. \n\n2 \n \n \n \n(b) & (c) BharatNet project is implemented in a phased manner to provide broadband \nconnectivity to all Gram Panchayats (GPs) in the country.  On 30.06.2021, t he scope of \nBharatNet was extended upto all inhabited villages beyond GPs in the country including Uttar \nPradesh. The targeted date for completion of the project is August, 2023.  As on 01.11.2021, a \ntotal number of 1,66,088 GPs are  Service Ready in the country.  The infrastructure created under \nBharatNet project is a national asset, accessible on a non -discriminatory basis to the Service \nProviders, and the same can be utilized to provide benefits of telecom services to the citizens.  \n(d) Under the PM-WANI framework, Wi-Fi Access Points are set up by small entrepreneurs \ndepending on the market demand with an objective to earn return on their investment. As \non 24.11.2021, about 4,323 Access Points have been set up in Uttar Pradesh. \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |677", "t": "Export Insurance", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 677 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01st DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nEXPORT INSURANCE \n677.  SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.: \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE & INDUSTRY (वािण᭔य एवं उ᳒ोग मंᮢी) be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is true that the Government has approved an infusion of more than \nRs.6,000 crore to entities providing export insurance cover to facilitate additional \nexports worth in excess of Rs.5.6 lakh crore over the next five years; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is also true that the Government has also approved the listing of State \nrun Export Credit Guarantee Corporation on the stock exchanges and if so, the \ndetails thereof; \n(d) whether it is a fact that the Government aims to create an additional 62 lakh jobs \nwith these measures; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nokf.kT; ,oa m|ksx ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h ¼Jherh vuqfiz;k iVsy½  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY \n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL) \n(a):  Yes Sir. \n(b):  The details are as below: \n(i) Government has approved capital infusion of Rs.4,400 crore into ECGC during the \nperiod from 2021-22 to 2025-26. \n(ii) Government has approved Grant-in-aid infusion of Rs.1,650 crore into National \nExport Insurance Account (NEIA) Trust during 2021-22 to 2025-26.  \n(iii) Capital infusion in ECGC will increase its capacity to underwrite risks up to \nRs.88,000 crore and this will enable ECGC to issue covers that can support \nadditional exports of Rs.5.28 lakh crore over the five-year period in line with the \nexisting pattern. With the Grant-in Aid infusion in the NEIA Trust, it will be able \nto support project exports worth up to Rs.33,000 crore. Cumulatively, an additional \nexport of Rs.5.61 lakh crore over the next five years are likely to be facilitated by \nthe approval.  \n\n(c): Yes Sir. Department of Investment and Public Asset Management (DIPAM) is in the \nprocess of implementing the Government’s decision.  \n(d): Yes Sir. \n(e):  As per the report on ‘Export to Jobs’ published by World Bank and International Labour \nOrganisation in February 2019, ₹5.28 lakh crore of additional exports (estimated support by \nECGC) will lead to formalization of 2.6 lakh workers. The total number of workers (both \nformal and informal) will increase by 59 lakhs as per the report. Further, Rs. ₹33,000 Crore of \nadditional exports (estimated support by NEIA) will help create 2.6 lakh new jobs, including \naround 12,000 in formal sector. Thus, the total additional jobs, in both formal and informal \nsectors, that can be created over the next five years are estimated to be 61.6 lakh. \n**** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |678", "t": "Digital forensic Kiosk", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 678 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \nDIGITAL FORENSIC KIOSK \n \n \n678   SHRI TALARI RANGAIAH:  \nSHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA:  \nSHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY:  \nSHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Centre for Development of Advanced Computing (C -DAC) has developed a \ndigital forensic kiosk recently; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the benefits of this digital forensic kiosk and the sectors \nwhere this can be used? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a) and (b):  Centre for Development  of Advanced Computing (C -DAC) has developed \nDigital Forensic Kiosk (DFK), which is a stand -alone forensic hardware platform for quick \npreview of evidential artefacts prese nt in electronic exhibits such as Smart Phones, SIM Cards, \nMemory Cards, Hard Disks and other USB storage devices. \n \nLaw Enforcement Agencies like National Investigation Agency  (NIA), Intelligence Bureau (IB), \nCentral Bureau of Investigation (CBI), Customs,  Revenue Intelligence, State Police Force  can \nmake use of the Digital Forensic Kiosk by  installing at Airports, Police Stations and other places \nwhere a search for digital artefacts in the devices seized by Law Enforcement Agencies (LEA) is \nrequired.  \n \nThe LEA can conduct a primary examination of the device, keeping the integrity of the \nelectronic data intact and can go for a forensic imaging. The primary advantage is that Kiosk \nshall enable the LEA to ascertain whether the suspected piece of evidence is av ailable in the \ndevice or not and thereby unnecessary seizure of the device can be avoided.  \n \n******** \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |679", "t": "Cyclone Warning Centres", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EARTH SCIENCES\nLOKSABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 679\nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, 1sTDECEMBER, 2021\nCYCLONE WARNING CENTRES\n679. SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND:\nDR. T. SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN:\nWill the Minister of EARTH SCIENCES be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has set up Cyclone Warning Centres in the coastal States of\nthe country and, if so, the details thereof, State-wise and the States likely to be\nbenefitted by these Centres;\n(b) the amount allocated and spent during the last five years inthis regard;\n(c) the details of schemes and projects of the Ministry implemented or being implemented\ninthe State of Tamil Nadu;\n(d) whether the Government has evaluated the performances of various schemes under\nimplementation by the Ministry, if so, the details thereof and action being taken\nthereon; and\n(e) the details of total funds allocated to such schemes and programmes?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR\nMINISTRY OF SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY\nAND EARTH SCIENCES\n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH)\n(a) All the 9 coastal states (viz., Gujarat, Maharashtra, Goa, Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil\nNadu, Andhra Pradesh, Odisha and West Bengal) and the Union Territories along the\ncoasts (viz., Dadara & Nagar Haveli, Daman, Diu, Mahe, Yanam, Puducherry) and the\nIsland Territories (Lakshadweep & Andaman and Nicobar Islands) have well developed\nCyclone early warning systems in place. There are 7 Cyclone warning Centers covering\nthe east & west coasts located at Chennai, Mumbai and Kolkata and four Cyclone\nWarning Centres (CWCs) at Thiruvananthapuram, Visakhapatnam, Ahmedabad and\nBhubaneswar and the Cyclone Warning Division centrally located in IMD Head\nQuarters at New Delhi.\nThe responsibility for operational storm warning work for the respective area rests with\nthe ACWCs and CWCs. Area of responsibility of ACWCs and CWCs is shown in the\nTable below.\nCentre Coastal area * Maritime State/UT\nState: West Bengal State: West Bengal\nACWC Kolkata UT: Andaman &Nicobar UT: Andaman & Nicobar\nIslands Islands\n\n\nACWC Chennai State: Tamil Nadu State: Tamil Nadu\nUT: Puducherry UT: Puducherry\nACWCMumbai State: Maharashtra & Goa State: Maharashtra &\nGoa\nCWC State: Kerala & Kamataka State: Kerala &\nThiruvananthapuram UT: Lakshadweep Kamataka\nUT: Lakshadweep, Mahe\nState: Gujarat State: Gujarat\nCWC Ahmedabad UT: Dadra-Nagar Haveli- UT: Dadra-Nagar Haveli-\nDaman-Diu Daman-Diu\nCWC Visakhapatnam State: Andhra Pradesh State: Andhra Pradesh,\nYanam\nCWC Bhubaneshwar State: Odisha State: Odisha\n*Coastai strip of responsibility extends upto 75kmfrom the coast line.\n(b) Various programs are being implemented in IMD under the umbrella scheme\n\"Atmosphere & Climate Research-Modelling Observing Systems & Services\n(ACROSS)\" of the MoES. Year-wise allocation and cumulative expenditure for all the\n4 sub-schemes of IMD under ACROSS namely, Atmospheric Observation Network\n(AON), Upgradation of Forecast System (UFS), Weather & Climate Services (WCS)\nand Commissioning of Polarimetric Doppler Weather Radars (PDWR) during last five\nyears are mentioned below:\n(Rs. in Crores)\nYear Budget Revised Actual Expenditure\nEstimates Estimates\n2016-17 241.60 166.41 144.08\n2017-18 188.75 149.15 134.09\n2018-19 186.46 185.89 175.94\n2019-20 207.17 224.73 206.04\n2020-21 249.22 155.20 150.33\n(c) The sub-schemes under ACROSS are aimed at augmenting the observational network,\nimproving the forecasting system and enhancement of Weather & Climate Services.\nThese activities are meant for the entire country including Tamil Nadu. Details of these\nsub-schemes are as follows:\nAtmospheric Observations Network (AON)\nMain activities of the program undertaken during 2017-21:\n• Sustenance and Augmentation of observational networks comprising of Doppler\nWeather Radars (DWRs), Automatic Rain Gauges (ARGs), Automatic Weather\nStations (AWSs), Upper Air, Surface and Environmental Observatories etc. Improve\nupon the spatial and temporal density of Radar observational network, particularly\nover the regions with large data gaps inthe country.\n\n\n• To improve and upgrade weather and climate services over north-eastern region by\nestablishing additional state of art surface and upper air observatories for real time\nobservations.\n• Sustenance & Establishment of Multi processing, computing and communication\nfacilities for Satellite Meteorological Applications.\nUpgradation of Forecast System (UFS)\nMain activities of the program undertaken during 2017-21:\n• Upgradation and sustenance of Communication Systems for Data and Product\ntransmission.\n• Development of an advanced Operational Forecast System, Delivery System for\nForecast and other services.\n• Conduct of special campaign for improving Cyclone, Thunderstorm and Fog\nforecasting through Aircraft reconnaissance and provision of additional\nobservations.\n• Integrated Himalayan Meteorological Programme for Western & Central\nHimalayas.\n• Capacity Building, Outreach, Planning and sustenance of specific process related\nobse"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |68", "t": "Introduction of Health System in School Curriculum", "head": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 68 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2nd DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nReservation in Allotment of Petrol Pumps and Gas Agencies \n \n†*68. SHRI CHIRAG KUMAR PASWAN:  \n SHRIMATI KESHARI DEVI PATEL: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the categories/persons being provided reservation or special \nconcession in allotment of petrol pumps and gas agencies; \n \n(b) the details of the application process for allotment of petrol pump or gas agency for \nthese categories/ persons under special concession;  \n \n(c) whether there are any inspection teams to check the quantity of petrol and diesel at \npetrol pumps in Bihar including Jamui district and in Uttar Pradesh including Prayagraj \ndistrict;  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(e) the details of the petrol pumps where inspections have been conducted including the \ndates of inspection; and  \n \n(f) the petrol pumps where difference has been reported in quantity and quality along \nwith the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  एवं \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  (\u0000ी  हरद\u0000प \u0000संह  पुर\u0000) \n \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n \n \n \n(a) to (f)    A statement is laid on the table of the House.   \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO. 68 ASKED BY SHRI CHIRAG KUMAR PASWAN AND SMT. KESHARI DEVI \nPATEL, MPs FOR ANSWER ON 2.12.2021 REGARDING “RESERVATION IN \nALLOTMENT OF PETROL PUMPS AND GAS AGENCIES”.  \n \n \n(a)&(b)   Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) have implemented new “Dealership \nSelection Guidelines” for Retail Outlets w.e.f. 24.11.2018 and “ Unified Guidelines for \nSelection of LPG Distributorships” w.e.f. 23.6.2016. Both these guidelines inter alia, \nhave a provision of 22.5% reservation for SCs/STs and 27% reservation for OBCs in \nallotment of retail outlet dealerships and LPG Distributorships. Within SC/ST, OBC & \nOpen categories, there is reservation also for Defence personnel, Para Military \nPersonnel/Central/State Govt. and Central/State PSU employees, Physically \nHandicapped Persons, Outstanding Sports Persons and Freedom Fighters.   \n \n \n \n \n \nApplication Procedure / Norms / Criteria / Reservation Policy for allotment of Retail \nOutlet Dealerships in the country are available on the respective websites of the Public \nSector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) i.e. www.iocl.com, www.bharatpetroleum.com \n& www.hindustanpetroleum.com. The detailed guidelines namely “Unified \nGuidelines for Selection of LPG Distributorships” are available on the website  \nwww.lpgvitarakchayan.in. \n  \n(c)to(f)   Regular/Surprise inspection of Retail Outlets are carried out by Public Sector \nOMCs throughout the country. The details of inspections carried out in Bihar and Uttar \nPradesh during 2020-21 and Apr-Sept`2021 and suspected cases of adulteration \n(quality issues) and short delivery (quantity) detected during inspection is given below :- \n \nParticulars  No. of Inspections \ncarreid out  \nNo. of cases of \nsuspected Quality \nissues detected \nNo of cases of short \ndelivery (Quantity \nissues) detected \nIOCL BPCL  HPCL IOCL BPCL HPCL IOCL  BPCL HPCL \nBihar  6403 2559 2191 4 0 1 5 0 4 \nUttar Pradesh 14416 6879 8159 1 0 1 114 26 7 \nTotal 20819 9438 10350  5 0 2 119 26 11 \nOMCs  40607 7 156 \nJamui Dist.  86 23 36 0 0 0 0 0 0 \nOMCs  145 0 0 \nPrayagraj Dist. 236 183 372 0 0 0 3 11 0 \nOMCs  791 0 14 \n \nIn all above cases of irregularities detected on Quality & Quantity issues, action has \nbeen initiated / taken by concerned OMC as per procedure/guidelines. \n \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |680", "t": "Consumer Welfare Corpus Fund", "head": " \n \n \n \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 680 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \n \nCONSUMER WELFARE CORPUS FUND  \n \n680. SHRI E.T. MOHAMMED BASHEER:  \n \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION  \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has information on the progress in various States with regard to constitution \nof Consumer Welfare Corpus Fund; and  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof including the States which have not implemented the said scheme? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k jkT; ea=h  \n(श्री अश्विनी कु मार चौबे) \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n \n(a) to (b) : Yes Sir. As on date,  total 17 states namely; Gujarat, Andhra Pradesh, Odisha, West Bengal, \nBihar, Nagaland, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Madhya Pradesh ., Kerala, Haryana, Jharkhand, Telangana, \nRajasthan, Sikkim, Mizoram and Tripura have established Consumer Welfare (Corpus) Fund in their \nrespective states.  \n \n Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Chhattisgarh, Goa, Himachal Pradesh, Maharashtra, Manipur, Meghalaya, \nPunjab, Uttar Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Andaman & Nicobar Islands, Chandigarh, Dadra & Nagar Haveli and \nDaman & Diu, Delhi , Jammu & Kashmir,  Lakshadweep, Puducherry and Lad akh have not established the \nConsumer Welfare (Corpus) fund in their States/UTs.  \n \n \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |681", "t": "Operational Post Offices", "head": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF POSTS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 681 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nOPERATIONAL POST OFFICES \n \n681. SHRI B.B.PATIL:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the total number of operational post offices across the country including rural areas, \nState/UT-wise;  \n \n(b)  whether postal services have been provided in all the villages of the country including \nTelangana and if so, the details thereof including the  details of all the services being provided by \npost offices;  \n \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefore and the number of such villages where postal services are yet \nto be provided, State/UT-wise;  \n \n(d)  whether the Government has formulated any plan to open post office in all the villages of \nthe country; and  \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) Sir, there are 1,56,434 post offices functioning across the country including rural areas. \nThe State/ UT-wise details are at Annexure-I. \n(b) Yes, Sir. The postal services are provided through a network of 1,41,055 Rural Post \nOffices covering all the villages in the country. Of these, 5203 Rural Post Offices are serving the \nvillages in Telangana. The details of postal services provided in all the villages of the country \nincluding Telangana are at Annexure-II. \n(c)  Villages without post office are provided with po stal services from existing nearby Post \nOffices and there are no villages without Postal Service coverage. \n(d) & (e)  No Sir, there is no plan for opening of post offices in all the villages of the \nCountry, in view of the reply above. \n \n\n2 \n \nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply of para (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 681 to be \nanswered on 1 st December, 2021 raised by Hon’ble Member of Parliament Shri B. B. Patil, \nregarding “Operational Post Offices”. \nState/ UT-wise number of Post Offices in the country as on 31.03.2021 \nS.No. Name of the State/UT No. of Post Offices  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 10595 \n2 Assam 4006 \n3 Bihar 9109 \n4 Chhattisgarh 3908 \n5 Delhi 511 \n6 Gujarat 8794 \n7 \nDadra & Nagar Haveli \nand Daman & Diu 52 \n8 Haryana 2696 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 2795 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 1591 \n11 Ladakh 109 \n12 Jharkhand 3852 \n13 Karnataka 9624 \n14 Kerala 5053 \n15 Lakshadweep 10 \n16 Madhya Pradesh 8278 \n17 Maharashtra 12744 \n18 Goa 255 \n19 Arunachal Pradesh 303 \n20 Manipur 694 \n21 Meghalaya 491 \n22 Mizoram 383 \n23 Nagaland 330 \n24 Tripura 711 \n25 Odisha 8258 \n26 Punjab 3817 \n27 Chandigarh 51 \n28 Rajasthan 10288 \n29 Tamil Nadu 11777 \n30 Puducherry 90 \n31 Telangana 5794 \n32 Uttarakhand 2722 \n33 Uttar Pradesh 17665 \n34 West Bengal 8769 \n35 Andaman & Nicobar 100 \n36 Sikkim 209 \n  Total 156434 \n \n\n3 \n \nANNEXURE- II \nAnnexure referred to in reply of para (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 681 to be answered \non 1 st December, 2021 raised by Hon’ble Member of Parliament Shri B. B. Patil, regarding \n“Operational Post Offices”. \nAN INDICATIVE LIST OF VARIOUS POSTAL SERVICES AVAILABLE IN POST OFFICES \n1. Mail Services \na) Speed post \nb) Money Order (MO) \nc) Stamps Sale \nd) Letter \n Registered Letter \n Ordinary letter \n Insured letter \ne) Parcel \nf) Delivery of all articles \nincluding e-post messages. \n2. Banking Services \na) National (small) Savings Scheme \n Post Office Savings Account \n One year Time Deposit \n Two year Time Deposit \n Three year Time Deposit \n Five year Time Deposit \n Five year Recurring Deposit \n Five year Senior Citizen Savings \nSchemes \n Five year monthly income scheme \n Five year National Savings \nCertificate  \n Public provident fund scheme \n SukanyaSamriddhi Account \n KisanVikasPatra \n \nb) Jansuraksha scheme \n Pradhan Mantri Jeevan \nJyotiBimaYojna(PMJJBY) \n Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima \nYojna(PMSBY)  \n Atal Pension Yojna(APY)  \nc) India Post Payments Bank \n Savings and current accounts \n Direct Benefit Transfer payment. \n Money Transfer Services.  \n Aadhaar Enabled Payment System \n(AePS) \n Digital Life Certificate (Jeevan \nPramaan). \n Mobile number update for Aadhaar. \n3. Insurance Services \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n******** \na) Postal Life Insurance \n Whole Life Assurance (Suraksha) \n Endowment Assurance (Santosh) \n Convertible Whole Life Assurance \n(Suvidha) \n Anticipated Endowment Assurance \n(Sumangal) \n Joint Life Assurance (Yugal Suraksha) \n Children Policy (Bal Jeevan Bima) \n Life Insurance through Bajaj Allianz \nlife Insurance Company and Pradhan \nMantri Jeevan JyotiBima Yojana \n(PMJJBY). \n General Insurance services. \nb) Rural Postal Life Insurance \n Whole Life Assurance (Gram \nSuraksha) \n Convertible Whole Life \nAssurance (Gram Suvidha) \n Endowment Assurance (Gram \nSantosh) \n 10 Years Rural PLI (Gram \nPriya) \n Anticipated Endowment \nAssurance (Gram Sumangal) \n Children Policy (Bal Jeevan \nBima) \n\n4 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |682", "t": "Performance of FCI", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & pUBLtc DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMEI{T OF FOOD AND PUBLIG DTSTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.682\nTO BE ANSWERED ON tsr DECEMBER,2O21\nPERFORMANCE OF FCI\n(a) whether the Government can -provide details of the pedormanee of\nFood corporation of tndia (FGl) for the last three years and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(b) the procurement undertaken by FGI in the states of Andhra Pradesh\nand Telangana for the last three years and the details thereof; crop.wisel\n(c) whether the Government is putting up the assets of FCI for sale and if\nso, the details and reasons thereof;\n(d) whether the Government is aware of the huge debt obligation of FGI;\nand\n(e) if so, the details thereof and measures is the Government taking to\nreduce the debt obligation of FGI?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT AND\ncoNsuMER AFFA!RS, FOOD & pUBLTC DTSTR!BUTION\n(sADHVt NTRANJAN JYOTT)\n(a) Yes, Sir. The performance of Food Corporation of lndia during the Iast\n3 years is as under: -\nYear Procurement\n(LMT)\nNo. of\nfarmers\nbenefited\n(in lakhs)\nMovt.\n(LMT)\nStocks as\non {st June\n(LMT)\n(including\nequivalent\nrice of\npaddy)\nTurnover -\nRevenue\nfrom\nOperataons\n{ln Rs.\nGrore)\nWheat Ric e\n20,18-19 341.32 44$.99 737.27 383.43 733.O,1 388.65 ,,55,8{ I\n2019-20 389.92 51lt.26 { 60.{ 6 389.18 82{.86 I,56,O00\n2020-21 433.44 600.78 172.23 594.51 972.69 414.70 2,62,096*\n*Un-audited\n......2t-\n682. SHRI KESINENI SRINIVAS:\nWill the Minister of GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC\nDISTRIBUTIoN sselt{il arrd, rrr4r slk srffi{, Fffl{ur d+ be pteased to\nstater\nStorage\ncapacity\nwith FCI\n(LMT}\n(Owned\n& Hired)\n412.O3\n\n-2-\n(b): Both Andhra Pradesh and Telangana are DCP states, wherein\nprocurement is undertaken by State Govt, through its agencies. In both the\nStates, there is no wheat procurement and only paddy/rice is procured.\nThe details of paddy (in terms of rice) procured under Central Pool in\nAndhra Pradesh and Telangana by state agencies in coordination with FGI\nduring the last three years is as unden-\n(Fig, in LMT)\nKMS Andhra Prade Telangan:\n2018-{ 9 48.06 51.90\n2019-20 55.33 74.54\n56.67 94.53\n(c): l{o, Sir.\n(d): Yes, Sir. The Government of lndia is aware of all the debt obligations\nthat are on FGl.\n(e): As on {5.O2.2O21, tlne total debt obligation of FGI was Rs. 4rO7r4O4.77\ncr in the form of Gash Credit limit, Bonds, NSSF Loans, WMA and Short term\nloan. Subsequently, it was addressed by GOI with allocation of Rs. 4,62,789\ncr. The entire outstanding NSSF loan was repaid and the arrear subsidy was\nbrought to Zero as on 3{.3.2O2{.\nln the current financia! year, FGI has received subsidy of Rs. {r25r2OO\nCr till October and the current debt obligation of FGI as on 3{,{O.2O2{ is Rs.\n461173 Gr. The regular altotment under budgeted food subsidy is released to\nFGI by GOI from time to time.\n2020-21"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |683", "t": "Stoppage at Taraori", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 683 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \nSTOPPAGE AT TARAORI \n \n†683.  SHRI SANJAY BHATIA: \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has discountinued the halt of Dadar Express running \nfrom Dadar to Amritsar at Taraori station; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether people who used to travel from Taraori station to Sangat Shri \nAnandpur Dham Bina  (Madhya Pradesh) by Dadar Express running for the last 60 -\n70 years, have to face problems due to discontinuance of halt at Taraori; \n \n(d) whether the Government has received any proposal to restart Dadar Express \ntrain; \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(f) the action taken/being taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n (SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW)  \n \n(a) to (c) As a part of the exercise of rationalization of time table and speeding up \ntrain services, few stoppages have been discontinued. \n At present, 11057 Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj(T) -Amritsar Express has \nscheduled stoppage at Karnal and Nilokheri, which a re 12 km and 04 km from \nTaraori respectively and from where passengers may avail changeover for their \nonward journey. \n \n(d) to (f) 11057/58 Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj(T) -Amritsar Express is \noperational/running and serving Mumbai -Bina-Amritsar sector. Demands, including \nthat from the Hon’ble Member of Parliament, have been received for provision of \nstoppage 11057 Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj(T) -Amritsar Express at Taraori.  \nThese have been examined but found operationally not expedient, at present. \nHowever, provision of stoppages in existing train services is an ongoing process \non Indian Railways subject to operational feasibility and commercial justification. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |684", "t": "Revamping BSNL", "head": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 684 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nREVAMPING BSNL \n \n†684. SHRI DINESH CHANDRA YADAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government is considering is to revamp BSNL;  \n \n(b)  whether it is a fact that Government‘s communication system is totally dependent on BSNL and \nif so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether Government proposes to make BSNL functional at par with private mobile companies \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d)  the number of officers and employees working in BSNL currently;  \n \n(e)  the present financial status of BSNL; and  \n \n(f)  the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) to (c)  The Government approved the revival plan for Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) \nand Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited (MTNL) on 23.10.2019. The revival plan inter-alia, includes \nmeasures to reduce the Staff cost through a Voluntary Retirement Scheme (VRS) for employees of age 50 \nyears and above, administrative allotment of spectrum for providing 4G services with funding through \nbudgetary allocation, monetisation of non -core and core assets to generate resources to retire debt, meet \nCAPEX and other requirements, debt restructuring by raising of Sovereign Guarantee Bonds and in -\nprinciple approval for merger of BSNL and MTNL. These measures are expected to he lp BSNL turn \naround and remain competitive. \n \nBSNL plays a significant role in providing communication systems under various projects of \nGovernment inter -alia Bharatnet, Network for Spectrum for Defence, for improving communication \nsystems in North Eastern Region,  Left -Wing Extremism (LWE) affected areas, border areas and other \ninaccessible areas. \n \n(d). Total number of officers/employees in BSNL as on 31.10.2021 are 63,010 (Executive-29,832 and \nNon-Executive-33,178). \n(e) to (f)  The financial Status of BSNL as on 31.03.2021 is as under: \n \n( Amount in Rs Cr) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n******** \n \nYear 2020-21 \nTotal Income 18,595 \nTotal Expenditure 26,036 \nNet Profit/ (Loss) (7,441) "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |685", "t": "Ration for Persons with Disabilities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTR|BUTIOI\nDEPARTMEI{T OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTIOT{\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.685\nTO BE ANSWERED ON .tsr DECEMBER,2O21\nRATION FOR PERSOI{S WITH OISABILITIES\n585. SHRI RAJMOHAN UNNITHAN\nWill the Minister of GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLTC\nDISTRIBUT|ON rctfrar arri, ar6a sit{ sr+sfrfi kf,rur d* be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) the number of persons with disabilities who were provlded ration\nunder PDS during the ongoang pandcmic; and\n(b) if so, the number of persons with disabilities who are covered under\nPDS?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPi'IENT AND\nGONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & pUBLtc DISTRIBUTION\n(sADHVt NtRAilJAN JYOTT)\n(a) & (b): To minimize the food security hardships of around 8O crore\nNational Food Security Act (NFSA) beneficiaries in the country during the\ntime of COVID-{9 crisis, the Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Anna Yolana (PM-\nGKAY) was designed in such a manner to provide additional free of cost\nfoodgrains at the scale of 5 kg per penson per month to all such NFSA\nbeneficiaries, over and aboye the regular foodgrain entitlements as per\ntheir Antyodaya Anna Yoiana (AAY) or Priority Household (PHH) ration card\nissued under the Act. PMGKAY has been extended until March, 2O22 with\nan overall expenditure of nearly Rs 2.6O lakh crore on all phases, Coverage\nfor NFSA beneficiaries are around 8O.OO Grore including disabled persons.\nHowever, no separate data base is maintained for disabled persons."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |686", "t": "Shortage of BSNL Towers", "head": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 686 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nSHORTAGE OF BSNL TOWERS \n \n686.  SHRI PRAJWAL REVANNA:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware of shortage of BSNL Towers and network related \nissues in Hassan district of Karnataka particularly in hilly and forest areas of Alur and \nSakaleshapura blocks which is causing inconvenience to public and impacting the functioning of \ngovernment offices;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken or being taken by the Government to \nsolve this problem; and  \n \n(c)  the details of BSNL towers approved, sanctioned and funds sanctioned, utilised in Hassan \ndistrict? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) & (b)  BSNL has informed that it augments its mobile network in its areas of operation \nincluding Hassan district of Karnataka based on techno -commercial considerations. Telecom \nRegulatory Authority of India (TRAI) monitors the performance of the Telecom Service \nProviders (TSPs) including BSNL against the benchmark for various Quality of Services (QoS) \nparameters laid do wn by TRAI by way of QoS regulations issued from time to time, through \nQuarterly Performance Monitoring Reports (PMRs). As per PMR for quarters ending June, 2020 \nto June, 2021, BSNL is meeting all the QoS parameters for its cellular and basic services in \nKarnataka. \n \n(c)  The total number of  3G towers commissioned through redeployment in Hassan district is  \n33. Rs. 27,80,588 has been utilised against sanctioned budget of Rs. 39,20,600 for this purpose. \n \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |687", "t": "Priority list under NFSA", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nM|NISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLTG DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD ]AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.687\nTO BE ANSWERED ON IST DECEIIIIBER, 2O2I\nPRIORITY LIST UNDER NFSA\n687. ADV. DEAN ]'(URIAKOSE:\nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIG\nDISTRIBUTION lvttt6l Trri, qr(q s+{ l{r&oa-{, ffr{ur d+ be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Govemment has received any proposa!/request to increase tlre\nnumber of beneficiaries in the priority list under National food Security Act, 2O{3,\nwith respect to the state of Kerala;\n(b) if so, the details of the action taken in this regard;\n(d) if so, the details of the action taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT AND CONSUMER\naFFA!RS, FOOD & PUBLTG DISTRIBUTION\n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI)\n(a) & (b): Yes Sir. lt was informed that the National Food Security Act, 2Ol3\n(NFSA) provides for coverage of upto 75\"/o oi the rural and 5O7o of the urban\npopulation, at the all lndia level, for receiving highly subsidized foodgrains.\nGorresponding to this coverage, State/UT-wise percentage coverage was\ndetermined by the then Planning Commission (Now NlTl Aayog), using 68th round\nof NSSO Household Gonsumption Expenditure Survey (2011-'121. Further, Section 9\nof the NFSA provides that the percentage coverage under the Targeted Public\nDistribution System in rural and urban areas for each State shall be determlned by\nthe Central Government and the tolal number of persons to be covered ln the\nState shall be calculated on the basis of the population estimates as per the\ncensus of which the relevant figures have been published. Therefore, any revision\nin the coverage under the NFSA shall be possible only after the relevant data of\nnext population Census is published. As the criteria for determination of State/UT-\nwise coverage is uniformly applicable for all the States/UTsr it is not possible to\nrevise the coverage qf a particular State/UTs.\n(c) & (d): Yes Sir. Food Gorporation of India proeures' distributes and stores rice\nin only two category that is \"Rice Grade A & Rice Common\" based on length and\nbreadth ratio as per specifications communicated by Department of Food & Public\nDistribution.\n*****\n(c) whether the Government has received any proposal/pet:tion to include more\nJaya and Surekha rice varieties instead of Sona Masuri in the PDS; and"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |688", "t": "Details of Service Providers", "head": "1 \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 688 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nDETAILS OF SERVICE PROVIDERS \n \n688.  SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL:  \nSHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of service providers who provide optical fiber high speed internet connections \nin rural areas of the country; specially in Rajasthan State;  \n \n(b)  the reasons for absence of optica l fiber high speed internet services in rural and interior \nareas of the country; and  \n \n(c)  remedial steps taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) & (b) As per the extant licensing  framework, Access Service Providers and Internet \nService Providers can provide optical fiber based high speed internet connections in their \nrespective License Service Area. Service area to be covered and the type of technology to be \nused for delivering high speed internet services are decided by each licensee based on its techno \ncommercial assessment. As on 30-9-2021, 321 licenses for Access Service and 2254 licenses for \nInternet Service are working in the country; out of which 15 licenses for Access Servi ce and 297 \nlicenses for Internet Service are working in the State of Rajasthan.  \n \n(c) BharatNet project is being implemented in a phased manner to provide broadband \nconnectivity to all Gram Panchayats (GPs) in the country. The infrastructure created under \nBharatNet project is a national asset, accessible on a non -discriminatory basis to the Service \nProviders. The scope of BharatNet has been recently extended upto all inhabited villages beyond \nGPs. As on 01.11.2021, by laying about 5.52 lakh km Optical Fibre  Cable (OFC), around 1.62 \nlakh GPs have been made Service Ready on OFC media in the country. In Rajasthan, by laying \nabout 30,156 km Optical Fibre Cable (OFC), around 8739 GPs have been made Service Ready \non OFC media. \n \n \n******** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |689", "t": "Google and Facebook", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION 689 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2021 \n \n \nGOOGLE AND FACEBOOK \n \n689. DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \nWill the Minister of Electronics and Information Technology be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has taken cognizance of the draft law proposed in Australia and \nother similar moves to make platforms such as Google and Facebook pay for news and if \nso, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the Ministry has taken any steps towards regulating this feature in a similar \nmanner and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) whether the Ministry has any concerns about the market power and dominance of \nplatforms such as Google and Facebook and if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR ELECTRONICS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n (SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR) \n \n(a): It is informed that the Australian Parliament had in February 2021 enacted a law, called \nthe “News Media and Digital Platform Mandatory Bargaining Code”,requiring global digital \ncompanies to pay for local news contents. \n \n(b)and (c): Government is aware of the growing risks of dominance of various big tech \nplatforms on the Internet and is deploying tools and capabilities to deal with the same \nincluding actively working with social media intermediaries. Presently, there is no prop osal \nforenactment of a law by this Ministry in this regard. \n \n******** \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |69", "t": "Funds under NCLP", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 69 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29.11.2021 \n \nFUNDS UNDER NCLP \n \n69. DR. HEENA GAVIT:  \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE:  \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL: \nDR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \n  DR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL: \n  \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)the funds sanctioned, allocated and utilized under the National \nChild Labour  Project (NCLP), State/ UT wise particularly in \nMaharashtra during the last three years and the current year; \n(b)the details of beneficiaries of PENCIL (Platform for Effective \nEnforcement for No Child Labour) which had been created under the \nNCLP scheme;  \n(c)the data of web visits on PENCIL (Platform for Effective \nEnforcement for No Child Labour) which had been created under the \nNCLP scheme; \n(d)the data of adolescent children rescued from hazardous work \nsituations; and \n(e)whether the Government is able to r aise awareness amongst the \ntarget communities and the other stakeholders regarding child \nlabour about the functionality of NCLP? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a): The Grant -in-Aid released during 2017 -18 to 2020 -21 under the \nNational Child Labour Project (NCLP) Scheme, State -wise including \nMaharashtra is at Annexure-I. \nContd..2/- \n\n\u0000\u0003\n\u0000\u0003\n\u00006\u00007\u0000$\u00007\u0000(\u00000\u0000(\u00001\u00007\u0000\u0003 \u00005\u0000(\u0000)\u0000(\u00005\u00005\u0000(\u0000'\u0000\u0003 \u00007\u00002\u0000\u0003 \u0000,\u00001\u0000\u0003 \u00005\u0000(\u00003\u0000/\u0000<\u0000\u0003 \u00007\u00002\u0000\u0003 \u00003\u0000$\u00005\u00007\u0000\u0003 \u0000\u000b\u0000D\u0000\f\u0000\u0003 \u00007\u00002\u0000\u0003 \u0000\u000b\u0000H\u0000\f\u0000\u0003 \u00002\u0000)\u0000\u0003 \u0000/\u00002\u0000.\u0000\u0003 \u00006\u0000$\u0000%\u0000+\u0000$\u0000\u0003\n\u00006\u00007\u0000$\u00005\u00005\u0000(\u0000'\u0000\u0003 \u00004\u00008\u0000(\u00006\u00007\u0000,\u00002\u00001\u0000\u0003 \u00001\u00002\u0000\u0011\u0000\u0019\u0000\u001c\u0000\u0003 \u0000)\u00002\u00005\u0000\u0003 \u0000\u0013\u0000\u0015\u0000\u0011\u0000\u0014\u0000\u0015\u0000\u0011\u0000\u0015\u0000\u0013\u0000\u0015\u0000\u0014\u0000\u0003 \u00005\u0000$\u0000,\u00006\u0000(\u0000'\u0000\u0003 \u0000%\u0000<\u0000\u0003 \u00006\u0000+\u00005\u0000,\u0000\u0003 \u0000$\u00006\u0000$\u0000'\u00008\u0000'\u0000'\u0000,\u00001\u0000\u0003\n\u00002\u0000:\u0000$\u0