long \n  Chandel \n  Ukhrul \n  Bishnupur \n  Thoubal \n  Imph"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |956", "t": "Injuries during Training in Defence Academies", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE  \nDEPARTMENT OF MILITARY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.956 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 3rd DECEMBER, 2021 \n \nINJURIES DURING TRAINING IN DEFENCE ACADEMIES \n 956. SHRI RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU: \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE  \nbe pleased to state: \n (a) the total number of cadets who were injured during their training in defence academies and \nas a result were invalidated out on medical grounds during the last five years, year-wise; \n (b) the total number of injuries that were attributable to service and non-attributable to service;  \n(c) the year-wise break-up of the number of medical board out cadets in the categories of those \nsuffering 20 percent disability, 30 percent disability, 50 percent or more and 100 percent \ndisability during the said period; \n (d) the total amount of ex-gratia payment released to such cadets by the Ministry during the last \nfive years, year-wise; and \n (e) whether such cadets have been issued identity cards or any other document of identification \nthat they can use to access healthcare facilities in military hospitals and if so, the details thereof? \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE     (SHRI AJAY BHATT) \nIN THE MINISTRY OF DEFENCE    \n \n \n(a)  The total number of cadets boarded out of service are as follows: \n \nYear Number of cadets boarded out \n2016 26 \n2017 13 \n2018 19 \n2019 21 \n2020 33 \n \n…2/- \n\n: 2 : \n \n(b) Injuries attributable to service and non-attributable to service: \n \n Total injuries \nInjuries attributable to service \n \n66 \nInjuries non-attributable to service \n \n36 \n \n(c) Year-wise break-up of cadets boarded out: \n \nYear 20% \nDisability \n30% \nDisability \n50%  \nor more \nDisability \n100% \nDisability \n2016 07 07 05 02 \n2017 02 03 04 Nil \n2018 12 03 03 Nil \n2019 10 06 Nil Nil \n2020 \n \n21 10 Nil 01 \n \n(d) The following Ex-gratia benefits are granted to cadets on being medically boarded out:  \n \n(i)  Ex-Gratia Disability Award (for 100% disability, reduced proportionately) – Rs. 16,200/- pm \n+ Dearness Relief.  No disability award shall be payable in cases where the degree of \ndisablement is less than 20% \n \n(ii)  Monthly Ex-Gratia – Rs. 9000/- pm + Dearness Relief. \n \n(iii) Constant Attendance Allowance (For 100% disability only) – Rs.6,750/- pm.  \n \nIn addition, Army Group Insurance Fund (AGIF) provides disability and Ex-gratia benefits to \ncadets boarded out with disability. \n \n(e)  Cadets medically boarded out from armed forces are not issued any identity card.  However, \nmedical treatment of such injured cadets is done at Military Hospitals \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |957", "t": "New Rehabilitation and Employment Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.957\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.12.2021\nNEW REHABILITATION AND EMPLOYMENT SCHEME\n957. SHRIMATI MANEKA SANJAY GANDHI:\nWill the Minister of External Affairs be pleased to state:\n(a) the total number of Indian expatriates in Afghanistan as on the date\nof the US withdrawal in 2021;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to introduce new rehabilitation\nand employment schemes for the returnees from Afghanistan, and if\nso, the details thereof; and \n(c) the  number of  expatriates  left in Afghanistan and the  measures\ntaken by the Government for their protection?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS\n[SHRI V. MURALEEDHARAN]\n(a) to (c) It is estimated that in early 2021, there were around 5,000\nIndians working in Afghanistan including those employed by the United\nNations, and other countries. In view of the deteriorating security situation\nin Afghanistan, the Government of India issued several security advisories\nfrom  April  2021  onwards. As  a  result,  many Indian nationals  had left\nAfghanistan.\nAfter 15 August 2021, under the Government of India’s “Operation\nDevi  Shakti”,  438  Indians  were  evacuated  from  Afghanistan.  A  few\nIndians, who had contacted the Special Afghanistan Cell and desired to be\nevacuated, still remain stranded. The Special Afghanistan Cell is in touch\nwith these remaining Indians.\n***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |958", "t": "Conflict between Modern Medicine and AYUSH System", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AYUSH \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 958 \n ANSWERED ON 03rd DECEMBER 2021 \n \nCONFLICT BETWEEN MODERN MEDICINE AND AYUSH SYSTEM \n958. MS. DEBASREE CHAUDHURI : \n        MS. RAMYA HARIDAS : \nWill the Minister of AYUSH be pleased to state: \na) whether  it is a fact that the Government is aware that there exists a conflict between \nmodern  and AYUSH medicinal  systems with regard to efficacy of medicines, \ntreatments and procedures and also there is huge status gap between the  two which is \ndetrimental to AYUSH resources; \n \nb) If so, the details thereof ; \n \nc) whether  it is also a fact  that the Government has  some plans to introduce a \nframework to integrate the modern and traditional systems so as to facilitate a \nmeaningful, cross learning and collaboration between the two systems; and \n \nd) If so, the details thereof ? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF AYUSH \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n (Da)   No conflict exists between modern  and AYUSH   medicinal systems with regard to \nefficacy of medicines, treatments and procedures. \n    b)   Does not arise. \n    c) & d)   Yes, Madam.  T he Ministry of Ayush  has  taken various initiatives to integrate \nthe modern and traditional systems so as to facilitate a meaningful, cross learning and \ncollaboration between two systems. The highlights of such initiatives have been the \nfollowing : \n (i)  Government of India has ta ken the initiative to integrate all the systems of medicine \nby constituting a  Committee on Integrated Health System under the Chairmanship of Dr. \nV.K. Paul, Member, NITI Aayog, and former Head of the Department of Paediatrics, All \nIndia Institute of Medic al Sciences ( AIIMS), New Delhi.  Four Working Groups have \nbeen further constituted under this Committee focussing on the aspects of education, \nmedicine, clinical research, and public health & administration to bridge certain gaps in  \nContd…2/-… \n\n-:     2     :- \nthe health service continuum of care  and administration of theprogrammes  and to realise \nuniversal health coverage, leveraging the strengths of Ayurveda, Yoga & Naturopathy,  \nUnani, Sidha, Sowa Rigpa and Homoeopathy ( AYUSH systems of medicine) and \nAllopathy. \n  (ii)     The Ministry of Ayush under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National AYUSH \nMission ( NAM) co -locates AYUSH facilities at Primary Health Centres (PHCs), \nCommunity Health Centres (CHCs) and District Hospitals (DHs), thus enabling choic e to \nthe patients for different systems of medicine under single window. The engagement of \nAYUSH Doctors/paramedics and their training are supported by the Department of Health \n& Family Welfare, while the support for AYUSH infrastructure, equipment/ furnit ure and \nmedicines are provided by the Ministry of Ayush  under the shared responsibilities. \n     (iii)  Three Research Councils of the Ministry of Ayush namely the Central Council for \nResearch in Ayurvedic Sciences ( CCRAS), the Central Council of Research in \nHomoeopathy ( CCRH) and the Central Council for Research in Unani Medicines \n(CCRUM) had  jointly taken up a project on the National Programme for Prevention and \nControl of Cancer , Diabetes, Cardiovascular Diseases and Stroke ( NPCDCS) through \nintegration of Allopathy and AYUSH systems on a pilot basis in different districts in \ncollaboration with  the Directorate General of Health Services ( DGHS), Ministry of \nHealth and Family Welfare.  \n(iv) The Central Council for Research in Homoeopathy  (CCRH) has co -location of \nHomoeopathy treatment centre in allopathic hospitals for providing treatment on \nvarious clinical conditions at the following places: \na. VardhamanMahavir Medical College and Safdarjung Hospital (VMMC & SJH), \nNew Delhi. \nb. Homoeopathic Research Institute for Disabilities, Chennai. \nc. Lady Hardinge Medical College & Hospital, New Delhi. \nd. Delhi Cantonment General Hospital, New Delhi. \ne. Delhi State Cancer Hospital, New Delhi. \nf. Clinical Trial Unit, at BRD Medical College and Hospital, Gorakhpur, U.P. \ng. Extension Centre at Pr incess DurruSevar Children and General Hospital, \nHyderabad, Telangana. \nh. Civil Hospital, Aizwal, Mizoram \ni. District Hospital, Dimapur, Nagaland. \nContd…3/-… \n\n-:     3     :- \n \n(v) The Central Council for Research in Unani Medicine ( CCRUM) has undertaken \nseveral collaborative projects with  the modern allopathic hospitals like AIIMS, \nNew Delhi; JN Medical College, Aligarh and Vallabh Bhai Patel Chest Institute, \nDelhi.   \n \n(vi) The Central Council for Research in Siddha ( CCRS), a  Research  Council  under \nthe aegis of  Ministry, has initiated various collaborative clinical studies with \nmodern allopathic hospitals like Government Stanley Medical College, Chennai; \nGovt. Theni Medical College, Tamil Nadu; Govt. Institute of Medical Sciences, \nGreater Noida; Sri Ramachandra Institute of Higher Education & Research, \nChennai; and Govt. Medical College & ESI Hospital, Coimbatore. \n \n(vii) To integrate Yoga in the mainstre"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|7|UNSTARRED |959", "t": "National Tracking System for Missing Children", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO.959 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03.12.2021 \n  \nNATIONAL TRACKING SYSTEM FOR MISSING CHILDREN \n  \n959.   SHRI MOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P. : \n          \n   Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n  \n(a) the present implementation status of the National Tracking System for Missing \nand Vulnerable Children, including details of participating States, State/UT-wise; \n(b) the number of cases of  missing and found persons reported on the portal during \neach of the last six years; and \n(c) the frequency at which details about found and rehabilitated children are updated \non the portal?  \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n(a) to (c) : The Ministry of Women and Child Development has created   “TrackChild \nPortal”,  to track the missing and found children. Police Stations, Juvenile Justice \nBoards and Child Care Institutions are required to update the information  about missing \nand found children regularly. The state/UT-wise and year-wise total number of cases of \nmissing and found children reported on the portal in the last six years is at Annexure -I \nand the frequency at which details about found and rehabilitated children is at \nAnnexure-II.  \n  \n  \n  \n\n ANNEXURE-I \n  \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (A) TO (C) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO.959 FOR ANSWER ON 03.12.2021 SHRI \nMOHAMMED FAIZAL P.P. REGARDING ‘NATIONAL TRACKING SYSTEM FOR \nMISSING CHILDREN’ \n  \nState/UT-wise and year -wise total number of missing and found children for last \nsix years \n  \nSI \nNo. \nState/UT Year 2015 Year 2016 Year 2017 Year 2018 Year 2019 Year 2020 \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nmissi\nng \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nfoun\nd \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nmissi\nng \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nfoun\nd \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nmissi\nng \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nfoun\nd \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nmissi\nng \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nfoun\nd \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nmissi\nng \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nfoun\nd \nChild\nren \nTotal \nNo. \nof \nmissi\nng \nChild\nren \nTotal \